[{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting started","html":"<p><code>@simple-html/grid</code> is a data grid for plain TypeScript or JavaScript apps, no framework needed. Its\nonly dependency is lit-html, which is installed with it. This page installs it and puts a first grid\non a page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-install\">Install</h2>\n<p>The package is not on npm. Install it from a git branch: the <code>prepare</code> script builds <code>dist</code> when it\nis installed.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"json\"><code>&quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;@simple-html/grid&quot;: &quot;github:airemote/simple-html#&lt;branch&gt;&quot;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Or make a tgz in a clone of the repo with <code>npm run build:tgz</code>, copy it from <code>build/</code> into your\nproject and point to the file:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"json\"><code>&quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;@simple-html/grid&quot;: &quot;file:./libs/simple-html-grid-X.Y.Z.tgz&quot;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-css\">Css</h2>\n<p>The grid needs its stylesheet. Date cells and date header filters open a date picker, which has a\nstylesheet of its own. Both are imported from the package root:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css'; // the date picker</code></pre>\n<p>A bundler such as esbuild or Vite handles the css import. Without one, link the files from\n<code>node_modules/@simple-html/grid/dist/</code> in your html.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-first-grid\">A first grid</h2>\n<p>A grid needs two things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <code>Datasource</code>: your rows, and the type of each field that is not text</li>\n<li>a <code>GridConfig</code>: the columns to show and the options of the grid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both take plain data, JSON, so they can be stored. What has to be code - a cell readonly from its\nrow, css classes from the row, changes to the menus - are hooks, given next to them:\n<code>createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks })</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<p><code>createGrid()</code> puts the grid in a container and returns the grid interface, the object you work\nwith the grid through. The grid fills the container, so the container needs a height:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"html\"><code>&lt;div id=&quot;grid&quot; style=&quot;height: 400px&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css';\n\n// the fields that are not text, with their type. A field that is not listed is text\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: {\n        age: 'number',\n        born: { type: 'date', label: 'Born' },\n        active: 'boolean'\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.setData([\n    { id: 1, name: 'Ann', age: 36, born: new Date(1990, 4, 17), active: true },\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', age: 41, born: new Date(1985, 0, 2), active: false },\n    { id: 3, name: 'Cid', age: 25, born: new Date(2001, 11, 24), active: true }\n]);\n\n// layout: where the columns go and how wide they are. columns: the options of a column, by field\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { label: 'Name' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['age'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['born'], width: 110 },\n            { fields: ['active'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(document.getElementById('grid')!, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"basic-grid\"></div>\n<p>Out of the box the user can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>sort: click a header label, shift+click to sort on one more column</li>\n<li>filter: type in the row under the labels and press Enter, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering\">Filtering</a></li>\n<li>resize a column (drag its right edge) and move it (drag its label) - with a mouse, a pen or a\nfinger, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a></li>\n<li>group: drag a label to the panel above the header, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping\">Sorting and grouping</a></li>\n<li>right click a cell, a label or a header filter for a menu, see <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The grid is <strong>readonly by default</strong>. Set <code>readonly: false</code> in the config to edit cells, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-why-the-fields-matter\">Why the fields matter</h2>\n<p>The datasource sorts, filters, groups and formats a field by its type. A <code>number</code> field sorts as a\nnumber and is right aligned, a <code>date</code> field gets a date picker and filters on the day, a <code>boolean</code>\nfield is a checkbox. A field you do not list is <code>text</code>. List every field that is not text:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        balance: 'number',\n        born: 'date', // the day: 2026-11-20\n        lastLogin: 'datetime', // the time too: 2026-11-20T08:15:00\n        active: 'boolean'\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The values must have the type: numbers for a <code>number</code> field, <code>Date</code> objects for <code>date</code> and\n<code>datetime</code>. Convert dates from JSON when you load them. See <a href=\"#guide/datasource\">Datasource</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-done-with-the-grid\">Done with the grid</h2>\n<p><code>destroy()</code> takes the grid off the page and disconnects it from the datasource. The datasource\nkeeps its rows, it can be shown in a new grid.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.destroy();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-placing-the-element-yourself\">Placing the element yourself</h2>\n<p><code>createGrid()</code> makes a <code>&lt;simple-html-grid&gt;</code> element that fills the container. To place and size it\nyourself, make the element and connect a <code>GridInterface</code> to it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { GridElement, GridInterface, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = { layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] } };\n\nconst element = document.createElement('simple-html-grid') as GridElement;\nelement.style.width = '100%';\nelement.style.height = '100%';\nelement.connectInterface(new GridInterface(config, datasource));\nhost.appendChild(element);</code></pre>\n<p>Removing such an element from the page disconnects the grid too, it connects again when the\nelement is put back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-where-to-go-next\">Where to go next</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a> - columns, layout, every option, the hooks and saving the config</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/state\">State</a> - save and restore what the user changed: sorting, filters, column order</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/datasource\">Datasource</a> - data in and out, keys, fields, selection</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a> - editing, change tracking, deleting rows, saving</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/filtering\">Filtering</a> - the header filters, the filter editor, filters from code</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping\">Sorting and grouping</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a> - how values are shown and parsed</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a> - every event and what it sends</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a> - change the right click menus, open menus of your own</li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/migrating-from-v5\">Migrating from v5</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a> - translate the grid: every text by id, <code>setTexts()</code></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"grid-config","title":"Grid config","html":"<p>The <code>GridConfig</code> is what you write: which columns go where, the options of each column, and the\noptions of the grid. What the user changes while working - column order and widths, sorting,\nfilters - is the <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>, not the config.</p>\n<p>The config is plain JSON: it has no functions, so it can be stored and loaded, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>. The functions of a grid - readonly and css classes from the\nrow, the menus - are <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">hooks</a>, given next to the config.</p>\n<p>The grid does not change the config object you pass in. It keeps its own copy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-columns-and-layout\">Columns and layout</h2>\n<p>A config has two parts for the columns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>layout</code> says where the columns go and how wide they are</li>\n<li><code>columns</code> has the options of a column, keyed by the field name</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a value the user has to fill in is a rule of the field: required\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { name: { validate: ['required'] }, age: 'number', born: 'date', balance: 'number' } });\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { label: 'Name' },\n        age: { readonly: true },\n        phone: { label: 'Phone' } // not in the layout: the user can add it from the column chooser\n    },\n    layout: {\n        left: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }], // pinned left, does not scroll sideways\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['age', 'born'], width: 100 }, // two fields stacked in one column\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 200 }\n        ],\n        right: [{ fields: ['balance'], width: 100 }] // pinned right\n    }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"column-layout\"></div>\n<ul>\n<li><code>layout.center</code> is required, <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> can be left out.</li>\n<li>Each entry of a list is one column, from left to right. <code>width</code> is in px.</li>\n<li>A column with more than one field shows them stacked, top to bottom. Every column gets as many\ncells as the column with the most fields, a column with fewer fields leaves the rest empty.</li>\n<li>A field in the layout without an entry in <code>columns</code> gets the default options. <code>company</code> and\n<code>born</code> above have none.</li>\n<li>A field in <code>columns</code> that is not in the layout is not shown. The column chooser in the header\nmenu lists every field the grid knows, and the user can drag one from it into the header.\n<code>gridInterface.getHiddenFields()</code> returns these fields (<code>[&#39;phone&#39;]</code> above).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The type of a column comes from the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>, not from the config, see\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/fields-and-types\">Datasource</a>. So do the validation rules: a field the user has to\nfill in has the <code>required</code> rule, which also shows its empty cells with stripes - see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>. The <code>mandatory</code> column option of v5 is gone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</h3>\n<p>The user changes the layout in the header, with a mouse, a pen or a finger:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Move</strong>: drag a label. While it is dragged the header shows where it can go: the left or right\nedge of a label makes the field a column of its own on that side, the top or bottom edge stacks it\nin that column, the middle swaps the two fields. Dropped on the panel above the header, the rows\nare grouped on it, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/grouping\">Grouping</a>. Dragged past the left or right\nside of the grid, the columns scroll.</li>\n<li><strong>Resize</strong>: drag the edge of a column&#39;s header - the right edge, the left one for a column pinned\nright. A column is at least 25px wide.</li>\n<li><strong>Sort</strong>: click a label, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/sorting\">Sorting</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A press on a label is a click until it is a drag: with a mouse or a pen once it has moved 5px, with a\nfinger once it has been held still for 200ms. A finger that moves sooner scrolls the grid, as a swipe\non the rows does. Only the left mouse button, the first finger or the pen&#39;s tip drag and resize.</p>\n<p>What the user makes of the layout is part of the grid&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>: <code>getState().layout</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-column-label\">The column label</h3>\n<p>A column&#39;s header shows the first of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>its <code>label</code> in <code>columns</code></li>\n<li>the field&#39;s <code>label</code> in the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code></li>\n<li>the field name, with the first letter in upper case and <code>_</code> as a space: <code>first_name</code> shows as\n<code>First Name</code>, but <code>firstName</code> as <code>Firstname</code>. Give such a field a label.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-options\">Column options</h3>\n<p>Every option of a <code>ColumnConfig</code> is optional.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>label</code></td>\n<td>the header text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>readonly</code></td>\n<td>the cell can not be edited, also when the grid can. Shown with stripes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderRow</code></td>\n<td>text shown in an empty cell, by default on the current row only (<code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderFilter</code></td>\n<td>text shown in the empty header filter. Default: the formatter&#39;s placeholder (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> for dates), else the field&#39;s type - <code>Text</code> or <code>Number</code> (texts <code>filter.placeholder.*</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>operator</code></td>\n<td>the operator the header filter starts with. Default <code>EQUAL</code> for text (the whole text: <code>ann</code> does not find <code>Anna</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/header-filters\">Filtering</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>numberOverride</code></td>\n<td>number fields only: <code>&#39;ZERO_TO_BLANK&#39;</code> shows 0 as an empty cell, <code>&#39;BLANK_TO_ZERO&#39;</code> shows an empty cell as 0. The value is not changed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>allowPasteClearOnly</code></td>\n<td>typing in the cell does nothing, paste and clear from the row menu work. For a field you set from a dropdown of your own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>focusButton</code></td>\n<td>shows a <code>...</code> button in the focused cell, see <a href=\"#guide/events/cell-events\">Events</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code></td>\n<td>another field of the row holds the type of this cell, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-type-from-another-field\">Formatting</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate</code></td>\n<td>sums the column up in the group rows and the totals row: <code>&#39;sum&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;avg&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;min&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;max&#39;</code> (number fields) or <code>&#39;count&#39;</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>focusButton</code> takes <code>&#39;ALWAYS&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code> or\n<code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_AND_CELL_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"required-placeholders\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-grid-options\">Grid options</h2>\n<p>Every option is optional.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>readonly</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the whole grid is readonly. Set <code>false</code> to edit, see <a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>22</code></td>\n<td>height of a cell in px</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellHeaderLabelHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>cellHeight</code></td>\n<td>height of a header label cell, make it taller for labels that wrap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>panelHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>25</code></td>\n<td>height of the grouping panel above the header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footerHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>45</code></td>\n<td>height of the footer: the row count and the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>selectSizeHeight</code></td>\n<td><code>18</code></td>\n<td>height of the row of column numbers under the header filters (click one to select a column). <code>0</code> hides the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hideLabels</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>hides the row of header labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hideFilter</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>hides the row of header filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>dims a cell that has the same value as the row above. The row menu can toggle it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>shows the state of each row on its row number: new, edited, marked for deletion, an error. The row numbers are 14px wider for the icon. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>editedMarker</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a marker in the corner of an edited cell: its tooltip has the original value, a click puts it back. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>shows <code>placeholderRow</code> on the current row only. <code>false</code> shows it on every row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>autoRemoveContextMenuOnScrollEvent</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>closes the open menu when the grid scrolls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tooltips</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the built-in tooltips: on the buttons of the filter editor, and the validation messages on a cell and its row number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>texts</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>the texts of this grid by id, e.g. <code>{ &#39;row.copy&#39;: &#39;Kopier:&#39; }</code>: they win over the page&#39;s (<code>setTexts()</code>). The tooltips are texts too, their ids end with <code>.tooltip</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePickerConfig</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>week start and sizes of the date picker: only the fields to change (<code>Partial&lt;IDateConfig&gt;</code>), e.g. <code>{ weekStart: 0, isoWeek: false }</code>. The rest are the defaults: weeks from monday with their numbers. The names of the months and days are texts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>initialState</code></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>the sorting, grouping and filter the grid starts with, see <a href=\"#guide/state/the-state-the-grid-starts-in\">State</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>groupAggregates</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the group rows show the columns&#39; <code>aggregate</code>s. <code>false</code>: the name of the group only. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>totalsRow</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>a row under the rows with the columns&#39; <code>aggregate</code>s over the rows the filter lets through. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/the-totals-row\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>How rows are selected is not a grid option: the datasource has the selection mode, see\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-selection-mode\">Datasource</a>. Readonly cells and css classes from the row, and\nchanges to the menus, are not options either: they are functions, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">Hooks</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"misc-options\"></div>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"tooltips\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-hooks\">Hooks</h2>\n<p>The hooks are the functions of a grid, the parts of it that can not be written as data. They are a\n<code>GridHooks</code> object, given next to the config:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>hook</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>isReadonly</code></td>\n<td>makes cells readonly, or editable, from their row, <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellClass</code></td>\n<td>adds css classes to cells from their row, <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>menus</code></td>\n<td>changes the right click menus, see <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const config: GridConfig;\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: { row: (defaults) =&gt; defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; !entry.id.startsWith('row.summary')) }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });</code></pre>\n<p>Every hook is optional. <code>new GridInterface(config, datasource, hooks)</code> takes them too, for an\nelement you place yourself. A config that has <code>isReadonly</code>, <code>cellClass</code> or <code>menus</code> throws: they are\nhooks.</p>\n<p>With a typed datasource the hooks get typed rows: <code>GridHooks&lt;Person&gt;</code> for a <code>Datasource&lt;Person&gt;</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">Readonly and css classes from the row</h3>\n<p><code>isReadonly</code> and <code>cellClass</code> are called for every cell when it is rendered, with the cell&#39;s row\nand field. Keep them fast.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>isReadonly(row, field, readonlyByConfig)</code> returns <code>true</code> (readonly) or <code>false</code> (editable).\n<code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code> leaves it to the column&#39;s <code>readonly</code> option, which is passed in as\n<code>readonlyByConfig</code>. A readonly grid (<code>readonly: true</code>) stays readonly, whatever it returns.</li>\n<li><code>cellClass(row, field)</code> returns <code>{ input, background }</code>: classes for the cell&#39;s <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> (the\ntext) and for the element behind it (the background). Both are optional, <code>null</code> adds nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['isActive'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\n\n// .negative { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.2); }\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    // company can not be edited on an inactive row, the other cells follow the config\n    isReadonly: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'company' &amp;&amp; row.isActive === false ? true : null),\n    cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.balance &lt; 0 ? { background: 'negative' } : null)\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });</code></pre>\n<p>The background element has no size on an editable cell: give its class\n<code>position: absolute; inset: 0</code>, as above.</p>\n<p>The row is always a data row, a group row has no cells. When what the hooks read changes, call\n<code>gridInterface.refresh()</code> to render the cells again.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"cell-styling\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-changing-the-hooks-updatehooks\">Changing the hooks: <code>updateHooks()</code></h3>\n<p><code>updateHooks()</code> changes the hooks while the grid is shown:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateHooks({ cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.balance &lt; 0 ? { input: 'red' } : null) });\ngridInterface.updateHooks({ isReadonly: undefined }); // removed: the column's readonly decides again\ngridInterface.updateHooks({ menus: undefined }); // the menus show their defaults</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the keys you pass change, the other hooks stay. A key passed as <code>undefined</code> removes that\nhook.</li>\n<li>The visible cells are rendered again once. A menu uses its new function the next time it opens.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-changing-options-updateconfig\">Changing options: <code>updateConfig()</code></h2>\n<p><code>updateConfig()</code> changes options while the grid is shown and builds the grid again once:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ readonly: false });\ngridInterface.updateConfig({ hideFilter: true, cellHeight: 30 });\ngridInterface.updateConfig({ tooltips: undefined }); // back to the default</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the keys you pass change. A key passed as <code>undefined</code> goes back to its default:\n<code>updateConfig({ readonly: undefined })</code> makes the grid readonly again.</li>\n<li><code>columns</code> replaces the options of every column, nothing is merged. A field you leave out gets\nthe defaults.</li>\n<li><code>layout</code> is shown at once and replaces the column order, pinning and widths the user made. It is\nalso the layout <code>resetState()</code> goes back to from then on. <code>layout: undefined</code> is ignored.</li>\n<li><code>initialState</code> changes nothing now, it is what the next <code>resetState()</code> goes back to.</li>\n<li>The sorting, grouping, filter and header filters stay as they are, and so do the handlers you\nsubscribed with <code>on()</code> and the hooks. Change a hook with <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/changing-the-hooks-updatehooks\"><code>updateHooks()</code></a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-saving-the-config\">Saving the config</h2>\n<p>The config, the datasource&#39;s fields and formatters, and the <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a> are plain JSON.\nThey can be stored - in a backend, say - and given back as they are. What is code stays in the\napp: the hooks, and the formatters of your own, registered by id with <code>registerFormatter()</code> (see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-formatter-of-your-own\">Formatting</a>).</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import {\n    createGrid,\n    Datasource,\n    type DatasourceOptions,\n    type GridConfig,\n    type GridHooks,\n    type GridState,\n    NumberFormatterDot,\n    registerFormatter\n} from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// what is stored, as JSON\ninterface SavedGrid {\n    config: GridConfig;\n    datasource: Pick&lt;DatasourceOptions, 'key' | 'fields' | 'formatters' | 'selectionMode'&gt;;\n    state: GridState | null;\n}\n\n// code: the formatters the stored fields name, registered before the datasource is made\nregisterFormatter('money', {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? value.toFixed(2) : '')\n});\n\n// code: the hooks\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    isReadonly: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.locked === true ? true : null)\n};\n\n// e.g. { &quot;config&quot;: { &quot;layout&quot;: ... }, &quot;datasource&quot;: { &quot;fields&quot;: { &quot;balance&quot;: { &quot;formatter&quot;: &quot;money&quot; ... } } } ... }\nconst saved: SavedGrid = await (await fetch('/api/grids/accounts')).json();\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource(saved.datasource);\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config: saved.config, datasource, hooks });\nif (saved.state) {\n    gridInterface.setState(saved.state);\n}\n\n// later: store what the user changed\nconst body: SavedGrid = { ...saved, state: gridInterface.getState() };\nawait fetch('/api/grids/accounts', { method: 'PUT', body: JSON.stringify(body) });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A formatter id that is not registered throws when the datasource is made, with the ids that are.</li>\n<li>The texts are JSON too: <code>texts</code> in the config for one grid, <code>setTexts()</code> with the texts of the page -\nsee <a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a>.</li>\n<li><code>container</code> is the one datasource option that is not data: a <code>DataContainer</code> is made in code.</li>\n<li>A <code>Date</code> does not come back from JSON as a <code>Date</code>, but as its ISO text: a <code>Date</code> in a filter of\nthe state, or of <code>initialState</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/state/dates-in-a-saved-state\">Dates in a saved state</a> for what the grid does\nwith it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-rendering-again\">Rendering again</h2>\n<p>The grid renders again by itself when the datasource changes: new data, sorting, filtering,\nselection - and a value set on a row from code, in the next animation frame (see\n<a href=\"#guide/live-data/values-show-on-their-own\">Live data</a>). When something changes that it can not see,\ntell it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>gridInterface.refresh()</code> renders the visible cells again. Call it when what the <code>isReadonly</code> /\n<code>cellClass</code> hooks read has changed. It is cheap.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code> builds the whole grid again: sizes, header, rows, footer. Call it when a\nformatter shows values differently now, after <code>setTexts()</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/texts/when-a-changed-text-shows\">Texts</a>),\nor after you changed the size of the grid&#39;s container without a resize event.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.autoResizeColumns()</code> sizes every column to its content (at most 750px) and\nrebuilds. It measures with the grid&#39;s font, so call it once the grid is on the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>let highlighted = 'ACME CORP';\ngridInterface.updateHooks({ cellClass: (row) =&gt; (row.company === highlighted ? { input: 'highlight' } : null) });\n\nhighlighted = 'GLOBEX'; // the hook reads it, the grid does not know\ngridInterface.refresh();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-fields-in-the-layout\">The fields in the layout</h2>\n<p><code>getLayoutFields()</code> lists the fields in the layout now, in the order the user sees them: pinned\nleft, center, pinned right. With <code>{ selectedOnly: true }</code> it lists only the fields of the columns\nthe user selected by clicking the column numbers under the header filters (ctrl+click for more).\nIt is <code>[]</code> when none is selected.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const selected = gridInterface.getLayoutFields({ selectedOnly: true });\nconst fieldsToExport = selected.length ? selected : gridInterface.getLayoutFields();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-done-with-a-grid-destroy\">Done with a grid: <code>destroy()</code></h2>\n<p><code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> removes the grid&#39;s element from the page and disconnects the grid: from\nthe datasource, the document and the handlers subscribed with <code>on()</code>. The datasource is not\nchanged. Do not use the interface after it, make a new one with <code>createGrid()</code>.</p>\n"},{"id":"state","title":"State","html":"<p>The config is what you write. The state is what the user changes while working with the grid. Read\nit with <code>getState()</code>, give it back with <code>setState()</code>, and go back to the start with <code>resetState()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-the-state-is\">What the state is</h2>\n<p><code>gridInterface.getState()</code> returns a <code>GridState</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>part</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>layout</code></td>\n<td>the column order, pinning and widths: the user drags, pins, hides and resizes columns, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sorting</code></td>\n<td>the sort, as <code>datasource.getLastSorting()</code>. When grouped, the grouped fields come first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grouping</code></td>\n<td>the grouping, as <code>datasource.getGrouping()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>expandedGroups</code></td>\n<td>the ids of the expanded groups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter</code></td>\n<td>the filter the rows are filtered by, as <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>. <code>null</code> when there is none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>headerFilters</code></td>\n<td>what each header filter cell shows: the value and the operator picked in its menu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const state = gridInterface.getState();\nstate.layout.center.map((column) =&gt; column.fields); // [['name'], ['age', 'born']]\nstate.sorting; // [{ attribute: 'name', ascending: true }]\nstate.filter; // { group: 'AND', children: [{ field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO', value: 30 }] }\nstate.headerFilters; // { age: { value: 30 } }</code></pre>\n<p><code>filter</code> is what the rows are filtered by. <code>headerFilters</code> is only what the header cells show: the\nconditions a header filter made are in <code>filter</code> already. Keep both, as <code>getState()</code> gives them, so\nthe header shows what the rows are filtered by.</p>\n<p>These are <strong>not</strong> state: the options (<code>readonly</code>, <code>dimRepeatedValues</code>, sizes ... see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>), the data and its edits, the selected rows, the current row, the\nselected columns and the scroll position.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-getstate-setstate-and-resetstate\"><code>getState()</code>, <code>setState()</code> and <code>resetState()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const saved = gridInterface.getState();\n\n// ... the user sorts, filters and moves columns around\n\ngridInterface.setState(saved); // back to what it was\ngridInterface.resetState(); // back to the state the grid started in</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getState()</code> returns a copy. Changing it changes nothing in the grid, and what the user does\nlater does not change it.</li>\n<li><code>setState()</code> applies the parts it is given and <strong>keeps the rest</strong>. Within a part nothing is\nmerged: <code>layout</code> replaces the whole layout, <code>headerFilters</code> every header filter.</li>\n<li><code>setState()</code> copies what it is given with <code>structuredClone</code>, so a <code>Date</code> stays a <code>Date</code>. The\nstate must be plain data: no functions, no rows from the datasource.</li>\n<li><code>resetState()</code> goes back to <code>initialState</code> (below), with the config&#39;s <code>layout</code>. What\n<code>initialState</code> leaves out is cleared: no sorting, no grouping, no filter.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.setState({ sorting: [{ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }] }); // only the sort\ngridInterface.setState({ grouping: [{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }], expandedGroups: [] });\ngridInterface.setState({ filter: null, headerFilters: {} }); // no filter, empty header filters</code></pre>\n<p>An empty <code>sorting</code> gives the rows back the order they were set in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-saving-it\">Saving it</h2>\n<p>The state is plain data, so it can be stored as JSON - with the config, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst KEY = 'orders-grid-state';\n\n// e.g. when the page is left\nwindow.addEventListener('beforeunload', () =&gt; {\n    localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(gridInterface.getState()));\n});\n\n// when the grid is made\nconst saved: GridState | null = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) ?? 'null');\nif (saved) {\n    gridInterface.setState(saved);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>A saved layout is the layout the user had. When you add a column to the config later, a state saved\nbefore does not have it. Put a version in the key, and change it when the layout in the config\nchanges.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-dates-in-a-saved-state\">Dates in a saved state</h3>\n<p>JSON has no dates: a <code>Date</code> in the filter or in a header filter is stored as its ISO text,\n<code>&#39;2026-11-20T08:15:00.000Z&#39;</code>, and comes back as that text. <code>setState()</code> does not turn it back into a\n<code>Date</code>, it keeps what it is given. What that means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> field reads the text as the same moment. The same rows are filtered, and\nthe header filter and <code>getFilterString()</code> show the same date. <code>getState()</code> gives the text from\nthen on, not a <code>Date</code>.</li>\n<li>A field that is not a <code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code> field in the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> filters on a <code>Date</code>\nas a date only while it is a <code>Date</code>. After JSON it is compared as text, and other rows are\nfiltered. List the type of every date field in <code>fields</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same goes for a <code>Date</code> in the config&#39;s <code>initialState</code>, when the config is stored as JSON.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"grid-state\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-state-the-grid-starts-in\">The state the grid starts in</h2>\n<p><code>initialState</code> in the config is the state the grid starts in, and the one <code>resetState()</code> goes back\nto. It takes the same parts as <code>setState()</code>. The layout comes from the config&#39;s <code>layout</code>, unless\n<code>initialState</code> has one.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type GridConfig, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['age'], width: 80 }\n        ]\n    },\n    initialState: {\n        sorting: [{ attribute: 'name', ascending: true }],\n        filter: where('age', 'GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO', 18),\n        headerFilters: { age: { value: 18 } } // what the age header filter shows\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>The grid applies <code>initialState</code> to the datasource when it is made: a filter, sort or grouping\nset on the datasource before is replaced, and <strong>cleared when <code>initialState</code> leaves it out</strong>. Set\nthem in <code>initialState</code>, or on the datasource after the grid is made.</p>\n<p><code>updateConfig({ initialState })</code> changes what the next <code>resetState()</code> goes back to, it changes\nnothing now. Call <code>resetState()</code> after it to apply it at once.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-header-filters-in-the-state\">Header filters in the state</h2>\n<p><code>headerFilters</code> is keyed by field. Only the columns whose header filter has a value, or an operator\npicked in its menu, are listed:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridState } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst headerFilters: GridState['headerFilters'] = {\n    name: { value: 'ann*' }, // typed in the name column\n    age: { value: 30, operator: 'LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO' }, // the operator picked in the menu\n    active: { value: true } // a boolean column's checkbox\n};</code></pre>\n<p>The operator picked in a header filter&#39;s menu is state. The column&#39;s <code>operator</code> in the config is\nonly the operator its header filter starts with, the grid never changes it. See\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/header-filters\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<p>To set a filter from code that the header also shows, set both:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ngridInterface.setState({\n    filter: and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway')),\n    headerFilters: { country: { value: 'Norway' } }\n});</code></pre>\n"},{"id":"datasource","title":"Datasource","html":"<p>The <code>Datasource</code> holds the rows. It sorts, filters and groups them, keeps the selection and the\ncurrent row, and tracks the edits. The grid shows what the datasource gives it, and most of what you\ndo from code goes through the datasource.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-making-one\">Making one</h2>\n<p>Every option is optional, <code>new Datasource()</code> works.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id', // the field that identifies a row\n    fields: {\n        age: 'number',\n        born: { type: 'date', label: 'Born' },\n        lastLogin: 'datetime',\n        active: 'boolean'\n    },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }, // the default formatter of every date field, by its id\n    selectionMode: 'multiple' // the grid showing it uses this\n});</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>key</code></td>\n<td>the field that identifies a row, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/keys\">Keys</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fields</code></td>\n<td>the type, label and formatter id of each field that is not plain text, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/fields-and-types\">Fields and types</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formatters</code></td>\n<td>the id of the default formatter per type, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>selectionMode</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> (default), <code>&#39;single&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;none&#39;</code>. A grid showing the datasource uses it, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/selection\">Selection</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>container</code></td>\n<td>a <code>DataContainer</code> to share the rows with another datasource, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/sharing-the-rows-between-two-datasources\">Sharing the rows</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>All but <code>container</code> are plain JSON: a formatter is named by its id (<code>&#39;dateDDMMYYYY&#39;</code>, or one of your\nown registered with <code>registerFormatter()</code>), so the options can be stored, see\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>.</p>\n<p>With TypeScript, give it the type of your rows. The rows it hands out are then typed:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    born: Date | null;\n}\n\nconst people = new Datasource&lt;Person&gt;({ key: 'id', fields: { born: 'date' } });\nconst names = people.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; row.name); // string[]</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-in-setdata\">Data in: <code>setData()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const rowsFromServer: object[];\ndeclare const moreRows: object[];\n\ndatasource.setData(rowsFromServer); // replaces the rows\ndatasource.setData(moreRows, { add: true }); // adds them after the rows there are\ndatasource.setData(moreRows, { add: true, refilter: false }); // shown even if the filter leaves them out</code></pre>\n<p>The new rows are filtered, sorted and grouped the way the datasource is: a filter that is set - from\ncode, the header filters or the filter editor - applies to them too. <code>refilter: false</code> shows every row\nset, also one the filter would leave out, while the filter stays set (the header filters and the\nfooter still show it) until it runs again. v5 did that by default.</p>\n<p>The datasource wraps each of your objects in a proxy that tracks its edits. The rows it hands out\nare those proxies, not your objects: set values on the rows you get from the datasource, a change\nmade on your original object is not seen as an edit. See <a href=\"#guide/editing\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<p>Dates must be <code>Date</code> objects, numbers must be numbers. Convert values from JSON when you load them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>interface OrderJson {\n    id: number;\n    amount: string;\n    ordered: string;\n}\ndeclare const json: OrderJson[];\n\ndatasource.setData(json.map((row) =&gt; ({ ...row, amount: Number(row.amount), ordered: new Date(row.ordered) })));</code></pre>\n<p>To delete rows, mark them: <code>markForDeletion(rows)</code> keeps them, struck through, so the delete can be\nundone (<code>unmarkForDeletion(rows)</code>), and <code>getChanges()</code> sends it to the server - see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</a> and <a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a>.\n<code>removeData(rows)</code> and <code>removeAllData()</code> remove rows for good, without tracking: for rows that never\ncame from a server.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"rows-data\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-data-out\">Data out</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method</th>\n<th>returns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>getAllData()</code></td>\n<td>every row, unfiltered and in the order they were set. Rows marked for deletion included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getDataRows()</code></td>\n<td>the rows that pass the filter, sorted. No group rows, and the rows of collapsed groups included</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getRows()</code></td>\n<td>the rows as the grid shows them: when grouped, a group row in front of each group, and the rows of a collapsed group left out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getRow(n)</code></td>\n<td>one row of <code>getRows()</code>, 0 based. <code>undefined</code> out of range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>length()</code></td>\n<td><code>getRows().length</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getSelectedRows()</code></td>\n<td>the selected rows, in displayed order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>currentEntity</code></td>\n<td>the current row, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate(field, fn, rows?)</code></td>\n<td>the sum, average, smallest or largest number of a field, or how many values it has: over <code>getDataRows()</code>, a group row&#39;s rows or rows you give. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/from-code\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>getRows()</code> and <code>getRow()</code> can return a group row when the datasource is grouped. <code>isGroupRow(row)</code>\ntells them apart, see <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/group-rows\">Sorting and grouping</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nfor (const row of datasource.getRows()) {\n    if (isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log(row.__groupName, row.__groupTotal);\n    } else {\n        console.log(row.name);\n    }\n}\n\nconst visibleNames = datasource.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; row.name);</code></pre>\n<p><code>getDataRows()</code> and <code>getRows()</code> return the datasource&#39;s own lists. Read them, do not change them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-current-row\">The current row</h3>\n<p><code>datasource.currentEntity</code> is the current row: the one the user clicked, or moved to with the\nkeyboard, or the last one selected from code. It is <code>null</code> until a row is made current. Use it for a\ndetail form next to the grid:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.on('current-entity', () =&gt; {\n    const row = datasource.currentEntity;\n    if (row &amp;&amp; !isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log('show in the form', row);\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.setRowAsCurrentEntity(0); // the first displayed row, 0 based</code></pre>\n<p>When grouped it can be a group row: moving the focus with the keyboard from the first data row up\ninto the header filters makes row 0 current, and that is a group row. Check with <code>isGroupRow()</code>.</p>\n<p><code>setData()</code> sets <code>currentEntity</code> to <code>null</code>, and sends <code>current-entity</code>, when the current row is no\nlonger in the data: replaced rows are new objects, also when a row with the same key comes back. Rows\nadded with <code>add: true</code>, or the same row objects set again, keep it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-keys\">Keys</h2>\n<p><code>key</code> names the field that identifies a row, e.g. <code>&#39;id&#39;</code>. With a key:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the selection follows the key, so it survives <code>setData()</code> with fresh rows from the server</li>\n<li><code>getChanges()</code> gives each edited row by its key, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/change-tracking\">Editing</a></li>\n<li>a row without a value in the key field gets a generated one, a negative number, written into\nthat field. A new row from <code>addNewEmpty()</code> gets one too, until the server gives it its own:\n<code>acceptChanges({ newKeys })</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without a key every row gets a generated key that is not written into the row (<code>row.__KEY</code> reads\nit). It is new every time the data is set, so the selection is lost on <code>setData()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-fields-and-types\">Fields and types</h2>\n<p><code>fields</code> lists the fields that are not plain text, with their type. Writing only the type\n(<code>born: &#39;date&#39;</code>) is the same as <code>born: { type: &#39;date&#39; }</code>. A field that is not listed is <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>,\nlabelled with its name.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>sorts and filters</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>edited with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code></td>\n<td>string</td>\n<td>as text, filters ignore case</td>\n<td>the text; the first line of a text of several lines</td>\n<td>an input; the editor over the cell for a text of several lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;number&#39;</code></td>\n<td>number</td>\n<td>as numbers</td>\n<td><code>12.5</code>, right aligned</td>\n<td>an input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code></td>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td>true / false</td>\n<td>a checkbox</td>\n<td>the checkbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;date&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Date</code></td>\n<td>on the day, the time is ignored</td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code></td>\n<td>an input and a date picker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetime&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>Date</code></td>\n<td>on the full timestamp</td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T08:15:00</code></td>\n<td>an input and a date picker</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong><code>date</code> or <code>datetime</code>?</strong> Both hold a <code>Date</code>. A <code>date</code> field is about the day: a filter\n<code>born = 2026-11-20</code> matches any time on that day, sorting and grouping ignore the time, and a change\nof the time only is not an edit. A <code>datetime</code> field keeps the time everywhere: filters, sorting,\ngrouping, and a change of the time is an edit (to the second).</p>\n<p>A <code>FieldConfig</code> can also have a <code>label</code> (the grid&#39;s column label falls back to it, and so does\n<code>getFilterString()</code>), a <code>formatter</code>: the id of a registered formatter, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>, <code>validate</code>: the field&#39;s validation rules, see below, and\n<code>multiline</code>: a text of several lines, a text field only - see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline\ntext</a>. <code>getField()</code> and <code>getFormatter()</code> give the formatter object.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        born: { type: 'date', label: 'Born' },\n        price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberComma', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }] },\n        notes: { multiline: true }\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.getField('born'); // { name: 'born', type: 'date', label: 'Born', formatter: DateFormatterYYYYMMDD, validate: [], multiline: false }\ndatasource.getField('name'); // { name: 'name', type: 'text', label: 'name', formatter: TextFormatter, validate: [], multiline: false }\ndatasource.getField('notes').multiline; // true\ndatasource.getField('price').validate; // [{ id: 'required' }, { id: 'min', value: 0 }]\ndatasource.getFormatter('born').format(new Date(2026, 10, 20)); // '2026-11-20'\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12.5); // '12,5'</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-validation-rules\">Validation rules</h3>\n<p><code>validate</code> lists the field&#39;s rules: the id of a validator as text (<code>&#39;required&#39;</code>), or an object with\nthe id, its options and a message of its own (<code>{ id: &#39;max&#39;, value: 150, message: &#39;too old&#39; }</code>). The\nbuilt-ins are <code>required</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>minLength</code>, <code>maxLength</code> and <code>pattern</code>; your own are\nregistered by id with <code>registerValidator()</code>, like the formatters, so the fields stay plain JSON. An\nid that is not registered throws when the datasource is made.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: {\n        name: { validate: ['required'] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'too old' }] },\n        zip: { validate: [{ id: 'pattern', value: '^[0-9]{4}$', message: 'Four digits' }] }\n    }\n});\n\npeople.setData([{ id: 1, name: 'Ann', age: 36, zip: '0150' }]);\npeople.getDataRows()[0].age = -1; // an edit is validated\npeople.getErrors(); // [{ row: &lt;Ann&gt;, field: 'age', messages: ['Must be at least 0'] }]\npeople.validate(); // every row, before a save\npeople.hasErrors(); // true</code></pre>\n<p>An edit is validated as it is made, <code>validate()</code> checks every row, and the grid shows the errors on\nthe cells and the row numbers. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a> for the rules, the\nmessages, when they run and rules of your own.</p>\n<p>To sort text with the rules of a language, set a collator: <code>datasource.setLocaleCompare(&#39;nb&#39;)</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-selection\">Selection</h2>\n<p>The user selects rows by clicking them: ctrl+click adds a row, shift+click a range. A click on the\ntop left corner of the grid selects every row, ctrl+click there deselects them. That is the default\n<code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> mode, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-selection-mode\">below</a> for the others. From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.select(1); // the first displayed row: select() counts from 1, getRow() from 0\ndatasource.select(5, { notify: true }); // and the grid scrolls to it\ndatasource.selectNext(); // also selectFirst(), selectPrev(), selectLast()\ndatasource.selectAll(); // 'multiple' mode only\ndatasource.deselectAll({ notify: true }); // notify: the grid shows it at once\n\nconst selected = datasource.getSelectedRows();</code></pre>\n<p><code>select()</code> and the <code>select*()</code> methods make the row current too. A group row can not be selected,\n<code>select()</code> takes the first data row after it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-selection-mode\">The selection mode</h3>\n<p>The datasource has the selection mode, the grid uses it - a grid has no option for it. Give it to\n<code>new Datasource()</code>, or change it later, also while a grid shows the datasource:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({ key: 'id', selectionMode: 'single' });\nconst config: GridConfig = { layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] } };\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource: people });\n\npeople.setSelectionMode('none'); // a click makes the row current, selects nothing</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>mode</th>\n<th>a click</th>\n<th>ctrl+click, shift+click</th>\n<th><code>selectAll()</code>, the top left corner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> (default)</td>\n<td>selects the row</td>\n<td>adds a row, selects a range</td>\n<td>selects every displayed row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;single&#39;</code></td>\n<td>selects the row</td>\n<td>selects the row, as a click does</td>\n<td>does nothing, sends no event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;none&#39;</code></td>\n<td>selects nothing</td>\n<td>selects nothing</td>\n<td>does nothing, sends no event</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>In every mode a click makes the row current.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"selection\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-sharing-the-rows-between-two-datasources\">Sharing the rows between two datasources</h2>\n<p>Two datasources can share their rows through one <code>DataContainer</code>, e.g. two grids that show the same\ndata with a different filter. Each has its own filter, sort, grouping and selection. The key goes to\nthe container:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { DataContainer, Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst container = new DataContainer('id');\nconst left = new Datasource({ container, fields: { born: 'date' } });\nconst right = new Datasource({ container, fields: { born: 'date' } });\n\nleft.setData([{ id: 1, name: 'Ann', born: new Date(1990, 4, 17) }]);\nright.reloadDatasource(); // right shows the new rows once it rebuilds its own</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The rows and their edits are the container&#39;s: an edit is the same edit in both, and\n<code>getChanges()</code> gives the same result on both. So is the undo history: <code>undo()</code> on one takes back\nan edit made through the other, and both send <code>history-changed</code>. The grid of the other one shows\nan undo too, it renders its cells again on that event.</li>\n<li>Each datasource keeps its own list of displayed rows. After <code>setData()</code>, <code>removeData()</code>,\n<code>acceptChanges()</code> or <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code> on one, call <code>reloadDatasource()</code> (or <code>filter()</code>)\non the other. Its selection is kept by key: a new row that <code>acceptChanges({ newKeys })</code> gave the\nserver&#39;s key is no longer selected there.</li>\n<li>A field listed in both must have the same type in both, the second datasource throws if not.\nThe validation rules are the container&#39;s too: a field with rules in one datasource is validated\non every edit of the rows, and two datasources that both give a field rules must give it the same.\nThe errors are the container&#39;s, like the changes - <code>validate()</code> on one sends its event on that one\nonly: <code>refresh()</code> the other grid.</li>\n<li>A <code>key</code> given to a datasource with a <code>container</code> must be the container&#39;s key, or it throws.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-events\">Events</h2>\n<p>The datasource sends events when its rows change, are sorted, filtered or grouped, and when the\nselection or the current row changes. Subscribe with <code>datasource.on()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-rows-from-a-server-upsert\">Rows from a server: <code>upsert()</code></h2>\n<p><code>setData()</code> replaces the rows. To merge what a server sends into the rows the grid shows - a poll\nevery second, changes pushed to the page - use <code>upsert()</code>: it updates the rows by their key, adds the\nnew keys and, with <code>removeMissing</code>, removes the rows the server no longer has. The selection, the\nexpanded groups and the scroll position stay, and an edit the user has not saved is not overwritten.\nSee <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const rowsFromServer: object[];\n\ndatasource.upsert(rowsFromServer, { removeMissing: true });</code></pre>\n"},{"id":"editing","title":"Editing","html":"<p>The grid is readonly by default. With editing on, the user changes values in the cells, and the\ndatasource tracks every change: which rows are new, edited or marked for deletion, and what changed.\nThe rules of the fields validate the edits, <code>validate()</code> checks every row before a save.\n<code>getChanges()</code> collects the changes for a server, and <code>acceptChanges()</code> takes them in once they are\nsaved.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-editing-on\">Editing on</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false, // the default is true\n    columns: {\n        id: { readonly: true } // this column stays readonly\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['id'], width: 60 },\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['born'], width: 110 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>Or turn it on and off while the grid is shown: <code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ readonly: false })</code>.</p>\n<p>In an editable grid, a cell is readonly when the <code>isReadonly(row, field)</code> hook returns <code>true</code>,\nand editable when it returns <code>false</code>. When it returns nothing (or there is none), the column&#39;s\n<code>readonly</code> option decides. See <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/readonly-and-css-classes-from-the-row\">Grid config</a>\nfor <code>isReadonly</code>. Readonly cells of an editable grid are shown with stripes.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"editing\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-the-user-edits\">How the user edits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Text, number and date cells</strong> are inputs. What the user types is parsed with the field&#39;s\nformatter (<code>parse</code>) and stored in the row as they type: a number field stores a number, a date\nfield a <code>Date</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Emptying a cell</strong> stores what the formatter&#39;s <code>parse(&#39;&#39;)</code> gives: <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for text, <code>null</code> for a\ndate, and <strong><code>0</code> for a number</strong> - the number formatters parse text that is no number as 0. The row\nmenu&#39;s clear (below) stores <code>null</code> in any field. For an emptied number cell to store <code>null</code>, give\nthe field a formatter of your own, see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/the-defaults-per-type\">Formatting</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Enter</strong> in an editable cell moves to the cell below - in a cell of several lines it opens the\neditor, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Date cells</strong> also open a date picker on click, when they are editable: &quot;Use&quot; stores the picked\ndate and time, &quot;Clear&quot; stores <code>null</code>. The picker needs <code>@simple-html/grid/date.css</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Boolean cells</strong> are checkboxes.</li>\n<li><strong>Paste and clear</strong> are in the row menu (right click a cell): paste puts the clipboard text into\nthat column of every selected row, clear sets it to <code>null</code>. Rows where the cell is readonly are\nskipped. The grid sends a <code>paste</code> event per row and a <code>clear</code> event, see <a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A column with <code>allowPasteClearOnly: true</code> can not be typed in, only pasted into and cleared. Use it\nfor a field the user sets with a picker of your own, such as a <a href=\"#guide/menus/a-dropdown-grid-as-a-block\">dropdown grid</a>.</p>\n<p>The grid sends no event with the edit itself. Read the changes when you need them, with\n<code>getChanges()</code> or the row&#39;s <code>__rowState</code> (below). <code>history-changed</code> says that an edit was recorded\nfor undo, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-multiline-text\">Multiline text</h2>\n<p>Notes, an address, a comment: a text of several lines. The input of a cell can not hold a line break -\nthe browser strips them from the value of <code>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot;&gt;</code> - so a cell of several lines shows its\nfirst line, and is edited in an editor over the cell, a bigger textarea.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        notes: { label: 'Notes', multiline: true }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The option</strong>: <code>multiline: true</code> in the field&#39;s <code>FieldConfig</code>, for a text field only - on another\ntype it throws when the datasource is made. <code>getField(&#39;notes&#39;).multiline</code> says it.</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic</strong>: a text cell whose value has a line break is multiline for that value, without the\noption. CR LF, CR and LF are line breaks. The option is for a field whose values have no line break\nyet: its cells are edited in the editor from the start.</li>\n<li><strong>The cell</strong> shows the first line, readonly (no typing in it), with a marker at its right when there\nare more lines. The whole text is its tooltip, after the validation errors when it has any (an empty\nline between them), cut after 15 lines or 600 characters. The rows keep their height.</li>\n<li><strong>Opening the editor</strong>: Enter, F2, a double click - or typing a character, in a cell that can be\nedited: the editor opens with the text and the character at its end (nothing is replaced). A click\nonly focuses the cell; Tab, Alt+arrows and the row menu (Shift+F10) work as in any cell.</li>\n<li><strong>In the editor</strong> the textarea has the text and the focus, the caret at the end. It is as wide as\nthe cell, at least 300px, six lines high, and can be resized. It is put over the cell, its top left\ncorner on the cell&#39;s, and kept in the window.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>in the editor</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>a new line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Enter, Cmd+Enter</td>\n<td>saves and closes, the focus back in the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes without saving, the focus back in the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>saves and closes, and goes on to the next / previous cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a click outside it</td>\n<td>saves and closes, the focus goes where the click put it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>scrolling the grid (the wheel, a swipe, the scrollbars)</td>\n<td>saves and closes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Opening a menu or another editor saves and closes it too - one is open at a time - and so does\n<code>destroy()</code>, or the grid leaving the page.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Saving</strong> writes the row once, and only when the text changed: one step of the <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">undo\nhistory</a> (Ctrl+Z in the cell takes it back), validated as an edit, and the cell gets\nthe edited marker. The line breaks of a saved text are LF (<code>\\n</code>): the textarea gives them so (the\nHTML spec normalises the value of a textarea to LF), a text from a server with CR LF keeps them\nuntil the user changes it. The value goes through the field&#39;s formatter (<code>parse</code>), as what is typed\nin a cell.</li>\n<li><strong>Readonly</strong>: in a readonly grid, a readonly column or cell (<code>readonly</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> hook) and\na column with <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code>, Enter, F2 and a double click open the editor read-only, to read\nthe whole text: Esc (or Ctrl+Enter, Tab, a click outside) closes it. Typing opens nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Paste</strong> from the row menu writes the clipboard text as it is, line breaks and all.</li>\n<li>The texts of the editor are <code>cell.multiline.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/multiline-cells\">Texts</a>, the\ncolour of the marker <code>--simple-html-grid-multiline-color</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/multiline-text\">Theming</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"multiline\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-edits-from-code\">Edits from code</h2>\n<p>Set the value on a row you got from the datasource. The grid shows it on its own, in the next\nanimation frame (see <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>) - no <code>refresh()</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nrow.name = 'Anna';</code></pre>\n<p>The datasource wraps your objects in proxies that track the edits. A value set on the object you\ngave to <code>setData()</code> reaches the row, but it is not tracked as an edit. Always use the rows the\ndatasource hands out: <code>getDataRows()</code>, <code>getSelectedRows()</code>, <code>currentEntity</code>, the <code>rowData</code> of an\nevent.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-new-rows\">New rows</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.addNewEmpty(); // an empty row at the end, selected, and the grid scrolls to it\ndatasource.addNewEmpty({ company: 'NEW COMPANY' }); // with values\ndatasource.addNewEmpty({ company: 'NEW COMPANY' }, { scrollTo: false }); // selection unchanged</code></pre>\n<p>A new row is listed in <code>getChanges().newEntities</code>. When the datasource has a <code>key</code>, the key field\nof the new row gets a generated negative number. When grouped, the row goes into its group and the\ngroup is expanded.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</h2>\n<p>Deleting a row marks it, so the delete can be undone:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.markForDeletion(datasource.getSelectedRows()); // a row or a list of rows\ndatasource.unmarkForDeletion(datasource.getSelectedRows()); // undo\ndatasource.markAllForDeletion(); // every row\ndatasource.unmarkAllForDeletion(); // undo for every row</code></pre>\n<p>A row marked for deletion stays in the grid, with the state <code>&#39;D&#39;</code> - struck through, with a trash can\non its row number, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a> - until the changes are saved.\nIt is listed in <code>getChanges().deletedEntities</code>. Unmarked, it is as it was before: its edits are kept.</p>\n<p>A new row is marked too. While it is marked it is in none of the lists of <code>getChanges()</code>: it was\nnever saved, there is nothing to send. Unmarked, it is a new row again.</p>\n<p>In an editable grid the row menu (right click a cell) has a <strong>Delete:</strong> section: <em>Rows (selected\nrows)</em> marks the selected rows, <em>Undo (selected rows)</em> takes the mark off - shown when a selected row\nis marked. Ctrl+Z takes a delete back too, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>. A row the <code>isReadonly</code> hook returns <code>true</code> for in every column is locked: the menu does not\nmark it. The columns&#39; <code>readonly</code> option does not lock a row, so a grid of readonly columns can still\ndelete rows. The entries are <code>row.delete</code>, <code>row.delete.rows</code> and <code>row.delete.undo</code>, change or hide\nthem with the <a href=\"#guide/menus\">menus hook</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>datasource.acceptChanges()</code> removes the marked rows once the deletes are saved, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a>. <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code> removes them too, and leaves the other changes.</li>\n<li><code>datasource.removeData(rows)</code> and <code>removeAllData()</code> remove rows for good, without marking them:\nno undo, and not listed in <code>getChanges()</code>. Use them for rows that never came from a server.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-change-tracking\">Change tracking</h2>\n<p>Every row has a state, <code>row.__rowState</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>state</th>\n<th>the row</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;N&#39;</code></td>\n<td>is new: <code>addNewEmpty()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;M&#39;</code></td>\n<td>has a field whose value differs from the original</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;D&#39;</code></td>\n<td>is marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>undefined</code></td>\n<td>is as it was set</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>getChanges()</code> returns copies of the changed rows, what to send to a server:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    born: Date | null;\n}\n\nconst people = new Datasource&lt;Person&gt;({ key: 'id', fields: { born: 'date' } });\npeople.setData([\n    { id: 1, name: 'Ann', born: new Date(1990, 4, 17) },\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', born: new Date(1985, 0, 2) }\n]);\n\nconst [ann, bob] = people.getDataRows();\nann.name = 'Anna';\nbob.born = new Date(1985, 0, 2, 13, 30); // the same day: no edit on a date field\npeople.addNewEmpty({ name: 'Cid' }, { scrollTo: false });\n\nconst { newEntities, modifiedEntities, deletedEntities } = people.getChanges();\n// newEntities:      [{ name: 'Cid', id: -1 }] - a generated key, the number varies\n// modifiedEntities: [{ id: 1, name: 'Anna' }] - the key and the changed fields\n// deletedEntities:  []</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>newEntities</code> and <code>deletedEntities</code> are copies of the whole rows. A row in <code>modifiedEntities</code> has\nthe key (under the key field&#39;s name, or <code>__KEY</code> without a key) and only the fields that changed.</li>\n<li>The copies are shallow and keep the types: a <code>Date</code> stays a <code>Date</code>. <code>JSON.stringify()</code> turns it\ninto its ISO text.</li>\n<li>A field set back to its original value is not a change any more. Setting an empty field\n(<code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code>) to <code>&#39;&#39;</code> is not a change either.</li>\n<li>A <code>date</code> field compares on the day: a date typed into a cell gets the current time of day, and\ntyping the same day again is no change. A <code>datetime</code> field, and a field not listed in <code>fields</code>,\ncompares to the second: a change of the time only is a change.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After a save, <code>acceptChanges()</code> makes the changes the new starting point, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/saving\">Saving</a>.\nBefore it, <code>validate()</code> checks the rows, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-throwing-changes-away\">Throwing changes away</h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.resetData(); // every edit undone, marked rows unmarked, new rows removed\ndatasource.resetDataSelectionOnly(); // the same, for the selected rows only</code></pre>\n<p>To undo a delete and keep the edits, use <code>unmarkForDeletion(rows)</code>. To revert one cell, or rows\nwithout removing the new ones, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>.\n<code>resetData()</code> clears the undo history; <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> is a step of it, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</h3>\n<p>The grid shows the state of each row on its row number, and marks the edited cells. Both are on by\ndefault:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>the row</th>\n<th>its row number</th>\n<th>its cells</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>new</td>\n<td>a plus</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>edited</td>\n<td>a pencil</td>\n<td>an edited cell: a small triangle in its top left corner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>marked for deletion</td>\n<td>a trash can, the number struck through</td>\n<td>the text struck through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>with an error</td>\n<td>an exclamation mark, and a red bar</td>\n<td>a cell with an error: a red border and a tint</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One icon</strong>: a row marked for deletion shows the trash can (it is not saved, its errors do not\nstop a save), else a row with an error the exclamation mark, else the plus or the pencil.</li>\n<li><strong>The tooltip of the row number</strong> has the state, then the errors, a line each: <code>Edited</code> and\n<code>Age: Must be at least 18</code>. A screen reader reads them with the row number, see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/what-a-screen-reader-gets\">Accessibility</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>An edited cell</strong> is a cell whose value differs from its original value, as <code>getChanges()</code> compares\nthem: a cell edited back is not edited. A new row has no markers, all of it is new.</li>\n<li><strong>The marker&#39;s tooltip</strong> has the original value, shown with the field&#39;s formatter (a boolean as\n<code>true</code> / <code>false</code>: give the field a formatter of your own for other words, see\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>), after the cell&#39;s errors. A blank value is <code>(blank)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A click on the marker</strong> puts the original value back - the row menu&#39;s <em>Undo cell edit</em>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>. In an editable grid only: a readonly grid\nshows the markers of the rows it is given, a click does nothing. The marker is not a stop of Tab\nand a screen reader does not see it; Ctrl+Z takes an edit back from the keyboard.</li>\n<li><strong>They follow the edits</strong>: as the user types, after undo and redo, a paste, a delete, and an edit\nfrom code.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <a href=\"#sample/editing\">editing sample</a> shows them. Both can be turned off:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    rowStatus: false, // the plain row number: no icon, no state in its tooltip\n    editedMarker: false, // no markers\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>rowStatus</code> makes the row numbers 14px wider, for the icon. <code>rowStatus: false</code> gives them the width\nthey had.</li>\n<li><strong>The classes are there either way</strong>, for a look of your own: <code>row-is-new</code>, <code>row-is-modified</code> and\n<code>row-is-deleted</code> on the element around the row number, <code>simple-html-row-error</code> on the row number of\na row with an error, <code>simple-html-cell-edited</code> on an edited cell. With <code>rowStatus</code> the cells of a row\nmarked for deletion get <code>simple-html-cell-deleted</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The colours are variables of the theme</strong>: <code>--simple-html-grid-new-color</code>,\n<code>--simple-html-grid-edited-color</code> (the pencil and the markers), <code>--simple-html-grid-deleted-color</code>\nand <code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code>, each with a light and a dark colour - see\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-row-state-and-errors\">Theming</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A look of your own, with <code>rowStatus: false</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.row-is-modified .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#4f46e5, #a5b4fc);\n}\n.row-is-deleted .simple-html-selector-text {\n    text-decoration: line-through;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Or show <code>__rowState</code> in a column of its own: put <code>&#39;__rowState&#39;</code> in the layout.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-changed-rows-only\">Changed rows only</h3>\n<p><code>filterChangedOnly(true)</code> shows only the changed rows - new, edited and marked for deletion - of the\nrows the filter lets through: the changes made before a save, to check them.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.filter(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'));\ndatasource.filterChangedOnly(true); // the changed rows in Norway\ndatasource.isChangedOnly(); // true\ndatasource.filterChangedOnly(false); // every row in Norway</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In the grid</strong>: the row menu&#39;s <em>View: Changed rows only</em> turns it on and off (a ✓ while it is\non), and the footer shows a chip <em>Changed rows only</em> in front of the filter&#39;s. The footer&#39;s <em>Clear</em>\nand the filter menu&#39;s <em>Clear All Filters</em> turn it off with the filter.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not part of the filter</strong>, it is ANDed with it: <code>getFilter()</code>, <code>getFilterString()</code>, the filter\neditor and the grid&#39;s state (<code>GridState</code>) do not have it, and a header filter, the filter editor\nand <code>filter()</code> leave it on. It is not saved with the state: the changes are not either.</li>\n<li><strong>It is applied when the rows are filtered</strong>, as the filter is: <code>filter()</code>, a header filter,\n<code>setData()</code>, <code>reloadDatasource()</code>, <code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code> ... An edit, undo and a\nrevert do not filter the rows: <strong>a row edited back to its original values stays shown until the\nrows are filtered next</strong>, it does not disappear while the user types in it. A row changed from code\nwhile it is not shown comes in on the next filter run too.</li>\n<li>After <code>setData()</code> with the rows from the server, or <code>acceptChanges()</code>, no row is changed: nothing\nis shown until it is turned off. The footer&#39;s chip says why.</li>\n<li>It is the datasource&#39;s, as the filter is: other datasources on the same container are not filtered.</li>\n<li>The filter menu&#39;s value list, with <em>Filter Available</em>, lists the values of the changed rows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For one state only, filter on <code>__rowState</code>: <code>where(&#39;__rowState&#39;, &#39;IN&#39;, [&#39;N&#39;])</code> is the new rows. That\nis a condition of the filter, in <code>getFilter()</code> and the state.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</h2>\n<p>The datasource keeps a history of the edits: <code>undo()</code> takes the last step back, <code>redo()</code> does it\nagain. In the grid the user has them too:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ctrl+Z</strong> (Cmd+Z on a Mac) undoes, <strong>Ctrl+Y</strong> and <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Z</strong> (Cmd+Shift+Z) redo - with the\nfocus in a cell of an editable grid. Not in the header filters and the filter editor: the keys are\nthe input&#39;s own there.</li>\n<li><strong>While the user types in a cell, Ctrl+Z is the input&#39;s own undo</strong> of the typing. The grid writes\nevery key to the row as it is typed, so what was typed in the cell is the last step of the history;\nonce the typing is undone back to the value the cell started with, or the focus left the cell,\nCtrl+Z undoes the history.</li>\n<li>The row menu (right click a cell) has an <strong>Edit:</strong> section: <em>Undo (Ctrl+Z)</em> and <em>Redo (Ctrl+Y)</em>,\ngreyed out when there is nothing to undo or redo, and the reverts, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>. The ids are <code>row.edit</code>, <code>row.edit.undo</code>,\n<code>row.edit.redo</code>, <code>row.edit.revertCell</code>, <code>row.edit.revertRow</code> and <code>row.edit.revertRows</code>, change or hide them with the\n<a href=\"#guide/menus\">menus hook</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nrow.name = 'Changed';\n\ndatasource.canUndo(); // true\ndatasource.undo(); // the name it had\ndatasource.redo(); // 'Changed' again</code></pre>\n<p><strong>A step</strong> is what one undo takes back:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>what the user typed in one cell, from when it got the focus until the focus left it</li>\n<li>a paste or a clear from the row menu, over every selected row</li>\n<li>a paste (Ctrl+V), a Delete or a clear of ranges of cells, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></li>\n<li>a delete or its undo: <code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>markAllForDeletion()</code>, <code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>,\n<code>unmarkAllForDeletion()</code> - from the row menu or from code</li>\n<li>a revert: <code>resetField()</code>, <code>resetRows()</code>, <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code></li>\n<li><strong>what code sets on the rows in one synchronous run</strong>: <code>row.name = &#39;x&#39;</code> is an edit like the user&#39;s,\nand is recorded. The changes made before the code awaits are one step, after an <code>await</code> the next\none starts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A value set that does not change the field - as the change tracking compares, so a <code>date</code> field on\nthe day - is not recorded.</p>\n<p><strong>Undo sets the values back the way an edit does</strong>: the change tracking follows (a value back at its\noriginal is no change, the <code>__rowState</code> goes back with it) and so does the validation - an error an\nedit made goes away when it is undone. The displayed rows are not filtered, sorted or grouped again,\nthe rows stay where they are, as after an edit. The grid renders its cells again.</p>\n<p><strong>What is not in it</strong>: new rows - <code>addNewEmpty()</code>, <code>setData(rows, { add: true })</code>. Undo never removes\na row and never adds one back; the edits of a new row are steps like any other. Rows removed for good\n(<code>removeData()</code>, <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code>, a new row <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> removed) take their\nchanges out of the history, and a step left without changes is dropped.</p>\n<p><strong>It is cleared</strong> - nothing to undo or redo, the edits stay as they are - by <code>setData()</code> without\n<code>add</code>, <code>removeAllData()</code>, <code>resetData()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code> (after a save the saved values are the\noriginals, also when only some rows were accepted) and <code>clearHistory()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>The last 100 steps</strong> are kept. A new step empties what there is to redo.</p>\n<p>The history is the <code>DataContainer</code>&#39;s, like the edits: datasources that share a container share it.</p>\n<p><code>history-changed</code> is sent when the history changes - for Undo and Redo buttons of your own:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const undoButton: HTMLButtonElement;\ndeclare const redoButton: HTMLButtonElement;\n\nconst showHistory = () =&gt; {\n    undoButton.disabled = !datasource.canUndo();\n    redoButton.disabled = !datasource.canRedo();\n};\ndatasource.on('history-changed', showHistory);\nshowHistory();\nundoButton.onclick = () =&gt; datasource.undo();\nredoButton.onclick = () =&gt; datasource.redo();</code></pre>\n<p>Its <code>info</code> says what changed it, see <a href=\"#guide/events/datasource-events\">Events</a>. A new step is told once\nthe code that made it is done (<code>&#39;edit&#39;</code>, in a microtask): a paste over many rows is one event, and so\nis what is typed in a cell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</h2>\n<p><code>resetField(row, field)</code> puts one field back to its original value: the value it had when the data\nwas set, or when the changes were last accepted. <code>resetRows(rows)</code> does it for every field of the\nrows, and takes their deletion marks off:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\ndatasource.resetField(row, 'name');\ndatasource.resetRows(datasource.getSelectedRows());</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A revert is a step of the history: <code>undo()</code> brings the edits back.</li>\n<li><strong>A new row stays</strong>, with the values it was added with, still new: a revert can be undone, and\nundo never adds a row back. To drop a new row, delete it. <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> removes the\nnew rows it resets, and undo does not bring those back.</li>\n<li>The fields are validated as for an edit.</li>\n<li>A field that is not edited (<code>getChanges()</code> would not list it) is left as it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In an editable grid the row menu (right click a cell) starts with an <strong>Edit:</strong> section:\n<em>Undo last change</em> (Ctrl+Z) and <em>Redo</em> (Ctrl+Y) go through the undo history; <em>Undo cell edit</em> puts\nthe original value of the cell right clicked back, greyed out while it is not edited - a click on the\nmarker of an edited cell does the same, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a>;\n<em>Undo row edits</em> puts the row right clicked back - no need to select it first - and\n<em>Undo row edits (selected rows)</em> the selected rows, each greyed out while none of its rows is edited\nor marked for deletion. Like the delete&#39;s undo they also work on readonly cells: they put values back,\nreadonly is about typing new ones.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-validation\">Validation</h2>\n<p>The rules are part of the datasource&#39;s fields, plain JSON like the rest of them - they can be\nstored in a backend with the fields. A field lists its rules in <code>validate</code>: the id of a validator as\ntext, or an object with the id, its options and a message of its own.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: {\n        name: { validate: ['required', { id: 'maxLength', value: 50 }] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: ['required', { id: 'min', value: 0 }, { id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'too old' }] },\n        email: { validate: [{ id: 'pattern', value: '^[^@\\\\s]+@[^@\\\\s]+$', message: 'Not an email address' }] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The validators are named by id, like the formatters: the built-in ones below, and your own with\n<code>registerValidator()</code>. An id that is not registered throws when the datasource is made, and so does\na built-in rule with a wrong option (<code>min</code> without a number, a <code>pattern</code> that is no regular\nexpression). <code>getField(name).validate</code> gives the rules of a field, written out as objects.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"validation\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-built-in-rules\">The built-in rules</h3>\n<p>They take their option as <code>value</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>rule</th>\n<th>the value fails when</th>\n<th>message</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;required&#39;</code></td>\n<td>it is <code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid Date, or 0</td>\n<td><code>Required</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;min&#39;, value: 0 }</code></td>\n<td>it is a number below <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;max&#39;, value: 150 }</code></td>\n<td>it is a number above <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;minLength&#39;, value: 2 }</code></td>\n<td>it is text shorter than <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value} characters</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;maxLength&#39;, value: 50 }</code></td>\n<td>it is text longer than <code>value</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value} characters</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ id: &#39;pattern&#39;, value: &#39;^[A-Z]{2}$&#39; }</code></td>\n<td>it is text the regular expression does not find</td>\n<td><code>Not in the expected format</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>0 fails <code>required</code>.</strong> The filter takes 0 as blank too (see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>),\nand an emptied number cell stores 0 - the number formatters parse <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as 0 - so with 0 as a value\na required number would never fail in the grid. <code>false</code> passes: it is a value, and a checkbox has\nno empty state. For a number where 0 is a real value, use a rule of your own (<code>present</code> below).</li>\n<li><strong>The other rules pass an empty value</strong> (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>): an optional field can be\nempty, add <code>&#39;required&#39;</code> to make it fail. They check the values of their kind only - <code>min</code> and\n<code>max</code> numbers, the others text - and pass the rest.</li>\n<li><strong><code>pattern</code> is not anchored</strong>: <code>&#39;[0-9]&#39;</code> finds a digit anywhere in the text, <code>&#39;^[0-9]+$&#39;</code> is\ndigits only. Written as text, so it stays JSON. In TypeScript a <code>\\</code> is written twice.</li>\n<li>Every rule runs: a value can fail more than one, and gets a message for each.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-messages\">Messages</h3>\n<p>A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> replaces the default, and <code>{name}</code> in it is replaced by the rule&#39;s option <code>name</code>:\n<code>{ id: &#39;max&#39;, value: 150, message: &#39;at most {value}&#39; }</code> gives <code>at most 150</code>.</p>\n<p>The default messages are texts: <code>&#39;validation.required&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.min&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.max&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;validation.minLength&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;validation.maxLength&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;validation.pattern&#39;</code> in <code>TEXTS</code>. Translate\nthem with <code>setTexts()</code> - for the page, the datasource has no grid, so a grid&#39;s <code>texts</code> do not change\nthem. A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> can be a text id as well, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/messages-of-your-own\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({ 'validation.required': 'Må fylles ut', 'validation.max': 'Kan være høyst {value}' });</code></pre>\n<p>A message is made when the value is validated: after <code>setTexts()</code> an error already there keeps its\ntext until the field is validated again.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-rules-of-your-own\">Rules of your own</h3>\n<p>A validator is a function <code>(value, row, rule) =&gt; message | null</code>: <code>null</code> when the value is valid,\nelse the message - or a text id, the way the built-ins return <code>&#39;validation.required&#39;</code>. An id of your\nown gets its text from <code>setTexts()</code>, so it can be translated. <code>rule</code> is\nthe rule written out, so a rule can carry options of its own. Register it before the datasource that\nnames it is made:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerValidator } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a number that must be there, 0 included\nregisterValidator('present', (value) =&gt; (value === null || value === undefined ? 'validation.required' : null));\n\n// { id: 'oneOf', values: [...] }: the options come from the rule\nregisterValidator('oneOf', (value, _row, rule) =&gt; {\n    if (value === null || value === undefined || value === '') {\n        return null; // an empty value is the required rule's job\n    }\n    return Array.isArray(rule.values) &amp;&amp; rule.values.includes(String(value)) ? null : 'Must be one of {values}';\n});\n\n// another field of the row: the end can not be before the start\nregisterValidator('afterStart', (value, row) =&gt;\n    value instanceof Date &amp;&amp; row.start instanceof Date &amp;&amp; value &lt; row.start ? 'Before the start' : null\n);\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        discount: { type: 'number', validate: ['present'] },\n        country: { validate: [{ id: 'oneOf', values: ['Norway', 'Sweden'] }] },\n        start: 'date',\n        end: { type: 'date', validate: ['afterStart'] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Keep a validator fast, it runs on every key the user types. Registering an id again replaces it, for\nthe datasources made after.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-when-it-runs\">When it runs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An edit is validated</strong>, from the grid as the user types and from code (<code>row.age = -1</code>): the\nfield&#39;s rules run, and so do the rules of the row&#39;s fields that have an error - an error that\ndepends on the edited value (<code>afterStart</code> above) clears once it is fixed. An edit that makes the\nfield valid clears its error.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows set with <code>setData()</code> are not validated, and neither are the fields of a new row from\n<code>addNewEmpty()</code></strong> until they are edited: an empty new row would be all errors before the user\ntyped anything. The stripes of the required fields show what to fill in, and <code>validate()</code> before\nthe save checks the fields never edited.</li>\n<li><strong><code>validate()</code> checks every row</strong>, or the rows given: every field with rules. It keeps the errors,\nthe grid shows them, and returns them - one <code>{ row, field, messages }</code> per field with an error.</li>\n<li><code>getErrors(rows?)</code> and <code>hasErrors()</code> read the errors there are now, without validating.</li>\n<li><code>acceptChanges()</code> and <code>resetData()</code> clear the errors, <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> those of the\nselected rows.</li>\n<li>Undo, redo, <code>resetField()</code> and <code>resetRows()</code> set values the way an edit does: the fields they set\nare validated.</li>\n<li>A row marked for deletion is not saved, so it has no errors: <code>validate()</code> skips it, <code>getErrors()</code>\nand <code>hasErrors()</code> leave it out. Its errors are back when it is unmarked.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Validate before a save:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare function showErrors(lines: string[]): void;\n\nconst errors = datasource.validate();\nif (errors.length) {\n    showErrors(errors.map(({ row, field, messages }) =&gt; `${row.name}, ${datasource.getField(field).label}: ${messages.join(', ')}`));\n} else {\n    const response = await fetch('/api/save', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(datasource.getChanges()) });\n    if (response.ok) {\n        datasource.acceptChanges();\n    }\n}\n\n// only what a save sends: the new and edited rows\ndatasource.validate(datasource.getAllData().filter((row) =&gt; row.__rowState === 'N' || row.__rowState === 'M'));</code></pre>\n<p><code>validate()</code> checks every row, also the rows the filter leaves out and the rows that were not\nedited: data from a server that breaks a rule blocks the save too. Pass the new and edited rows to\ncheck what the save sends only.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-how-the-grid-shows-the-errors\">How the grid shows the errors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A cell with an error</strong> gets the class <code>simple-html-cell-error</code> - a red border inside the cell\nand a tint - and its messages as the tooltip, one per line.</li>\n<li><strong>The row number</strong> of a row with an error gets <code>simple-html-row-error</code> - a red bar on its left and\na tint - and an exclamation mark (<code>rowStatus</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a>).\nIts tooltip lists the row&#39;s state, then the server&#39;s error, then a line per field:\n<code>Age: Must be at least 0</code>.</li>\n<li>The colors are css variables: <code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code> and\n<code>--simple-html-grid-error-bg-color</code>.</li>\n<li>The tooltips are the grid&#39;s own, as on the filter editor&#39;s buttons: <code>tooltips: false</code> in the\nconfig turns them off. The <code>data-tooltip</code> attribute is written either way, for a tooltip library\nof your own.</li>\n<li>An edit in the grid shows its errors at once, and so does <code>validate()</code>. An edit from code shows\nthem in the next animation frame, with its value.</li>\n<li>A field with the <code>required</code> rule shows stripes on its empty editable cells, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/required-fields-and-placeholders\">Required fields and placeholders</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-saving\">Saving</h2>\n<p>A save usually sends everything in one transaction: the deleted, edited and new rows of\n<code>getChanges()</code>. After a successful save, <code>acceptChanges()</code> makes the grid show what was saved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the rows marked for deletion are removed</li>\n<li>the new rows become ordinary rows</li>\n<li>the values the rows have now become their original values: nothing counts as edited</li>\n<li>the row errors (<code>setErrors()</code>) and the validation errors are cleared</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The displayed rows are rebuilt once, and <code>hasChanges()</code> is <code>false</code> after.</p>\n<p><strong>Keys of new rows.</strong> Until it is saved, a new row has a temporary key: a generated negative number,\nin the key field (<code>id: -1</code>), or as <code>__KEY</code> in <code>getChanges().newEntities</code> when the datasource has no\n<code>key</code>. The server gives the row its real key. <code>acceptChanges({ newKeys })</code> takes a <code>Map</code> from each\ntemporary key to the server&#39;s key, and puts the server&#39;s key in the row - in the key field, or as the\nrow&#39;s <code>__KEY</code>. Setting it is no edit, and a selected row stays selected.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Person {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n}\n\n// what the server answers: the id it gave each new row\ninterface SaveResult {\n    newIds: { temporaryId: number; id: number }[];\n}\n\nconst people = new Datasource&lt;Person&gt;({ key: 'id' });\npeople.setData([\n    { id: 1, name: 'Ann' },\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }\n]);\n\n// the user edits, deletes and adds rows\nconst [ann, bob] = people.getDataRows();\nann.name = 'Anna';\npeople.markForDeletion(bob);\npeople.addNewEmpty({ name: 'Cid' }, { scrollTo: false }); // a temporary id: -1, say\n\n// one transaction\nconst changes = people.getChanges();\n// newEntities:      [{ name: 'Cid', id: -1 }]\n// modifiedEntities: [{ id: 1, name: 'Anna' }]\n// deletedEntities:  [{ id: 2, name: 'Bob' }]\nconst response = await fetch('/api/people/save', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(changes) });\nif (response.ok) {\n    const result: SaveResult = await response.json();\n    people.acceptChanges({ newKeys: new Map(result.newIds.map((row) =&gt; [row.temporaryId, row.id])) });\n}\n\npeople.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; [row.id, row.name]); // [[1, 'Anna'], [7, 'Cid']] - Cid has the server's id\npeople.hasChanges(); // false</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>newKeys</code> compares the keys as text: <code>-1</code> and <code>&#39;-1&#39;</code> are the same key, so a map made from the keys\nof a JSON object works too - <code>new Map(Object.entries(json))</code>.</li>\n<li>A key in <code>newKeys</code> that is not the key of a new row throws, and so does a blank server key - before\nanything changes. A map the wrong way round (server key to temporary key) is caught that way. A\nnew row that is not in <code>newKeys</code> keeps its key.</li>\n<li>When only some rows were saved, pass them: <code>acceptChanges({ rows: savedRows, newKeys })</code>. The\nother rows keep their changes.</li>\n<li>Keep the grid readonly while the save runs - <code>gridInterface.updateConfig({ readonly: true })</code>, and\n<code>false</code> after - or an edit made in between is accepted without being saved.</li>\n<li>Validate first: <code>validate()</code> returns the errors, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>.</li>\n<li>When the save fails, do not call <code>acceptChanges()</code>: the changes stay, to be saved again. Show the\nserver&#39;s errors on the rows with <a href=\"#guide/editing/errors-from-a-server\"><code>setErrors()</code></a>.</li>\n<li>Without a <code>key</code>, the server&#39;s key becomes the row&#39;s <code>__KEY</code>: a later edit of the row is sent with\nit, as <code>__KEY</code> in <code>modifiedEntities</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>hasChanges()</code> is <code>true</code> while there is something to save, for an &quot;unsaved changes&quot; indicator. The\ngrid sends no event for an edit, so check it when it matters:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>window.addEventListener('beforeunload', (event) =&gt; {\n    if (datasource.hasChanges()) {\n        event.preventDefault();\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Setting the data again with the rows from the server works too: <code>setData(freshRows)</code> starts the state\nof every row over, and with a <code>key</code> the selection is kept. <code>acceptChanges()</code> keeps the rows, so the\ncurrent row, the scroll position and the expanded groups stay as they are.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"saving\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-errors-from-a-server\">Errors from a server</h2>\n<p><code>setErrors()</code> on the datasource&#39;s container puts an error text on rows, by key - what a server said\nabout a save it refused. It is a <code>Map</code> from the key as text to the message. Every row not in the map\ngets <code>null</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] }\n};\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    // the cells of the row too: .row-error { position: absolute; inset: 0; background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.2); }\n    cellClass: (row) =&gt; (row.__rowError ? { background: 'row-error' } : null)\n};\nconst grid = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });\n\ndatasource.getDataContainer().setErrors(new Map([['2', 'The name is taken']]));\n\nconst row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nconsole.log(row.__rowError); // the message, or null</code></pre>\n<p>The grid shows <code>__rowError</code> on the row number, with the row&#39;s validation errors: the class\n<code>simple-html-row-error</code>, and the message as the first line of its tooltip, in the next animation\nframe after <code>setErrors()</code>. To mark the cells of the row too, use the <code>cellClass</code> hook as above.</p>\n<p><strong>Server errors and validation errors are kept apart.</strong> A server error is the row&#39;s <code>__rowError</code>,\nset with <code>setErrors()</code>; a validation error is a field&#39;s, from the rules (<code>getErrors()</code>). <code>setErrors()</code>\ndoes not change the validation errors, and <code>validate()</code>, <code>getErrors()</code> and <code>hasErrors()</code> do not read\n<code>__rowError</code> - a row the server refused is not invalid by the rules, and the next save may well work.\n<code>acceptChanges()</code> clears both.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-required-fields-and-placeholders\">Required fields and placeholders</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A field with the <code>required</code> rule (<code>validate: [&#39;required&#39;]</code> in the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>) shows its empty cells with stripes, until they have a value - empty as\nthe rule sees it, so 0 too. Only in an editable grid, and only on editable cells. Restyle the\nstripes with the <code>simple-html-mandatory</code> class. It replaces the <code>mandatory</code> column option of v5.</li>\n<li><code>placeholderRow</code> is text shown in an empty cell. By default only on the current row, set\n<code>placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly: false</code> in the config to show it on every row.</li>\n<li><code>placeholderFilter</code> is text shown in the empty header filter.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst companies = new Datasource({ fields: { company: { validate: ['required'] } } });\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    placeholderRowCurrentEntityOnly: false,\n    columns: {\n        company: { placeholderRow: 'required' },\n        phone: { placeholderRow: '+47 ...', placeholderFilter: 'search phone' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['phone'], width: 120 }\n        ]\n    }\n};\ncreateGrid(host, { config, datasource: companies });</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"required-placeholders\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-edits-and-live-data\">Edits and live data</h2>\n<p>A value set from code shows in the grid on its own, in the next animation frame: the datasource sends\n<code>rows-changed</code> with the rows changed, and the grid renders the ones on screen - <code>refresh()</code> is not\nneeded after an edit from code, an undo or a revert. The cell the user is typing in is never rendered\nover. When rows from a server are merged with <code>upsert()</code>, an edit the user has not saved stays: a\nfield the server changed too is a conflict, resolved with <code>resolveConflict()</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"filtering","title":"Filtering","html":"<p>The datasource has one filter: a condition, or a group of conditions and groups. The user builds it\nin the grid - with the header filters, the menus and the filter editor - and you set it from code.\nEverything works on the same filter, <code>datasource.getFilter()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-header-filters\">Header filters</h2>\n<p>The row under the header labels has a filter cell per column. The user types a value and presses\nEnter, or leaves the cell, and the grid filters on it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field type</th>\n<th>the cell</th>\n<th>the operator it starts with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>an input</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code>: the whole text, ignoring case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>number</td>\n<td>an input</td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>date, datetime</td>\n<td>an input, and a date picker on click</td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>boolean</td>\n<td>a checkbox: no filter, then checked (<code>true</code>), then unchecked (<code>false</code>), then no filter again</td>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A text filter matches the whole text</strong> (<code>EQUAL</code>, ignoring case): <code>ann</code> finds <code>Ann</code> and <code>ANN</code>, but\nnot <code>Anna</code>. <code>*</code> and <code>%</code> are wildcards: <code>ann*</code> starts with ann, <code>*ann*</code> has ann in it.</li>\n<li>A number or date is typed as the field&#39;s formatter shows it (<code>12,5</code> works too, the date in the\nformat of the placeholder) and parsed with the formatter.</li>\n<li>The <code>operator</code> option of a column sets the operator its header filter starts with, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/a-contains-search\">below</a>.</li>\n<li>Emptying the cell removes the column&#39;s condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-contains-search\">A contains search</h3>\n<p>For a search anywhere in the text, give the column <code>operator: &#39;CONTAINS&#39;</code> in <code>columns</code>. Each column\nhas its own, the text columns without one keep <code>EQUAL</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { operator: 'CONTAINS' }, // `ann` finds Ann, Anna and Joanna\n        city: { operator: 'BEGIN_WITH' } // `osl` finds Oslo\n        // company: EQUAL, the default\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['name'], width: 150 },\n            { fields: ['city'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 150 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p>The user can also pick the operator of a column in the header filter&#39;s menu (Set Operator, below),\nand type <code>*ann*</code> in any text header filter.</p>\n<p>A header filter only replaces the conditions on its own column, at the top of the filter. The rest\nof the filter is kept, and the column&#39;s condition is ANDed with it.</p>\n<p>What each header cell shows is part of the <a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">state</a>:\n<code>getState().headerFilters</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"filtering\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-header-filter-menu\">The header filter menu</h3>\n<p>Right click a header filter for its menu:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clear Filter</strong> removes the conditions on this column, <strong>Clear All Filters</strong> the whole filter</li>\n<li><strong>Set &quot;Is Blank&quot;</strong> / <strong>Set &quot;Is Not Blank&quot;</strong> replace the column&#39;s conditions with <code>IS_BLANK</code> /\n<code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></li>\n<li><strong>Advanced Filter</strong> opens the <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-filter-editor\">filter editor</a></li>\n<li><strong>Set Operator</strong> picks the operator the column&#39;s header filter uses: Equal, Not Equal and\nContains for text, Greater than or equal, Less than or equal and Equal for numbers and dates. The\nfilter runs again with it, and the grid sends a <code>filter-operator-change</code> event. The operator\npicked is state (<code>headerFilters[field].operator</code>).</li>\n<li>the <strong>value list</strong>, for text fields</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"filter-operator-header\"></div>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-value-list\">The value list</h3>\n<p>The value list is the part of a text column&#39;s filter menu with a checkbox per value, as in a\nspreadsheet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It lists the values of the rows the <em>other</em> columns&#39; filters let through, text in upper case,\nwith the number of rows of each. &quot;Blank&quot; is every empty value: <code>null</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, and also <code>0</code> and <code>false</code>.</li>\n<li>It lists at most 100 values, and then says so. The counts are only shown when every value is\nlisted.</li>\n<li><strong>Filter Available</strong>, when other columns filter rows: uncheck it to list the values of every row.</li>\n<li>The search box narrows the list. With search text, &quot;Run Search&quot; also adds a <code>CONTAINS</code> condition\nfor it.</li>\n<li><strong>Run Search</strong> filters the column on the checked values: an <code>IN</code> condition with them, or a\n<code>NOT_IN</code> with the unchecked ones when there are fewer of those.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-filters-from-the-row-menu\">Filters from the row menu</h3>\n<p>Right click a cell for the row menu. Its &quot;Filter:&quot; part filters on the value of that cell:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Replace filter (all)</strong> replaces the whole filter with <code>field = value</code>, and empties the header\nfilters</li>\n<li><strong>Add to filter (and)</strong> ANDs <code>field = value</code> with the filter there is</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An empty cell gives <code>IS_BLANK</code> instead of <code>= value</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-editor\">The filter editor</h2>\n<p>The filter editor is a dialog where the user builds any filter: conditions and AND/OR groups,\nnested. It opens from the header filter menu (Advanced Filter), from a click on the filter in the\nfooter, and from code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>document.getElementById('advanced-filter')!.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; gridInterface.openFilterEditor());</code></pre>\n<p>It starts with the filter there is. A condition picks a field (every field the grid knows), an\noperator (all of them, see <a href=\"#guide/filtering/operators\">below</a>) and a value, or another field to compare with. A\nclick on AND / OR in a group switches it. &quot;Filter &amp; Close&quot; and &quot;Filter Only&quot; apply it, &quot;Close&quot;\nleaves the filter as it was. A condition with no field or operator picked is left out when it is\napplied. Applying it empties the header filter cells.</p>\n<p>It is a modal <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, over everything on the page: the grid behind it can not be used while it is\nopen. The focus moves to the dialog when it opens, and goes back to where it was when it closes. Esc\ncloses it - or the field, operator or date picker open in it, then Esc again closes the editor. Drag\nit by its title bar, resize it from any edge or corner: with the mouse, a finger or a pen. It keeps\nits place and size while it is open, and opens in the middle of the window again. The dialog is\n<code>&lt;dialog class=&quot;simple-html-grid filter-editor-content&quot;&gt;</code>, the grey layer behind it its <code>::backdrop</code>.</p>\n<p>Its texts - the buttons, the labels, the names of the operators, the tooltips - are <code>filterEditor.*</code>,\nthose of the header filter menu and its value list <code>filter.*</code>: see <a href=\"#guide/texts/filter-editor\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-footer\">The footer</h2>\n<p>The footer shows the number of rows shown (<code>datasource.length()</code>, group rows included) and the\nnumber of rows there are (<code>12/500</code>), and the\nfilter as a row of chips: field, operator, value, with the groups in brackets. &quot;✕ Clear&quot; removes the\nwhole filter, a click on the chips opens the filter editor. The labels are the column labels, the\nrest are texts (<code>footer.*</code>, the operators <code>footer.operator.*</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/texts/footer\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<p>While only the changed rows are shown (below), a chip <em>Changed rows only</em> comes first, ANDed with the\nfilter&#39;s chips. &quot;✕ Clear&quot; turns it off too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-changed-rows-only\">Changed rows only</h2>\n<p><code>datasource.filterChangedOnly(true)</code> shows only the new, edited and deleted rows of the rows the\nfilter lets through - the row menu&#39;s <em>View: Changed rows only</em> in the grid. It is not a condition of\nthe filter but on top of it (AND): <code>getFilter()</code>, the filter editor and the state do not have it, and\nsetting the filter leaves it on. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/changed-rows-only\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.filterChangedOnly(true);\ndatasource.isChangedOnly(); // true\ndatasource.filterChangedOnly(false);</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-filters-from-code\">Filters from code</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, or, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.filter(where('balance', 'GREATER_THAN', 2500)); // set a filter and run it\n\n// country = 'Norway' AND (age &lt; 30 OR age &gt; 60)\ndatasource.filter(\n    and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'), or(where('age', 'LESS_THAN', 30), where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 60)))\n);\n\ndatasource.filter(); // run the filter that is set again, e.g. after rows were edited\ndatasource.filter(null); // no filter: every row\n\ndatasource.setFilter(where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 18)); // set it without running it\ndatasource.getFilter(); // the filter that is set, null when there is none</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>filter(expression)</code> replaces the filter, also the header filters&#39; conditions. The header cells\nkeep showing their text: to change both, use <code>gridInterface.setState({ filter, headerFilters })</code>,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/state/header-filters-in-the-state\">State</a>.</li>\n<li><code>setData()</code> runs the filter on the new rows. <code>{ refilter: false }</code> shows them all, the filter stays\nset, see <a href=\"#guide/datasource/data-in-setdata\">Datasource</a>.</li>\n<li>The grid shows the result by itself, it follows the datasource&#39;s events.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-format\">The filter format</h2>\n<p>A filter is a <code>FilterExpression</code>: a condition or a group. It is plain data, so it can be stored and\ncompared like any object, and stored as JSON.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { FilterCondition, FilterExpression, FilterGroup } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst condition: FilterCondition = { field: 'age', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18 };\n\nconst group: FilterGroup = {\n    group: 'OR', // 'AND': every child must match. 'OR': one is enough\n    children: [\n        { field: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Norway' },\n        { field: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Sweden' }\n    ]\n};\n\nconst filter: FilterExpression = { group: 'AND', children: [condition, group] };\ndatasource.filter(filter);</code></pre>\n<p><code>where(field, operator, value?)</code>, <code>and(...children)</code> and <code>or(...children)</code> build the same objects.\n<code>where(&#39;email&#39;, &#39;IS_BLANK&#39;)</code> leaves <code>value</code> out.</p>\n<p>A condition has no type of its own. It is compared as the type of its field in the datasource&#39;s\n<code>fields</code> (<code>text</code> when the field is not listed). A number value always compares as a number, and a\n<code>Date</code> value as a date.</p>\n<p><strong>Compare with another field</strong> of the same row with <code>valueField</code>. It wins over <code>value</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// rows changed after they were approved\ndatasource.filter({ field: 'changed', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', valueField: 'approved' });</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Ignored</strong>: a condition without <code>field</code> or <code>operator</code>, a condition on a date field without a value,\nand an empty group filter nothing out. An empty group is kept as it is (<code>filter(and())</code> keeps every\nrow); use <code>filter(null)</code> for no filter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-operators\">Operators</h2>\n<p>The field&#39;s type decides how a condition compares the row&#39;s value with its value.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>operator</th>\n<th>text</th>\n<th>number</th>\n<th>date, datetime</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>the whole text, ignoring case. <code>*</code> and <code>%</code> are wildcards</td>\n<td>the same number</td>\n<td>the same day (<code>date</code>) or second (<code>datetime</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>not <code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>not <code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>not <code>EQUAL</code>. A blank row date matches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td>before, compared as lower case text</td>\n<td>smaller</td>\n<td>earlier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>LESS_THAN</code> or <code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>smaller or the same</td>\n<td>earlier or the same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td>after, compared as lower case text</td>\n<td>larger</td>\n<td>later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>GREATER_THAN</code> or <code>EQUAL</code></td>\n<td>larger or the same</td>\n<td>later or the same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td>has the value in it, ignoring case</td>\n<td>as <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>as <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td>does not have the value in it</td>\n<td>as <code>LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td>as <code>LESS_THAN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td>starts with the value</td>\n<td>as <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>as <code>GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>END_WITH</code></td>\n<td>ends with the value</td>\n<td>as <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td>as <code>LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IN</code></td>\n<td>one of a list, ignoring case</td>\n<td>one of a list, as the formatter shows the number</td>\n<td>one of a list, as the formatter shows the date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IN</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IN</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or <code>0</code></td>\n<td><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or <code>0</code></td>\n<td><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> or an invalid date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td>not <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>boolean</strong> field compares every operator as <code>EQUAL</code>, apart from <code>IN</code>, <code>NOT_IN</code>, <code>IS_BLANK</code> and\n<code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code>. The value must be <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li>\n<li><code>IN</code> / <code>NOT_IN</code> take a list as <code>value</code>: <code>where(&#39;country&#39;, &#39;IN&#39;, [&#39;Norway&#39;, &#39;Sweden&#39;])</code>. Text with\none value per line works too. <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code> in the list matches an empty value.</li>\n<li>A <strong>blank row date</strong> is not before, after or on any date: of the comparisons it only matches\n<code>NOT_EQUAL_TO</code>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>blank number</strong> compares as 0: <code>where(&#39;age&#39;, &#39;LESS_THAN&#39;, 5)</code> also matches the rows without an\nage.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-0-is-blank\">0 is blank</h3>\n<p>A row value of <code>0</code> counts as blank, on purpose: in ERP style data 0 and empty mean the same.\n<code>IS_BLANK</code> matches 0 and <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code> leaves it out, in every field that holds the number 0, and\nthe value list puts 0 under &quot;Blank&quot;. The text <code>&#39;0&#39;</code> is not blank. <code>IN</code> on a number field compares\nthe formatted text, so there 0 is <code>&#39;0&#39;</code>, not <code>&#39;NULL&#39;</code>. The <code>required</code> validation rule takes 0 as\nmissing too, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/the-built-in-rules\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-filter-as-text\">The filter as text</h2>\n<p><code>getFilterString()</code> gives the filter as one line of text, <code>getFilterTree()</code> as a tree for your own\nrendering. Both leave out what the filter ignores, and use the fields&#39; labels unless you pass a\nfunction for them:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { and, Datasource, or, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({ fields: { age: { type: 'number', label: 'Age' } } });\npeople.filter(and(where('country', 'EQUAL', 'Norway'), or(where('age', 'LESS_THAN', 20), where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 60))));\n\npeople.getFilterString();\n// ([COUNTRY] &lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt; 'NORWAY' AND ([AGE] &lt;&lt;LESS_THAN&gt;&gt; '20' OR [AGE] &lt;&lt;GREATER_THAN&gt;&gt; '60'))\n\npeople.getFilterString((field) =&gt; (field === 'country' ? 'Land' : field));\n// ([LAND] &lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt; 'NORWAY' AND ([AGE] &lt;&lt;LESS_THAN&gt;&gt; '20' OR [AGE] &lt;&lt;GREATER_THAN&gt;&gt; '60'))\n\npeople.getFilterTree();\n// { kind: 'group', logicalOperator: 'AND', children: [\n//     { kind: 'condition', label: 'country', operator: 'EQUAL', value: 'Norway', hasValue: true },\n//     { kind: 'group', logicalOperator: 'OR', children: [ ...the two age conditions ] }\n// ] }</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getFilterString()</code> is <code>&#39;&#39;</code> and <code>getFilterTree()</code> is <code>null</code> when no filter is set, or nothing of\nit is left.</li>\n<li>The values are formatted with the field&#39;s formatter. The tree&#39;s root is always a group, also for\na single condition.</li>\n<li>The grid&#39;s footer uses <code>getFilterTree()</code> with the column labels.</li>\n<li>The operators and AND / OR of <code>getFilterString()</code> are texts, <code>filterString.*</code>: change them with\n<code>setTexts()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/getfilterstring\">Texts</a>. <code>getFilterTree()</code> has the operators as they\nare (<code>&#39;EQUAL&#39;</code>), for your own rendering.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"sorting-grouping","title":"Sorting and grouping","html":"<p>Sorting and grouping are done by the datasource. The user does them in the grid&#39;s header, you do\nthem from code, and the grid shows the result.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-sorting\">Sorting</h2>\n<p>The user clicks (or taps) a header label to sort on that column, ascending. A second click sorts it\ndescending. Shift+click adds the column to the sort there is. A press that moves the label is a drag,\nnot a click, see <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/moving-and-resizing-columns\">Moving and resizing columns</a>. The header menu&#39;s &quot;Remove sorting&quot;\nremoves the sort, apart from the grouped fields.</p>\n<p>From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.sort({ attribute: 'company', ascending: true }); // replaces the sort there was\ndatasource.sort({ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }, { add: true }); // company, then age\n\ndatasource.sort([\n    { attribute: 'country', ascending: true },\n    { attribute: 'company', ascending: false }\n]);\n\ndatasource.sort(); // sort again with the sort there is, e.g. after edits\ndatasource.getLastSorting(); // [{ attribute: 'country', ascending: true }, { attribute: 'company', ascending: false }]</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>With <code>{ add: true }</code>, a field that is in the sort already gets the new direction and keeps its\nplace. A list (<code>sort([a, b])</code>) always replaces the sort.</li>\n<li><code>setOrderBy(args, { add })</code> sets the sort without sorting, <code>sort()</code> sorts.</li>\n<li><code>resetSort()</code> clears the sort, the rows keep their order. <code>filter()</code> after it gives them back the\norder they were set in - sorted on the grouped fields when grouped.</li>\n<li>The sort stays when the data changes: <code>setData()</code> sorts the new rows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How values compare:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Empty values (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid date) come first when ascending, last when\ndescending. <code>0</code> and <code>false</code> are values, not empty.</li>\n<li>Text is compared with <code>localeCompare</code>. <code>datasource.setLocaleCompare(&#39;nb&#39;)</code> sorts it with the\nrules of a language (an <code>Intl.Collator</code>).</li>\n<li>A <code>date</code> field sorts on the day, the time is ignored. A <code>datetime</code> field sorts on the full time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The sort is part of the grid&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>: <code>getState().sorting</code>. To start sorted, put it\nin the config&#39;s <code>initialState</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-grouping\">Grouping</h2>\n<p>The user groups by dragging a header label to the panel above the header. A group shows as a chip\nin the panel: its icon removes it, and the chips can be dragged to change the order. A group row\nshows the group&#39;s name and its number of rows, and its <code>+</code> / <code>-</code> icon expands or collapses it. The\nheader menu has &quot;Expand all&quot;, &quot;Collapse all&quot; and &quot;Clear all&quot;.</p>\n<p>From code:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.group([{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }]);\ndatasource.group([{ attribute: 'company', title: 'Company' }], { add: true }); // country, then company\n\ndatasource.expandGroup(); // every group\ndatasource.collapseGroup(); // every group\ndatasource.expandGroup('Norway'); // one group, by its id\n\ndatasource.group([]); // no grouping. removeGroup() does the same</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>attribute</code> is the field, <code>title</code> the text in front of the value in the group row:\n<code>Country: Norway</code>. Its first letter is shown in upper case.</li>\n<li><code>group()</code> sorts the rows on the grouped fields, ascending, and replaces the sort there was. A\n<code>sort()</code> after it sorts within the groups, the grouped fields stay first.</li>\n<li><strong>Groups start collapsed</strong>, only the ids given to <code>setExpanded()</code> start expanded. <code>getExpanded()</code>\nlists the expanded ids.</li>\n<li>A row is grouped on its value as the field&#39;s formatter shows it: a <code>date</code> field groups by day, a\nnumber with its decimals as the formatter shows them. The rows with an empty value are one group\nat the top, its id is <code>&#39; blank&#39;</code>.</li>\n<li>Use <code>group()</code> to group. <code>setGrouping()</code> only stores the grouping: the next call that builds the\ndisplayed rows (<code>group()</code>, <code>sort()</code>, <code>filter()</code>, <code>setData()</code> ...) groups them.</li>\n<li><strong>The rows are always grouped sorted on the grouped fields</strong>, whatever led there: <code>group()</code>,\n<code>sort()</code>, <code>filter()</code> after <code>setGrouping()</code>, <code>setOrderBy()</code> or <code>resetSort()</code>. The grouped fields come\nfirst in the sort, each with the direction the sort gives it (ascending when it has none), the other\nfields of the sort after them. A group is built from each run of equal values, so the rows of a\ngroup have to be next to each other.</li>\n<li><code>removeGroup(group)</code> removes one level, found by its field: the object from <code>getGrouping()</code> or a\ncopy, e.g. from a saved state. A field that is not grouped on changes nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Sums in the group rows</strong>: a column with an <code>aggregate</code> (<code>&#39;sum&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;avg&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;min&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;max&#39;</code> or\n<code>&#39;count&#39;</code>) shows it in its place in every group row, over the group&#39;s rows - collapsed sub groups\nincluded. The name stays at the left. See <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The grouping and the expanded groups are part of the grid&#39;s <a href=\"#guide/state\">state</a>:\n<code>getState().grouping</code> and <code>expandedGroups</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"sorting-grouping\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-rows\">Group rows</h2>\n<p>When the datasource is grouped, <code>getRows()</code> has a group row in front of each group, and leaves out\nthe rows of a collapsed group. A group row is a <code>GroupRow</code>, made by the datasource, not one of your\nobjects. <code>isGroupRow(row)</code> tells it from a data row, and narrows the type:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.group([{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }]);\ndatasource.expandGroup();\n\nfor (const row of datasource.getRows()) {\n    if (isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log(row.__groupName, row.__groupTotal); // 'Country: Norway', 2\n    } else {\n        console.log('  ', row.name);\n    }\n}\n\n// the data rows only, the rows of collapsed groups included\nconst names = datasource.getDataRows().map((row) =&gt; row.name);</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>__group</code></td>\n<td>always <code>true</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupID</code></td>\n<td>the id for <code>expandGroup(id)</code> / <code>collapseGroup(id)</code>: the value as formatted, e.g. <code>&#39;Norway&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupName</code></td>\n<td>the text the grid shows: <code>&#39;Country: Norway&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupLvl</code></td>\n<td>the level, 0 for the first grouped field. The grid indents by it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupTotal</code></td>\n<td>the number of data rows in the group, its sub groups included. More sums of a group: <code>datasource.aggregate(field, fn, groupRow)</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates/from-code\">Aggregates</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupChildren</code></td>\n<td>its rows: data rows, or the group rows of the next level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>__groupExpanded</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> when the group is expanded</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Read the fields, do not change them. A group row can not be selected, edited or marked for\ndeletion: <code>getSelectedRows()</code>, <code>getDataRows()</code> and <code>getAllData()</code> never have one. <code>getRow(n)</code> and\n<code>currentEntity</code> can, check them with <code>isGroupRow()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-groups-in-groups\">Groups in groups</h2>\n<p>Each field given to <code>group()</code> is one level. The id of a sub group starts with the ids of the groups\nabove it, joined by <code>-</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.group([\n    { attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' },\n    { attribute: 'company', title: 'Company' }\n]);\n\ndatasource.expandGroup('Norway'); // the first level\ndatasource.expandGroup('Norway-ACME'); // company ACME in Norway, and Norway if it was collapsed\n\nconst ids = datasource\n    .getRows()\n    .filter(isGroupRow)\n    .map((row) =&gt; `${'  '.repeat(row.__groupLvl)}${row.__groupID}`);</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Expanding a sub group expands the groups above it too, so it is shown:\n<code>expandGroup(&#39;Norway-ACME&#39;)</code> while <code>Norway</code> is collapsed expands both.</li>\n<li>A sub group stays expanded when the group above it is collapsed and expanded again.</li>\n<li><code>expandGroup()</code> and <code>collapseGroup()</code> without an id work on every level.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"formatting","title":"Formatting","html":"<p>A formatter turns the values of a field into text, and text back into values. Every field has one:\nits own, or the datasource&#39;s default for the field&#39;s type.</p>\n<p>The datasource&#39;s options name a formatter by its id: <code>formatter: &#39;numberComma&#39;</code>. The built-in\nformatters are registered under ids from the start, your own you register with\n<code>registerFormatter()</code>. So the options are plain JSON and can be stored, the formatters stay code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-formatter-does\">What a formatter does</h2>\n<p>A formatter is a plain object with two methods and two optional parts:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { Formatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst percent: Formatter = {\n    // value -&gt; text: a cell, the edit input, the header filter, a group row, the footer, getFilterString()\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? `${Math.round(value * 100)} %` : ''),\n    // text the user typed or pasted -&gt; the value stored in the row\n    parse: (text) =&gt; (text.trim() === '' ? null : Number(text.replace('%', '').trim()) / 100),\n    // optional: text typed in a filter -&gt; the value filtered on. Left out, parse is used\n    parseFilter: (text) =&gt; Number(text.replace('%', '').trim()) / 100,\n    // optional: shown in the empty header filter\n    placeholder: '0 %'\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>format</code> gets whatever the row holds and returns text: <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for an empty value.</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> and <code>parseFilter</code> always get text from the grid.</li>\n<li>A boolean field is shown as a checkbox from the raw value. Its formatter is used where it is\nshown as text: the filter string, group rows, and text pasted into the column.</li>\n<li>Do not use <code>this</code> in the methods: the datasource can call them on their own.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-defaults-per-type\">The defaults per type</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>default formatter</th>\n<th>shows</th>\n<th>parses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>text</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code>, <code>TextFormatter</code></td>\n<td>the text, <code>0</code> and <code>false</code> as <code>0</code> and <code>false</code></td>\n<td>the text as it is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>number</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code>, <code>NumberFormatterDot</code></td>\n<td><code>1234.5</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;12.5&#39;</code> and <code>&#39;12,5&#39;</code> as 12.5. Text that is no number, and <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, as 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>, <code>BooleanFormatter</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;true&#39;</code> (any case) as true, other text as false, <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>date</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;dateYYYYMMDD&#39;</code>, <code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDD</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-20&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeYYYYMMDD&#39;</code>, <code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;2026-11-20T13:45:12&#39;</code> as a <code>Date</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Every formatter shows <code>null</code> and <code>undefined</code> as <code>&#39;&#39;</code>.</p>\n<p>An emptied number cell stores <code>0</code>, not <code>null</code>: the number formatters parse <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as 0. &quot;Clear&quot; in the\nrow menu stores <code>null</code>. Both count as blank in a filter, see\n<a href=\"#guide/filtering/0-is-blank\">0 is blank</a>. For <code>null</code> from an emptied cell, give the field a formatter\nthat parses <code>&#39;&#39;</code> as <code>null</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type Formatter, NumberFormatterDot, registerFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst emptyIsNull: Formatter = {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    parse: (text) =&gt; (text.trim() === '' ? null : NumberFormatterDot.parse(text))\n};\nregisterFormatter('numberEmptyIsNull', emptyIsNull);\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({ fields: { amount: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberEmptyIsNull' } } });</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-built-in-formatters\">The built-in formatters</h2>\n<p>Each is registered under an id, the options use the id. The object is exported too: call it\nyourself (<code>NumberFormatterComma.format(12.5)</code>), or build a variant of it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>formatter</th>\n<th>shows</th>\n<th>placeholder</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;text&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>TextFormatter</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>BooleanFormatter</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>NumberFormatterDot</code></td>\n<td><code>1234.5</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;numberComma&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>NumberFormatterComma</code></td>\n<td><code>1234,5</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;dateYYYYMMDD&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDD</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code></td>\n<td><code>YYYY-MM-DD</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;dateDDMMYYYY&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterDDMMYYYY</code></td>\n<td><code>20.11.2026</code></td>\n<td><code>DD.MM.YYYY</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeYYYYMMDD&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterYYYYMMDDTHHMMSS</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20T13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;datetimeDDMMYYYY&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>DateFormatterDDMMYYYYTHHMMSS</code></td>\n<td><code>20.11.2026T13:45:12</code></td>\n<td><code>DD.MM.YYYYTHH:MM:SS</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The date formatters fill in what the typed text leaves out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a missing month is the current month, a missing day (or year, for the <code>DD.MM.YYYY</code> ones) the\ncurrent one</li>\n<li><code>parse</code> gives the date the current time of day, for the parts of the time the text has not.\n<code>parseFilter</code> gives them 0, so a filter starts at the start of the day.</li>\n<li><code>&#39;&#39;</code> is <code>null</code>, text that is not a date gives <code>&#39;&#39;</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The number formatters read both <code>12.5</code> and <code>12,5</code>, whichever separator they show.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-different-default-for-a-type\">A different default for a type</h2>\n<p><code>formatters</code> in the datasource&#39;s options sets the formatter of every field of a type that has none\nof its own, by id:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: 'number', amount: 'number', born: 'date' },\n    formatters: { number: 'numberComma', date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n});</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"formatters\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-formatter-for-one-field\">A formatter for one field</h2>\n<p>Give the field a <code>formatter</code>, the id of a registered formatter:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'numberComma' },\n        born: { type: 'date', formatter: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n    }\n});\n\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12.5); // '12,5'</code></pre>\n<p><code>datasource.getFormatter(field)</code> returns the formatter of a field - the object, not the id -\n<code>getField(field).formatter</code> too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-formatter-of-your-own\">A formatter of your own</h2>\n<p>Register it under an id with <code>registerFormatter(id, formatter)</code>, and name the id in the options. A\nbuilt-in formatter is a plain object, so a variant is a spread away:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type Formatter, NumberFormatterDot, registerFormatter, TextFormatter } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// shown in upper case, stored as typed\nconst upperCase: Formatter = {\n    ...TextFormatter,\n    format: (value) =&gt; TextFormatter.format(value).toUpperCase()\n};\n\n// two decimals and a space between the thousands: 12345.6 -&gt; '12 345.60'\nconst money: Formatter = {\n    ...NumberFormatterDot,\n    format: (value) =&gt; (typeof value === 'number' ? value.toFixed(2).replace(/\\B(?=(\\d{3})+(?!\\d))/g, ' ') : ''),\n    parse: (text) =&gt; NumberFormatterDot.parse(text.replaceAll(' ', ''))\n};\n\n// when the page loads, before a datasource names them\nregisterFormatter('upperCase', upperCase);\nregisterFormatter('money', money);\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        country: { formatter: 'upperCase' }, // a text field: no type needed\n        price: { type: 'number', formatter: 'money' }\n    },\n    formatters: { date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' } // an id works for the default of a type too\n});\n\ndatasource.getFormatter('price').format(12345.6); // '12 345.60'</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Register before the datasource is made.</strong> The datasource looks its ids up once, when it is\nmade, and keeps the formatters it found. An id that is not registered throws there, and the\nmessage lists the ids that are.</li>\n<li><strong>Registering an id again replaces the formatter</strong>, for the datasources made after; one made\nbefore keeps the formatter it has. A built-in id can be replaced too, and replacing a default\n(<code>&#39;numberDot&#39;</code> ...) changes every field of that type without a formatter of its own. Use an id of\nyour own unless that is what you want.</li>\n<li>There is one registry for the page, every datasource uses it.</li>\n<li>The id is any text. The options stay plain JSON, so <code>fields</code> and <code>formatters</code> can be stored - in a\nbackend, say - and the formatters registered in code. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/saving-the-config\">Saving the config</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"custom-formatter\"></div>\n<p>A formatter that reads a setting of your own (the number of decimals, say) can change what it\nshows: call <code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code> after the setting changed, so the cells and the header\nfilters show it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-type-from-another-field\">The type from another field</h2>\n<p>For property/value style data - a column of values of different types - a text column can take the\ntype of each cell from another field of its row, with <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst datasource = new Datasource(); // value and valueType are text fields: not listed\ndatasource.setData([\n    { property: 'Name', valueType: 'text', value: 'Ann' },\n    { property: 'Age', valueType: 'number', value: 36 },\n    { property: 'Born', valueType: 'date', value: new Date(1990, 4, 17) },\n    { property: 'Active', valueType: 'boolean', value: true }\n]);\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    readonly: false,\n    columns: {\n        value: { dynamicCellTypeColumn: 'valueType' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['property'], width: 120 },\n            { fields: ['valueType'], width: 100 },\n            { fields: ['value'], width: 150 }\n        ]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The field must be a <code>text</code> field, which is what a field not listed in <code>fields</code> is.</li>\n<li>The other field holds <code>&#39;text&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;number&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;boolean&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;date&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;datetime&#39;</code>. Anything else\nleaves the cell text.</li>\n<li>Each cell is shown and edited as its type, with the datasource&#39;s default formatter for it:\n<code>datasource.getFormatter(field, type)</code>. A date cell gets the date picker, a boolean a checkbox.</li>\n<li>Sorting, grouping and filtering still treat the field as text.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"dynamic-cell-types\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-showing-0-or-an-empty-cell-numberoverride\">Showing 0 or an empty cell: <code>numberOverride</code></h2>\n<p>The column option <code>numberOverride</code> changes what a number cell shows, not the value:\n<code>&#39;ZERO_TO_BLANK&#39;</code> shows 0 as an empty cell, <code>&#39;BLANK_TO_ZERO&#39;</code> an empty cell as <code>0</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config/column-options\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"events","title":"Events","html":"<p>The datasource and the grid interface both send events. The datasource&#39;s are about the rows: they\nchanged, were sorted, filtered or grouped, the selection or the current row changed. The grid&#39;s are\nabout the cells: clicks, focus, the ranges of cells selected, copy and paste from the row menu.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-subscribing-on\">Subscribing: <code>on()</code></h2>\n<p><code>on(name, handler)</code> subscribes to one event. The handler gets the event&#39;s payload, typed from the\nevent name. <code>on()</code> returns a function that unsubscribes:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const offFiltered = datasource.on('collection-filtered', (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('filtered by', data.info, '-', datasource.getDataRows().length, 'rows left');\n});\n\nconst offClick = gridInterface.on('cell-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log(data.attribute, 'clicked in row', data.row, data.rowData);\n});\n\n// later\noffFiltered();\noffClick();</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>The handler stays subscribed until you call the function <code>on()</code> returned. What it returns is\nignored.</li>\n<li>To listen once, unsubscribe in the handler:\n<code>const off = datasource.on(&#39;select&#39;, () =&gt; { off(); ... })</code>.</li>\n<li>The same function subscribed twice to the same event is called once.</li>\n<li>An error thrown by a handler is not caught: it stops the handlers after it for that event.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.destroy()</code> unsubscribes every handler of the grid. The handlers on the datasource\nstay, the datasource lives on.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To type a handler you write on its own, use <code>EventHandler</code> with the event map:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { DatasourceEvents, EventHandler, GridEvents } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst onSelect: EventHandler&lt;DatasourceEvents['select']&gt; = (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('row', data.row, 'selected by', data.info);\n};\nconst onPaste: EventHandler&lt;GridEvents['paste']&gt; = (data) =&gt; {\n    console.log('pasted into', data.attribute, data.entity);\n};\n\ndatasource.on('select', onSelect);\ngridInterface.on('paste', onPaste);</code></pre>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"events\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-datasource-events\">Datasource events</h2>\n<p><code>DatasourceEvents</code> lists them. An event with nothing to tell sends an empty object.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>collection-changed</code></td>\n<td>rows were added, replaced or removed</td>\n<td><code>{ added?, removed?, info?, data? }</code>, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-filtered</code></td>\n<td>the displayed rows were rebuilt</td>\n<td><code>{ info }</code>, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-sorted</code></td>\n<td><code>sort()</code> ran</td>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-grouped</code></td>\n<td>the grouping changed: <code>group()</code>, <code>removeGroup()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ info: &#39;group&#39;, groupings }</code> or <code>{ info: &#39;removeGroup&#39; }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-expand</code></td>\n<td><code>expandGroup()</code> expanded one or every group</td>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-collapse</code></td>\n<td><code>collapseGroup()</code> collapsed one or every group</td>\n<td><code>{}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>current-entity</code></td>\n<td><code>currentEntity</code> was set: a row was clicked or selected, or <code>setRowAsCurrentEntity()</code>. Also when <code>setData()</code> set it to <code>null</code>, the current row no longer in the data, and when a method that removes rows removed it (<code>removeData()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code>, <code>resetData()</code> ...) - after their own event</td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>datasource.currentEntity</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>selection-change</code></td>\n<td>the selected rows changed. <code>deselectAll()</code> sends it only with <code>{ notify: true }</code>, <code>selectAll()</code> only in the <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code> selection mode. Also when a method that removes rows removed a selected one, after its own event</td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>datasource.getSelectedRows()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>select</code></td>\n<td>a row was selected from code, the grid scrolls to it</td>\n<td><code>{ info, row }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validate</code></td>\n<td><code>validate()</code> ran, the grid shows the errors. An edit is validated without an event</td>\n<td><code>{ errors }</code>, what <code>validate()</code> returned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>history-changed</code></td>\n<td>the undo history changed: read <code>canUndo()</code> and <code>canRedo()</code>. Sent by every datasource on the same <code>DataContainer</code>, the history is the container&#39;s</td>\n<td><code>{ info }</code>, see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rows-changed</code></td>\n<td>values of rows changed - set from code, by the user, by undo/redo, a revert, <code>upsert()</code> - or a row&#39;s deletion mark, server error or conflict. Once the code that changed them is done (a microtask), one event for all of them. The grid renders the rows on screen, see <a href=\"#guide/live-data/values-show-on-their-own\">Live data</a>. Sent by every datasource on the same <code>DataContainer</code></td>\n<td><code>{ rows, fields }</code>: the rows, and <code>fields.get(row)</code> the fields set on each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conflicts-changed</code></td>\n<td>the conflicts of <code>upsert()</code> changed: one came or went. Once per run of code, after <code>rows-changed</code>. Sent by every datasource on the same <code>DataContainer</code></td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>datasource.getConflicts()</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>collection-changed</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>setData(rows)</code></td>\n<td><code>{ added: false }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>setData(rows, { add: true })</code>, <code>addNewEmpty()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ added: true }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>removeData(rows)</code></td>\n<td><code>{ removed: true, info: &#39;removeData&#39;, data: rows }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>removeAllData()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ removed: true, info: &#39;removeData&#39;, data: null }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code></td>\n<td><code>{ removed: true, info: &#39;clearMarkedForDeletion&#39; }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>upsert(rows)</code></td>\n<td><code>{ info: &#39;upsert&#39;, added }</code>, and <code>removed: true</code> when it removed rows. Only when it added or removed rows or the displayed rows changed (filter, sort order, groups): an upsert that only changed values sends <code>rows-changed</code> only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>collection-filtered</code>, by <code>info</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>info</code></th>\n<th>sent by</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;filter&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>filter()</code>, <code>reloadDatasource()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;resetData&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>resetData()</code>, <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;markForDeletion&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>markForDeletion()</code>, <code>markAllForDeletion()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;unmarkForDeletion&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>, <code>unmarkAllForDeletion()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;acceptChanges&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>acceptChanges()</code>. It sends no <code>collection-changed</code>, also when it removed the rows marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;__internalUpdate, forced&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>setData(rows, { refilter: false })</code> and <code>addNewEmpty()</code> while there is no filter, sort or grouping, before their <code>collection-changed</code>. A <code>setData()</code> that runs the filter (the default) sends only <code>collection-changed</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>history-changed</code>, by <code>info</code> - see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>info</code></th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;edit&#39;</code></td>\n<td>a step was recorded: an edit, what was typed in a cell, a paste, a clear, a (un)mark for deletion, a revert. Sent once the code that made it is done (in a microtask), one event per step. Also when what was typed in a cell went back to the value it started from, and its step was dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;undo&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>undo()</code>. The grid renders its cells again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;redo&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>redo()</code>. The grid renders its cells again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;clear&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the history was emptied: <code>clearHistory()</code>, <code>setData()</code> without <code>add</code>, <code>removeAllData()</code>, <code>resetData()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code>. Only when there was something in it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;remove&#39;</code></td>\n<td>rows were removed for good (<code>removeData()</code>, <code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code>, a new row <code>resetDataSelectionOnly()</code> removed, <code>upsert(rows, { removeMissing: true })</code>) and their changes left the history. Or <code>upsert()</code> gave an edited field the server&#39;s value as its original, and a change of the history that does nothing now was dropped</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>select</code>: <code>row</code> is the index in the displayed rows (<code>getRows()</code>), 0 based. <code>info</code> is\n<code>&#39;select-first&#39;</code> (from <code>select(row, { notify: true })</code> and <code>selectFirst()</code>), <code>&#39;select-prev&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;select-next&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;select-last&#39;</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.on('selection-change', () =&gt; {\n    const count = datasource.getSelectedRows().length;\n    document.getElementById('delete-button')!.toggleAttribute('disabled', count === 0);\n});\n\ndatasource.on('collection-grouped', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.info === 'group') {\n        console.log('grouped on', data.groupings?.map((group) =&gt; group.attribute));\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>rows-changed</code> has the rows an edit changed, and the fields: an edit of a cell, by the user or from\ncode, is in it - see <a href=\"#guide/live-data\">Live data</a>. <code>history-changed</code> says a step was recorded, not\nwhat it is.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-grid-events\">Grid events</h2>\n<p><code>GridEvents</code> lists them. Most send the cell the event happened on, a <code>GridCellEventData</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell</code></td>\n<td>the cell element</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row</code></td>\n<td>the index of the row in the displayed rows (<code>datasource.getRows()</code>), 0 based. 0 for a header cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>column</code></td>\n<td>the index of the column in its part of the grid, 0 based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>celno</code></td>\n<td>the index of the cell in a column with stacked fields, 0 based</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>colType</code></td>\n<td>the part of the grid: <code>&#39;left-pinned&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;middle-pinned&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;right-pinned&#39;</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cellType</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;row-cell&#39;</code> for a data cell, <code>&#39;filter&#39;</code> for a header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>attribute</code></td>\n<td>the field of the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowData</code></td>\n<td>the cell&#39;s row, always a data row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-cell-events\">Cell events</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell-click</code></td>\n<td>a data cell was clicked. Sent after the click, so the row is current already</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>originalEvent</code>: the click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-row-focus</code></td>\n<td>a data cell got focus, by a click or the keyboard</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>originalEvent</code>: the focus event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-focus-button-click</code></td>\n<td>the <code>...</code> button of a cell was clicked, or Space pressed in the cell</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>target</code>: the cell&#39;s input, <code>originalEvent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell-header-focus</code></td>\n<td>a header filter got focus</td>\n<td>the cell, <code>rowData</code>: see below</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>cell-click</code> and <code>cell-row-focus</code> are not sent for a checkbox (boolean) cell.</p>\n<p>The focus button is shown in the focused cell by the column option <code>focusButton</code> (<code>&#39;ALWAYS&#39;</code>,\n<code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;SHOW_IF_GRID_AND_CELL_NOT_READONLY&#39;</code>). Use it to open a picker of\nyour own, and focus <code>target</code> to give the focus back to the cell:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('cell-focus-button-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.attribute === 'company') {\n        console.log('pick a company for', data.rowData);\n        data.target.focus();\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>cell-header-focus</code> sends as <code>rowData</code> the first displayed row, not a row of the header: a group\nrow when the datasource is grouped, <code>undefined</code> when there are no rows. Check it with\n<code>isGroupRow()</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-menu-events\">Menu events</h3>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) sends these when the user picks an entry:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>copy-cell</code></td>\n<td>Copy: Cell Value</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copy-column</code></td>\n<td>Copy: Column (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copy-row</code></td>\n<td>Copy: Row (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>copy-row-col</code></td>\n<td>Copy: Row (selected rows/col)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>paste</code></td>\n<td>Paste: Paste cell (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell, without <code>rowData</code>. <code>entity</code>: the row pasted into. Sent once per row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear: Clear cell (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The entries of the ranges of cells (Range, Paste range, Clear range) and Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V / Delete on\na range send none of these: the cells changed are in <code>rows-changed</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>.</p>\n<p><code>filter-operator-change</code> is sent when the user picks an operator in a header filter&#39;s menu, after\nthe filter ran with it. It sends <code>{ field, operator, column, rebuildHeader }</code>: <code>column</code> is the\ncolumn&#39;s options as the grid uses them, change them and call <code>rebuildHeader()</code> to show it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('filter-operator-change', (data) =&gt; {\n    data.column.placeholderFilter = data.operator; // show the operator in the empty filter cell\n    data.rebuildHeader();\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-range-change\"><code>range-change</code></h3>\n<p><code>range-change</code> is sent when the ranges of cells change: the user dragged over the cells, Shift+clicked,\nCtrl/Cmd+clicked, pressed Alt+Shift+arrows or Esc, moved to another cell or typed in one, or\n<code>setRanges()</code> / <code>clearRanges()</code> were called. Also when the grid cleared them because the displayed rows\nor the layout changed. While the mouse drags it is sent each time the range reaches another cell; it is\nnot sent when nothing changed. The payload is <code>{ ranges }</code>, what <code>getRanges()</code> gives now - <code>[]</code> when\nthey were cleared. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-api\">Selection and clipboard</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('range-change', ({ ranges }) =&gt; {\n    const last = ranges[ranges.length - 1];\n    console.log(last ? `rows ${last.fromRow}-${last.toRow}, ${last.fromField} to ${last.toField}` : 'no cells selected');\n});</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"section-grid-connected\"><code>grid-connected</code></h3>\n<p><code>grid-connected</code> is sent when a grid element connects to the interface: <code>connectInterface()</code>, or\nthe element is put back on the page. <code>createGrid()</code> connects before it returns, so a handler\nsubscribed after <code>createGrid()</code> does not hear the first one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-date-picker\">The date picker</h2>\n<p>A <code>DateInterface</code> - a date picker of your own, outside a grid - sends <code>date-select</code> when the user\npicks a date: the &quot;Use&quot; button sends the date and time, &quot;Clear&quot; sends <code>null</code>. Removing its element\nfrom the page unsubscribes every handler.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type DateElement, DateInterface, type IDateConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/date.css';\n\nconst config: IDateConfig = {\n    datepicker: true,\n    datepickerDate: new Date(),\n    monthsToShow: 1,\n    monthColumns: 1,\n    startMonth: 0,\n    startYear: 2026,\n    weekStart: 1,\n    showWeek: true,\n    isoWeek: true,\n    rowHeight: 22,\n    monthWidth: 230,\n    monthMargin: 10,\n    hideDimmedDates: false,\n    showYearInMonth: false,\n    headerTitle: ''\n};\n\nconst dateInterface = new DateInterface(config);\nconst element = document.createElement('simple-html-date') as DateElement;\nelement.classList.add('simple-html-date');\nelement.connectInterface(dateInterface);\nhost.appendChild(element);\n\ndateInterface.on('date-select', (date) =&gt; {\n    console.log(date ? date.toISOString() : 'cleared');\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The names of the months and days and the buttons are texts, <code>datePicker.*</code>: the page&#39;s\n(<code>setTexts()</code>), or the picker&#39;s own in <code>texts</code> of its config - see <a href=\"#guide/texts/date-picker\">Texts</a>.</p>\n<p>The date pickers the grid opens in date cells and date header filters are its own: they write the\ndate into the cell or the filter, you do not subscribe to them. They show the grid&#39;s texts.</p>\n"},{"id":"menus","title":"Menus","html":"<p>The grid has three right click menus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>row menu</strong>, on a cell: copy, filter on the value, dimmed values, paste, clear, delete, a\nsummary</li>\n<li>the <strong>header menu</strong>, on a header label: pin, hide, the column chooser, sizes, grouping</li>\n<li>the <strong>filter menu</strong>, on a header filter: clear, is blank, the filter editor, the operator, the\nvalue list</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each is a list of entries with ids. The <code>menus</code> hook changes them: hide entries, reorder them,\nchange their labels, add your own. <code>openMenu()</code> opens a menu of your own, in the same style.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-a-menu-opens-and-closes\">How a menu opens and closes</h2>\n<p>Every menu of the grid - the three right click menus, <code>openMenu()</code>, the column chooser, the field\nand operator pickers of the filter editor and the date picker - is a popover (<code>popover=&quot;manual&quot;</code>):\nthe browser shows it in the top layer, over everything on the page. A container with\n<code>overflow: hidden</code> does not cut it off, and no z-index puts something over it. It is on\n<code>document.body</code>, or inside the <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> (the filter editor, or one of yours) or the popover of yours\nthat the element it opens from is in: everything outside a modal dialog is inert, a menu there could\nnot be clicked. A menu has the classes <code>simple-html-grid simple-html-grid-popover</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is one menu at a time: opening one closes the one before.</li>\n<li>It closes on a click outside it, on Esc, when another menu opens, on scroll (with\n<code>autoRemoveContextMenuOnScrollEvent: true</code>) and when the grid leaves the page. Clicking an item\ncloses it too, then runs the item&#39;s action; a click in a block does not.</li>\n<li>An item does not take the focus: it stays on the cell the menu was opened from. When the focus is\nin the menu as it closes - a search field - it goes back to the element that had it when the menu\nopened.</li>\n<li>The column chooser is a panel more than a menu: it stays open while you drag columns from it into\nthe header, and when another menu opens. It closes with its Close button, Esc in its search field,\nand when the grid leaves the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why <code>manual</code> and not <code>auto</code>, which would close it on a click outside and Esc by itself: where the\nbrowser sends <code>contextmenu</code> on the button press (macOS, Linux), the pointerup that follows is outside\nthe menu, and an <code>auto</code> popover would be closed by it the moment a right click opened it. So the grid\ncloses its menus itself, the same way everywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-changing-the-grids-menus\">Changing the grid&#39;s menus</h2>\n<p>The <code>menus</code> hook has a function per menu: <code>row</code>, <code>header</code> and <code>filter</code>. It gets the grid&#39;s own\nentries (<code>defaults</code>) and what the menu was opened on (<code>context</code>), and returns the entries to show.\nIt is called every time the menu opens. Like the other <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">hooks</a> it is given\nnext to the config, not in it: the config stays plain JSON.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks, isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['age'], width: 80 }] }\n};\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: {\n        row: (defaults, context) =&gt; [\n            // hide built-in entries by id: here the summary\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; !entry.id.startsWith('row.summary')),\n            // add your own\n            { type: 'section', id: 'my.section', label: 'Row:' },\n            {\n                id: 'my.logRow',\n                label: 'Log row',\n                visible: () =&gt; !isGroupRow(context.row),\n                action: () =&gt; console.log(context.field, context.row)\n            }\n        ],\n        // reorder: the value list first\n        filter: (defaults) =&gt; [\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; entry.id === 'filter.values'),\n            ...defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; entry.id !== 'filter.values')\n        ],\n        // relabel\n        header: (defaults) =&gt; defaults.map((entry) =&gt; (entry.id === 'header.column.hide' ? { ...entry, label: 'Hide column' } : entry))\n    }\n};\n\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource, hooks });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>defaults</code> always has every entry of the menu. The ones that do not apply here have a <code>visible</code>\ncallback that hides them: paste and clear on a readonly cell, delete in a readonly grid, the\nsummary of a column that is not a number, the operators of another type.</li>\n<li>A section header or a separator is shown as long as it is in the list: hide it together with the\nentries under it.</li>\n<li>The entries in <code>defaults</code> are new objects every time, change them as you like. Their labels are\nthe texts of their ids, already looked up: to translate the menus use the texts (see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts\">Texts</a>), the hook is for changes in one menu.</li>\n<li><code>updateHooks({ menus })</code> changes the functions while the grid is shown,\n<code>updateHooks({ menus: undefined })</code> gives back the defaults.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"context-menu\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-entries\">Entries</h2>\n<p>A <code>MenuEntry</code> is one of:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>entry</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>{ id, label, action, hint?, visible?, disabled?, selected? }</code> - a <code>MenuItem</code></td>\n<td>an entry to click. <code>type: &#39;item&#39;</code> can be left out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;section&#39;, id, label, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuSection</code></td>\n<td>a section header with a line under it, <code>Copy:</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;separator&#39;, id, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a dashed line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{ type: &#39;block&#39;, id, element, visible? }</code> - a <code>MenuBlock</code></td>\n<td>your element, as it is</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Clicking an item closes the menu, then calls <code>action(context)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>hint</code> is shown after the label, in italics and in parentheses: <code>Paste cell (selected rows)</code>.</li>\n<li><code>visible(context)</code>: <code>false</code> leaves the entry out. <code>disabled(context)</code>: <code>true</code> shows the item greyed\nout, a click does nothing. <code>selected(context)</code>: <code>true</code> marks it as the one picked now. They are\ncalled when the menu opens.</li>\n<li>Labels are shown as they are given. The grid&#39;s own come from the texts, by the id of the entry,\nand its section labels end with a colon.</li>\n<li>Every entry is rendered with its id in a <code>data-menu-id</code> attribute, for css and tests. A disabled\nitem has the class <code>simple-html-grid-menu-item-disabled</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every callback gets a <code>MenuContext</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>what it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>menu</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;row&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;header&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;filter&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;custom&#39;</code> (opened with <code>openMenu()</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>field</code></td>\n<td>the field of the cell the menu was opened on. <code>&#39;&#39;</code> for <code>openMenu()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row</code></td>\n<td>row menu: the cell&#39;s row. Header and filter menus: the first displayed row (a group row when grouped, <code>undefined</code> without rows). <code>openMenu()</code>: <code>undefined</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowIndex</code></td>\n<td>the index of <code>row</code> in the displayed rows: 0 in the header and filter menus, -1 for <code>openMenu()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell</code></td>\n<td>the cell the menu was opened on, <code>undefined</code> for <code>openMenu()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gridInterface</code></td>\n<td>the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datasource</code></td>\n<td>the grid&#39;s datasource</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-built-in-ids\">The built-in ids</h2>\n<p><code>MENU_IDS</code> lists the ids of every built-in entry, per menu, in the order the menus show them. A\nsection is <code>menu.section</code>, the entries under it <code>menu.section.entry</code>. The ids stay the same between\nversions, so a menu changed by id keeps working.</p>\n<p>The ids are the ids of the texts too: the label of <code>row.copy.cell</code> is the text <code>row.copy.cell</code>, its\nhint <code>&lt;id&gt;.hint</code>. Change them with <code>setTexts()</code> or the grid&#39;s <code>texts</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/row-menu\">Texts</a>.\nThe &quot;shown as&quot; below is the English text.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-row-menu-menu_idsrow\">Row menu (<code>MENU_IDS.row</code>)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row.edit</code></td>\n<td>Edit: - first in the menu</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo</code></td>\n<td>Undo last change (Ctrl+Z): <code>datasource.undo()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>. Greyed out when there is nothing to undo</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo</code></td>\n<td>Redo (Ctrl+Y): <code>datasource.redo()</code>. Greyed out when there is nothing to redo</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertCell</code></td>\n<td>Undo cell edit: the cell&#39;s original value, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/reverting-a-cell-or-a-row\">Reverting a cell or a row</a>. Greyed out when the cell is not edited</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRow</code></td>\n<td>Undo row edits: the row right clicked back to its original values, its deletion mark taken off - no selection needed. Greyed out when the row is not edited or marked</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows</code></td>\n<td>Undo row edits (selected rows): the selected rows back, deletion marks taken off. Greyed out when no selected row is edited or marked</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy</code></td>\n<td>Copy:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.cell</code></td>\n<td>Cell Value</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column</code></td>\n<td>Column (selected rows)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row</code></td>\n<td>Row (selected rows)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td>Row (selected rows/col): the selected columns, every column when none is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.range</code></td>\n<td>Range: the last range of cells, as Ctrl+C copies it - see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n<td>there are ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter</code></td>\n<td>Filter:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace</code></td>\n<td>Replace filter (all)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add</code></td>\n<td>Add to filter (and)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view</code></td>\n<td>View:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td>Use dimmed values, with a ✓ when it is on</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td>Changed rows only, with a ✓ when it is on: the new, edited and deleted rows of the filter, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/changed-rows-only\">Changed rows only</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste</code></td>\n<td>Paste:</td>\n<td>the grid and the cell are editable, or the grid is and there are ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell</code></td>\n<td>Paste cell (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the grid and the cell are editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.range</code></td>\n<td>Paste range: the clipboard as Ctrl+V pastes it with ranges - one value into every cell of them, a block from the top left of the last one. Reads the clipboard with <code>navigator.clipboard.readText()</code>, the browser may ask the user first</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, there are ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear:</td>\n<td>the grid and the cell are editable, or the grid is and there are ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell</code></td>\n<td>Clear cell (selected rows)</td>\n<td>the grid and the cell are editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.range</code></td>\n<td>Clear range: every cell of the ranges, as Delete</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, there are ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete</code></td>\n<td>Delete:</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows</code></td>\n<td>Rows (selected rows): marks them for deletion, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/deleting-rows\">Deleting rows</a>. Greyed out when no selected row is left to mark</td>\n<td>the grid is editable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo</code></td>\n<td>Undo (selected rows): takes the mark off</td>\n<td>the grid is editable, and a selected row is marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary</code></td>\n<td>Summary:</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.count</code></td>\n<td>Count: the number of selected rows. A click copies the value</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.sum</code></td>\n<td>Sum: of the selected rows&#39; numbers, <code>datasource.aggregate(field, &#39;sum&#39;, rows)</code>. With two decimals at most, as max, min and avg</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.max</code></td>\n<td>Max: the largest number</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.min</code></td>\n<td>Min: the smallest number, 0 included</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.avg</code></td>\n<td>Avg: the average of the numbers, a blank is not counted</td>\n<td>a number field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export</code></td>\n<td>Export:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.csv</code></td>\n<td>CSV, all rows: <code>gridInterface.downloadCsv()</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/export\">Export</a></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.csvSelected</code></td>\n<td>CSV, selected rows: <code>downloadCsv(&#39;export.csv&#39;, { rows: &#39;selected&#39; })</code>. Greyed out when no row is selected</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-header-menu-menu_idsheader\">Header menu (<code>MENU_IDS.header</code>)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>header.column</code></td>\n<td>Column:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeft</code></td>\n<td>Pin left</td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeftCopy</code></td>\n<td>Pin left (copy): pinned, and kept where it is</td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRight</code></td>\n<td>Pin right</td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRightCopy</code></td>\n<td>Pin right (copy)</td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.unpin</code></td>\n<td>Unpin</td>\n<td>pinned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.hide</code></td>\n<td>Hide</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.removeSorting</code></td>\n<td>Remove sorting: keeps the sort of the grouped fields</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooserSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooser</code></td>\n<td>Column chooser: every field, drag one into the header</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size</code></td>\n<td>Size:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.column</code></td>\n<td>Resize Column: to its content</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.all</code></td>\n<td>Resize all columns</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping</code></td>\n<td>Grouping:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.expandAll</code></td>\n<td>Expand all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.collapseAll</code></td>\n<td>Collapse all</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear all: no grouping</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-menu-menu_idsfilter\">Filter menu (<code>MENU_IDS.filter</code>)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>shown as</th>\n<th>shown when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter</code></td>\n<td>Filter:</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clear</code></td>\n<td>Clear Filter: the column&#39;s conditions</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clearAll</code></td>\n<td>Clear All Filters</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.blankSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isBlank</code></td>\n<td>Set &quot;Is Blank&quot;</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isNotBlank</code></td>\n<td>Set &quot;Is Not Blank&quot;</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advancedSeparator</code></td>\n<td>a line</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advanced</code></td>\n<td>Advanced Filter: opens the filter editor</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator</code></td>\n<td>Set Operator:</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Greater than or equal</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Less than or equal</td>\n<td>a number, date or datetime field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.equal</code></td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.notEqualTo</code></td>\n<td>Not Equal</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.contains</code></td>\n<td>Contains</td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values</code></td>\n<td>the value list: a search box, a checkbox per value, &quot;Run Search&quot;. One <code>MenuBlock</code>, its texts are <code>filter.values.*</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/filtering/the-header-filter-menu\">Filtering</a> for what the filter menu&#39;s entries do.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { type GridHooks, MENU_IDS } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a row menu with the copy entries only\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: { row: (defaults) =&gt; defaults.filter((entry) =&gt; entry.id.startsWith('row.copy')) }\n};\n\nMENU_IDS.row.includes('row.paste.cell'); // true</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-menu-of-your-own-openmenu-and-closemenu\">A menu of your own: <code>openMenu()</code> and <code>closeMenu()</code></h2>\n<p><code>gridInterface.openMenu(at, entries)</code> opens a menu of your own, with the same entries:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.on('cell-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.attribute !== 'fruit') {\n        return;\n    }\n    const pick = (fruit: string | null) =&gt; {\n        data.rowData.fruit = fruit;\n        gridInterface.refresh();\n    };\n    gridInterface.openMenu(data.originalEvent, [\n        { type: 'section', id: 'fruit', label: 'Pick a fruit:' },\n        { id: 'fruit.apple', label: 'Apple', action: () =&gt; pick('Apple') },\n        { id: 'fruit.banana', label: 'Banana', action: () =&gt; pick('Banana') },\n        { type: 'separator', id: 'fruit.separator' },\n        { id: 'fruit.none', label: 'None', hint: 'clears the cell', action: () =&gt; pick(null) }\n    ]);\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>at</code> is where it opens:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a mouse event: its top left corner at the mouse</li>\n<li>a <code>MenuPosition</code>, <code>{ x, y }</code> in px from the window&#39;s left and top edges: its top left corner there</li>\n<li>an element: under the element, lined up with its left edge</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The menu is kept in the window, 4px from its edges - every menu of the grid, the long row menu too:\nit opens below the point, above it when there is no room below, and next to it (on its right, or on\nits left when there is no room on the right) when there is room neither below nor above. A menu taller\nthan the window gets the window&#39;s height and scrolls.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is one menu at a time: opening one closes the one before, the grid&#39;s own too.</li>\n<li>The grid closes it as its own menus: on a click outside it, on Esc, when another menu opens, on\nscroll (with <code>autoRemoveContextMenuOnScrollEvent: true</code>) and when the grid leaves the page, see\n<a href=\"#guide/menus/how-a-menu-opens-and-closes\">above</a>. Opened from a click handler, it stays open: that click does\nnot count as a click outside.</li>\n<li>Opened from an element in a <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> of yours, or a popover, it is put in that element, so it can\nbe used there.</li>\n<li><code>gridInterface.closeMenu()</code> closes the open menu from code.</li>\n<li>The callbacks of its entries get a context with <code>menu: &#39;custom&#39;</code> and no field, row or cell: let\nthem use what they were made for, as <code>pick</code> above.</li>\n<li>It does nothing while the grid is not on the page.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-dropdown-grid-as-a-block\">A dropdown grid as a block</h2>\n<p>A <code>MenuBlock</code> shows an element of yours in the menu: a second grid as a dropdown, a form, a color\npicker. The grid puts it on the page, places it and closes it as any menu - Esc too, also when the\nfocus is in the block. A click in the block does not close the menu: call <code>closeMenu()</code> when the user\nhas picked. The dropdown grid&#39;s own menus open over it.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, type GridConfig, type GridElement, GridInterface } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// in the main grid's config:\n// readonly: false, columns: { company: { readonly: true, focusButton: 'SHOW_IF_GRID_NOT_READONLY' } }\n\nconst companies = new Datasource();\ncompanies.setData([{ name: 'ACME' }, { name: 'GLOBEX' }, { name: 'INITECH' }]);\n\nconst dropdownConfig: GridConfig = {\n    panelHeight: 0,\n    footerHeight: 10,\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 250 }] }\n};\n\ngridInterface.on('cell-focus-button-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    if (data.attribute !== 'company') {\n        return;\n    }\n\n    const dropdown = document.createElement('simple-html-grid') as GridElement;\n    dropdown.style.width = '280px';\n    dropdown.style.height = '250px';\n    dropdown.connectInterface(new GridInterface(dropdownConfig, companies));\n\n    // a click on a company picks it: the cell's click made its row current\n    dropdown.addEventListener('click', (event) =&gt; {\n        if (event.target instanceof HTMLElement &amp;&amp; event.target.classList.contains('simple-html-grid-cell-input')) {\n            gridInterface.closeMenu();\n            data.rowData.company = companies.currentEntity?.name;\n            gridInterface.refresh();\n            data.target.focus();\n        }\n    });\n\n    gridInterface.openMenu(data.cell, [{ type: 'block', id: 'company-picker', element: dropdown }]);\n});</code></pre>\n<p>Give the element its size, the menu&#39;s frame gives it a border and a shadow. The column is readonly,\nso the dropdown is the only way to change the value. <code>allowPasteClearOnly: true</code> instead of\n<code>readonly</code> keeps paste and clear working too.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"dropdown-grid\"></div>\n"},{"id":"migrating-from-v5","title":"Migrating from v5","html":"<p>v6 changes the API: the datasource knows its fields, the grid config is split from its state, events\nare subscribed one by one, and filters, menus and options are plain data. The config and the\ndatasource&#39;s fields are plain JSON: formatters are named by id, and the functions of a grid are\nhooks next to the config. TypeScript points to most of the places to change.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md\">MIGRATION.md</a> has every breaking change with code before and after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#package\">Package</a>: the css path, installing from git</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#renamed\">Renamed</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#removed\">Removed</a>: <code>Formater</code> is\n<code>Formatter</code>, <code>deSelectAll</code> is <code>deselectAll</code> ...</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#events\">Events</a>: <code>on()</code> instead of <code>addEventListener</code>, kebab case names</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#group-rows-are-not-entities\">Group rows are not entities</a>: <code>GroupRow</code>,\n<code>isGroupRow()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#fields-and-formatters\">Fields and formatters</a>: <code>new Datasource(options)</code>, types in\n<code>fields</code>, <code>date</code> and <code>datetime</code>, formatters per field by id, <code>registerFormatter()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#filters\">Filters</a>: one filter format, <code>and()</code> / <code>or()</code> / <code>where()</code>, and\n<a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#saved-configs\">a converter for saved v5 filters</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#options-objects-instead-of-boolean-flags\">Options objects instead of boolean flags</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#change-tracking-getchanges\">Change tracking</a>: the typed <code>getChanges()</code>, a new\nrow marked for deletion stays (marked), <code>unmarkForDeletion()</code>, <code>acceptChanges()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#validation-mandatory-is-the-required-rule\">Validation</a>: <code>mandatory</code> is the\nfield&#39;s <code>required</code> rule, <code>registerValidator()</code>, <code>validate()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#grid-config\">Grid config</a>: <code>columns</code> and <code>layout</code>, the state, and\n<a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#a-v5-config-converted\">a converter for a v5 config</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#setup-callbacks-and-methods-of-the-grid\">Setup, callbacks and methods of the grid</a>:\n<code>createGrid()</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> / <code>cellClass</code> hooks, <code>updateConfig()</code>, <code>updateHooks()</code>, renamed\nmethods</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#menus\">Menus</a>: the <code>menus</code> hook, <code>openMenu()</code></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#texts\">Texts</a>: every text has an id, <code>setTexts()</code> and <code>GridConfig.texts</code>;\n<code>tooltipText</code> is <code>texts</code> with <code>.tooltip</code> ids, <code>OPERATORS</code> and the month and day names are texts</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#other-behaviour-changes\">Other behaviour changes</a>: <code>currentEntity</code> after\n<code>setData()</code>, <code>selectAll()</code>, <code>removeGroup()</code>, <code>expandGroup()</code>, grouping always sorted</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#internals-are-no-longer-in-the-typings\">Internals are no longer in the typings</a>\nand <a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#types-are-stricter\">Types are stricter</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below are the ten changes almost every app has to make.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-1-css-and-setup\">1. Css and setup</h2>\n<p>The css is imported from the package root, and <code>createGrid()</code> replaces the element, its class and\n<code>connectInterface()</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nimport '@simple-html/grid/dist/grid.css';\n\nconst gridInterface = new GridInterface(gridConfig, datasource);\nconst element = document.createElement('simple-html-grid') as GridElement;\nelement.style.width = '100%';\nelement.style.height = '100%';\nelement.classList.add('simple-html-grid');\nelement.connectInterface(gridInterface);\ncontainer.appendChild(element);</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { createGrid, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\nimport '@simple-html/grid/grid.css';\n\ndeclare const gridConfig: GridConfig;\n\nconst grid = createGrid(host, { config: gridConfig, datasource });\ngrid.destroy(); // when done with it, new in v6</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/getting-started\">Getting started</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-2-the-datasource-takes-one-options-object-with-the-types\">2. The datasource takes one options object, with the types</h2>\n<p><code>new Datasource(new DataContainer(key), options)</code> is <code>new Datasource({ key, ... })</code>. The types of\nthe fields move from the grid config&#39;s <code>attributes</code> and <code>setDates()</code> to <code>fields</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nconst datasource = new Datasource(new DataContainer('id'));\ndatasource.setDates(['born']);\n// and in the grid config: selectionMode: 'single',\n// attributes: [{ attribute: 'age', type: 'number' }, { attribute: 'born', type: 'date' }]</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    key: 'id',\n    fields: { age: 'number', born: 'date' }, // a field not listed is text\n    selectionMode: 'single'\n});</code></pre>\n<p>The selection mode is the datasource&#39;s: <code>GridConfig.selectionMode</code> is removed. v5&#39;s grid set its own\n(default <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>) on the datasource, replacing the datasource&#39;s. See\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/the-selection-mode\">Datasource</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-3-columns-and-layout-replace-attributes-and-columns\">3. <code>columns</code> and <code>layout</code> replace <code>attributes</code> and <code>columns*</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nconst gridConfig: GridConfig = {\n    columnsPinnedLeft: [{ rows: ['name'], width: 150 }],\n    columnsCenter: [{ rows: ['age', 'born'], width: 100 }],\n    attributes: [\n        { attribute: 'name', label: 'Name', placeHolderFilter: 'search' },\n        { attribute: 'age', readonly: true }\n    ]\n};</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst gridConfig: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        name: { label: 'Name', placeholderFilter: 'search' },\n        age: { readonly: true }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        left: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }],\n        center: [{ fields: ['age', 'born'], width: 100 }]\n    }\n};</code></pre>\n<p><code>placeHolderRow</code> and <code>placeHolderFilter</code> are <code>placeholderRow</code> and <code>placeholderFilter</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/grid-config\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<p><strong><code>mandatory</code> is a validation rule of the datasource field.</strong> v5&#39;s <code>mandatory: true</code> only drew\nstripes; v6 validates the field - the edits, and <code>validate()</code> before a save - and draws the same\nstripes. A config with <code>mandatory</code> throws, naming the field:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nattributes: [{ attribute: 'company', mandatory: true }]</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst companies = new Datasource({ fields: { company: { validate: ['required'] } } });</code></pre>\n<p><code>required</code> takes 0 as missing, like the filter&#39;s <code>IS_BLANK</code>: a number field of 0 is striped now,\nand a checkbox that is unchecked (<code>false</code>) is not. See <a href=\"#guide/editing/validation\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-4-events-on\">4. Events: <code>on()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.addEventListener({\n    handleEvent: (e) =&gt; {\n        if (e.type === 'selectionChange') {\n            updateToolbar();\n        }\n        return true; // keep listening\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\ndeclare function updateToolbar(): void;\n\nconst off = datasource.on('selection-change', () =&gt; updateToolbar());\noff(); // unsubscribe</code></pre>\n<p>The handler gets the payload, not <code>{ type, data }</code>. What it returns is ignored. <code>selectionChange</code>,\n<code>currentEntity</code> and <code>gridConnected</code> are kebab case now: <code>selection-change</code>, <code>current-entity</code> and\n<code>grid-connected</code>. See <a href=\"#guide/events\">Events</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-5-filters-one-format-and-builders\">5. Filters: one format and builders</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.filter({\n    type: 'GROUP',\n    logicalOperator: 'AND',\n    filterArguments: [\n        { type: 'CONDITION', logicalOperator: 'NONE', attribute: 'age', attributeType: 'number', operator: 'GREATER_THAN', value: 18, valueType: 'VALUE' }\n    ]\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { and, where } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.filter(and(where('age', 'GREATER_THAN', 18)));\ndatasource.filter(null); // no filter. In v5 filter(null) ran the filter again</code></pre>\n<p>The type comes from the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code>. A filter saved by v5 is ignored, MIGRATION.md has a\n<a href=\"https://github.com/airemote/simple-html/blob/v6_maybe/MIGRATION.md#saved-configs\">converter</a>. See <a href=\"#guide/filtering\">Filtering</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-6-options-objects-instead-of-boolean-flags\">6. Options objects instead of boolean flags</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.setData(rows, true);\ndatasource.sort({ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }, true);\ndatasource.markForDeletion(null, true);\nconst dataRows = datasource.getRows(true);</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\ndeclare const rows: object[];\n\ndatasource.setData(rows, { add: true });\ndatasource.sort({ attribute: 'age', ascending: false }, { add: true });\ndatasource.markAllForDeletion();\nconst dataRows = datasource.getDataRows();</code></pre>\n<p>A boolean passed the v5 way throws a <code>TypeError</code> that shows the v6 way to write it.</p>\n<p><strong><code>setData()</code> runs the filter that is set</strong> on the new rows. v5 showed them all (<code>reRunFilter</code>\ndefaulted to <code>false</code>), also the ones the filter leaves out. <code>setData(rows, { refilter: false })</code> does\nwhat v5 did. See <a href=\"#guide/datasource/data-in-setdata\">Datasource</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-7-formatters-per-field\">7. Formatters per field</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ndatasource.setNumberFormater(NumberFormaterComma);\ndatasource.setDateFormater(DateFormaterDDMMYYYY);</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst orders = new Datasource({\n    fields: { price: 'number', ordered: 'date' },\n    formatters: { number: 'numberComma', date: 'dateDDMMYYYY' }\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>Formater</code> is spelled <code>Formatter</code>, and a formatter is an object with <code>format</code> and <code>parse</code>. The\noptions name it by its id: the built-in ones are registered from the start, register your own with\n<code>registerFormatter(&#39;myId&#39;, formatter)</code> and give <code>formatter: &#39;myId&#39;</code> to the field. See\n<a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-8-the-state-getstate-and-setstate\">8. The state: <code>getState()</code> and <code>setState()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\nlocalStorage.setItem('grid', JSON.stringify(gridInterface.saveConfig()));\ngridInterface.loadConfig(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('grid')));\ngridInterface.loadInitConfig();</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nlocalStorage.setItem('grid', JSON.stringify(gridInterface.getState()));\ngridInterface.setState(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('grid') ?? '{}'));\ngridInterface.resetState();</code></pre>\n<p>The state has no options: change an option with <code>updateConfig()</code>. A grid that starts sorted,\ngrouped or filtered sets it in <code>initialState</code>, not as <code>sorting</code> / <code>grouping</code> / <code>filter</code> in the\nconfig. See <a href=\"#guide/state\">State</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-9-callbacks-are-hooks-renamed-methods\">9. Callbacks are hooks, renamed methods</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ngridInterface.readonlySetter((attribute, rowData) =&gt; (attribute === 'company' &amp;&amp; !rowData.isActive ? true : null));\ngridInterface.cellAppendClassSetter((attribute, rowData) =&gt;\n    attribute === 'balance' &amp;&amp; rowData.balance &lt; 0 ? { inputClass: 'red', dimmedClass: '' } : { inputClass: '', dimmedClass: '' }\n);\ngridInterface.triggerScrollEvent();\ngridInterface.triggerRebuild();</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\nimport { createGrid, type GridConfig, type GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst gridConfig: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['company'], width: 150 }, { fields: ['balance'], width: 100 }] }\n};\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    isReadonly: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'company' &amp;&amp; !row.isActive ? true : null),\n    cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; (field === 'balance' &amp;&amp; row.balance &lt; 0 ? { input: 'red' } : null)\n};\nconst grid = createGrid(host, { config: gridConfig, datasource, hooks });\n\ngrid.updateHooks({ cellClass: undefined }); // was cellAppendClassSetter(null)\ngrid.refresh(); // was triggerScrollEvent()\ngrid.rebuild(); // was triggerRebuild()</code></pre>\n<p>The row comes first. The hooks are given next to the config, so the config stays plain JSON.\n<code>getAttributeColumns()</code> is <code>getLayoutFields()</code>, <code>getOptionalAttributes()</code> is <code>getHiddenFields()</code>.\nSee <a href=\"#guide/grid-config/hooks\">Grid config</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-10-menus\">10. Menus</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v5\ngridInterface.contextMenuCustom(data.originalEvent, [{ value: 'Apple', label: 'Apple', isHeader: false }], data.cell, (value) =&gt; {\n    data.rowData.fruit = value;\n    gridInterface.triggerScrollEvent();\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// v6\ngridInterface.on('cell-click', (data) =&gt; {\n    gridInterface.openMenu(data.originalEvent, [\n        {\n            id: 'fruit.apple',\n            label: 'Apple',\n            action: () =&gt; {\n                data.rowData.fruit = 'Apple';\n                gridInterface.refresh();\n            }\n        }\n    ]);\n});</code></pre>\n<p><code>setContextMenuElement(element)</code> is a block of a menu: <code>openMenu(cell, [{ type: &#39;block&#39;, id, element }])</code>.\nThe grid&#39;s own menus are changed with the <code>menus</code> hook. See <a href=\"#guide/menus\">Menus</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-group-rows\">Group rows</h2>\n<p>One more change that hits code that reads rows: when grouped, the group rows in <code>getRows()</code> are a\n<code>GroupRow</code>, not an entity. <code>isGroupRow(row)</code> replaces <code>row.__group</code>, and <code>getDataRows()</code> gives the\ndata rows only. See <a href=\"#guide/sorting-grouping/group-rows\">Sorting and grouping</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"texts","title":"Texts","html":"<p>Every text the grid shows has an id: the menus, the filter editor, the column chooser, the footer,\nthe tooltips, the date picker and the labels a screen reader reads - and so do the texts the\ndatasource makes, the validation messages and <code>getFilterString()</code>. <code>TEXTS</code> has them all, with their\nEnglish text. Change them with <code>setTexts()</code> for the page, and with <code>texts</code> in the config for one\ngrid. A translation is a JSON object of texts by id: it can be stored with the config, in a backend.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"translation\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-translating\">Translating</h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts, type Texts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// a text for each id to change, the rest stay English\nconst norwegian: Texts = {\n    'row.copy': 'Kopier:',\n    'row.summary.count': 'Antall: {count}',\n    'filterEditor.addCondition.tooltip': 'Legg til betingelse',\n    'validation.required': 'Må fylles ut'\n};\n\n// once, when the page loads: before the datasources and grids are made\nsetTexts(norwegian);</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>TEXTS</code> is every id with its English text: <code>JSON.stringify(TEXTS, null, 2)</code> is a file to hand a\ntranslator. The ids are <a href=\"#guide/texts/all-ids\">below</a>.</li>\n<li><code>Texts</code> takes some of the ids. Typed <code>Record&lt;TextId, string&gt;</code> instead, a translation must have every\none: the compiler names those missing, also after an update of the grid adds ids. The\n<a href=\"#sample/translation\">Translation sample</a> does that.</li>\n<li>A translation is plain JSON, it can come from a backend as it is:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst response = await fetch('/api/texts/nb');\nsetTexts(await response.json());</code></pre>\n<p><code>setTexts()</code> throws a <code>TypeError</code> when it gets something that is not an object, or a text that is not\na string - and so does a grid given such <code>texts</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-where-a-text-comes-from\">Where a text comes from</h2>\n<p>A text is looked up in this order, the first that has the id wins:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>GridConfig.texts</code> - the texts of one grid, for a grid that differs from the rest of the page</li>\n<li><code>setTexts()</code> - the texts of the page</li>\n<li><code>TEXTS</code> - the English defaults</li>\n</ol>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig, setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({ 'row.copy': 'Kopier:', 'row.paste': 'Lim inn:' }); // every grid of the page\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] },\n    texts: { 'row.copy': 'Copy:' } // this grid: Copy: and Lim inn:\n};\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<p><strong>The datasource has no grid</strong>: the texts it makes - the validation messages (<code>validation.*</code>),\n<code>getFilterString()</code> (<code>filterString.*</code>) and the name of the group of blank values (<code>grouping.blank</code>) -\ncome from <code>setTexts()</code> and the defaults only. A grid&#39;s <code>texts</code> does not change them, also not where\nthat grid shows them. There is no <code>texts</code> option on the datasource: the validation runs in its\n<code>DataContainer</code>, which datasources can share, and a page has one language.</p>\n<p><strong>Each replaces what was there</strong>: <code>setTexts()</code> replaces the texts set before, so an id left out shows\nits default again and <code>setTexts({})</code> is English. <code>updateConfig({ texts })</code> replaces the grid&#39;s texts\nas a whole, <code>texts: undefined</code> removes them. The object given is copied by <code>setTexts()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-texts-with-values\">Texts with values</h2>\n<p>A text with <code>{name}</code> in it gets a value when it is shown: <code>&#39;row.summary.count&#39;: &#39;Count: {count}&#39;</code>\nshows <code>Count: 3</code>. The tables <a href=\"#guide/texts/all-ids\">below</a> name the values of each text. Keep the names in a\ntranslation, a value can go anywhere in the text; a name the text has no value for is shown as it is.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({ 'row.summary.count': '{count} rader', 'footer.rowCount': 'Viser {shown} av {total}' });</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-ids\">The ids</h2>\n<p>The ids are grouped by area, the area first. Within an area:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A menu entry&#39;s text has the entry&#39;s id</strong>, see <a href=\"#guide/menus/the-built-in-ids\">Menus</a>: the text of the\nentry <code>row.copy.cell</code> is <code>row.copy.cell</code>. The text in parentheses after an entry, its hint, is\n<code>&lt;id&gt;.hint</code>. The separators and the value list (<code>filter.values</code>, a block) have no text of their own,\nthe parts of the value list are <code>filter.values.*</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>A tooltip is <code>&lt;id&gt;.tooltip</code></strong>: <code>filterEditor.filterOnly</code> is the button, <code>filterEditor.filterOnly.tooltip</code>\nits tooltip. A tooltip of an icon has no text without <code>.tooltip</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>An operator has a name in each place it is shown</strong>, the <code>FilterOperator</code> at the end:\n<code>filterEditor.operator.EQUAL</code> in the filter editor, <code>footer.operator.EQUAL</code> on a chip in the footer\n(symbols, so a chip stays short) and <code>filterString.operator.EQUAL</code> in <code>getFilterString()</code>. AND and OR\nas well: <code>filterEditor.operatorAnd</code>, <code>footer.and</code>, <code>filterString.and</code>. The filter menu&#39;s operators\nare menu entries: <code>filter.operator.equal</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-names-of-the-months-and-days\">The names of the months and days</h3>\n<p>The date picker shows the names of the months, and of the days over its columns. Each name is a text\nof its own - <code>datePicker.month.january</code> to <code>datePicker.month.december</code>, <code>datePicker.day.sunday</code> to\n<code>datePicker.day.saturday</code> - not a list in the date picker&#39;s config. So a translation is one flat\nobject of strings for every part of the grid, and <code>setTexts()</code> reaches the date pickers of every grid\nwith the rest. The day names are in javascript&#39;s order, Sunday first; <code>weekStart</code> picks the first\ncolumn.</p>\n<p>A <code>DateInterface</code> of your own shows the same texts: the page&#39;s, or its own in <code>IDateConfig.texts</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-messages-of-your-own\">Messages of your own</h2>\n<p>A validator returns the message, or a text id: the built-ins return the <code>validation.*</code> ids. Return an\nid of your own and give it a text with <code>setTexts()</code>, and the message is translated like the\nbuilt-ins&#39;. A rule&#39;s <code>message</code> can be a text id too. An id no text is found for is shown as it is, so\na message written out works as well.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource, registerValidator, setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nregisterValidator('phoneNO', (value) =&gt;\n    typeof value === 'string' &amp;&amp; value !== '' &amp;&amp; !/^\\d{8}$/.test(value) ? 'validation.phoneNO' : null\n);\n\nsetTexts({\n    'validation.phoneNO': 'Må være 8 siffer',\n    'age.tooOld': 'For gammel, høyst {value}'\n});\n\nconst people = new Datasource({\n    fields: {\n        phone: { validate: ['phoneNO'] },\n        age: { type: 'number', validate: [{ id: 'max', value: 150, message: 'age.tooOld' }] }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"#guide/editing/messages\">Validation</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-when-a-changed-text-shows\">When a changed text shows</h2>\n<p>Nothing is shown again when a text changes, it is read when what shows it is made:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>what</th>\n<th>shows a changed text</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the grid: header, rows, footer</td>\n<td>after its next rebuild. <code>updateConfig({ texts })</code> rebuilds, after <code>setTexts()</code> call <code>gridInterface.rebuild()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a menu, the column chooser, the filter editor, the date picker</td>\n<td>the next time it opens (the filter editor also when it is rendered again after an edit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>getFilterString()</code></td>\n<td>on its next call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a validation message</td>\n<td>when the value is validated: an edit of the field, or <code>validate()</code>. An error already there keeps its text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the name of the group of blank values</td>\n<td>the next time the rows are grouped: a filter, a sort, <code>setData()</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>So <code>setTexts()</code> goes before the datasources and grids are made. To switch the language of a page\nthat is shown:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts, type Texts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndeclare const swedish: Texts;\n\nsetTexts(swedish);\ngridInterface.rebuild(); // every grid of the page</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-not-texts\">Not texts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>What the config and the data have: the column labels (<code>columns.&lt;field&gt;.label</code>, <code>FieldConfig.label</code>),\n<code>placeholderRow</code> and <code>placeholderFilter</code>, the group titles, the values.</li>\n<li>The placeholders of the date formatters, <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> and <code>DD.MM.YYYY</code> in an empty date header\nfilter: give the column a <code>placeholderFilter</code>, or use a formatter of your own with another\n<code>placeholder</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/formatting/a-formatter-of-your-own\">Formatting</a>).</li>\n<li>Symbols and numbers: <code>✕</code> closes the filter editor, <code>✓</code> marks the dimmed values on, <code>?</code> a footer\nchip without a field or operator, <code>(12)</code> the count of a value in the\nvalue list.</li>\n<li><code>headerTitle</code> of a <code>DateInterface</code> calendar: your own text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-all-ids\">All ids</h2>\n<h3 id=\"section-row-menu\">Row menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a cell.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>row.copy</code></td>\n<td><code>Copy:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>Cell Value</code></td>\n<td>copies the value of the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Column</code></td>\n<td>copies the column of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.column.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row</code></td>\n<td><code>Row</code></td>\n<td>copies the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.row.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns</code></td>\n<td><code>Row</code></td>\n<td>copies the selected columns of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.selectedColumns.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows/col</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.copy.range</code></td>\n<td><code>Range</code></td>\n<td>copies the last range of cells, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace</code></td>\n<td><code>Replace filter</code></td>\n<td>filters on the value of the cell, replacing the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.replace.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>all</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add</code></td>\n<td><code>Add to filter</code></td>\n<td>ANDs the value of the cell with the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.filter.add.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>and</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view</code></td>\n<td><code>View:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.dimRepeatedValues</code></td>\n<td><code>Use dimmed values</code></td>\n<td>turns <code>dimRepeatedValues</code> on and off. A <code>✓ </code> is put in front while it is on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.view.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Changed rows only</code></td>\n<td>turns <code>datasource.filterChangedOnly()</code> on and off: the new, edited and deleted rows only. A <code>✓ </code> is put in front while it is on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste:</code></td>\n<td>section header, an editable cell or ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste cell</code></td>\n<td>pastes into the column of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.cell.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.paste.range</code></td>\n<td><code>Paste range</code></td>\n<td>pastes the clipboard into the ranges of cells, as Ctrl+V</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear:</code></td>\n<td>section header, an editable cell or ranges of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear cell</code></td>\n<td>empties the column of the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.cell.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.clear.range</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear range</code></td>\n<td>empties the cells of the ranges, as Delete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete:</code></td>\n<td>section header, an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows</code></td>\n<td><code>Rows</code></td>\n<td>marks the selected rows for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.rows.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo</code></td>\n<td>takes the mark off the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.delete.undo.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit</code></td>\n<td><code>Edit:</code></td>\n<td>section header, an editable grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo last change</code></td>\n<td>undoes the last step of the undo history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.undo.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+Z</code></td>\n<td>its hint, the key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo</code></td>\n<td><code>Redo</code></td>\n<td>redoes the last step undone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.redo.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+Y</code></td>\n<td>its hint, the key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertCell</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo cell edit</code></td>\n<td>puts the original value of the cell back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRow</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo row edits</code></td>\n<td>puts the original values of the row right clicked back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows</code></td>\n<td><code>Undo row edits</code></td>\n<td>puts the original values of the selected rows back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.edit.revertRows.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>selected rows</code></td>\n<td>its hint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary</code></td>\n<td><code>Summary:</code></td>\n<td>section header, a number column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.count</code></td>\n<td><code>Count: {count}</code></td>\n<td><code>{count}</code>: the number of selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.sum</code></td>\n<td><code>Sum: {sum}</code></td>\n<td><code>{sum}</code>: their sum, formatted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Max: {max}</code></td>\n<td><code>{max}</code>: the largest value, formatted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Min: {min}</code></td>\n<td><code>{min}</code>: the smallest value, formatted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.summary.avg</code></td>\n<td><code>Avg: {avg}</code></td>\n<td><code>{avg}</code>: the average, formatted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export</code></td>\n<td><code>Export:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.csv</code></td>\n<td><code>CSV, all rows</code></td>\n<td>downloads the rows the filter lets through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>row.export.csvSelected</code></td>\n<td><code>CSV, selected rows</code></td>\n<td>downloads the selected rows</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-header-menu\">Header menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a header label.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>header.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Column:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeft</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin left</code></td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinLeftCopy</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin left (copy)</code></td>\n<td>not pinned left</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRight</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin right</code></td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.pinRightCopy</code></td>\n<td><code>Pin right (copy)</code></td>\n<td>not pinned right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.unpin</code></td>\n<td><code>Unpin</code></td>\n<td>pinned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.hide</code></td>\n<td><code>Hide</code></td>\n<td>takes the column out of the layout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.removeSorting</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove sorting</code></td>\n<td>keeps the sort of the grouped fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.column.chooser</code></td>\n<td><code>Column chooser</code></td>\n<td>opens the column chooser</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size</code></td>\n<td><code>Size:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.column</code></td>\n<td><code>Resize Column</code></td>\n<td>sizes the column to its content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.size.all</code></td>\n<td><code>Resize all columns</code></td>\n<td>sizes every column to its content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping</code></td>\n<td><code>Grouping:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.expandAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Expand all</code></td>\n<td>expands every group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.collapseAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Collapse all</code></td>\n<td>collapses every group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header.grouping.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear all</code></td>\n<td>removes the grouping</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-menu\">Filter menu</h3>\n<p>Right click on a header filter.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear Filter</code></td>\n<td>removes the column&#39;s conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.clearAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear All Filters</code></td>\n<td>removes the whole filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isBlank</code></td>\n<td><code>Set &quot;Is Blank&quot;</code></td>\n<td>replaces the column&#39;s conditions with <code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.isNotBlank</code></td>\n<td><code>Set &quot;Is Not Blank&quot;</code></td>\n<td>replaces the column&#39;s conditions with <code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.filter.advanced</code></td>\n<td><code>Advanced Filter</code></td>\n<td>opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator</code></td>\n<td><code>Set Operator:</code></td>\n<td>section header, not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.greaterThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater than or equal</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.lessThanOrEqualTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Less than or equal</code></td>\n<td>a number or date field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.equal</code></td>\n<td><code>Equal</code></td>\n<td>not a boolean field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.notEqualTo</code></td>\n<td><code>Not Equal</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.operator.contains</code></td>\n<td><code>Contains</code></td>\n<td>a text field</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-value-list\">The value list</h3>\n<p>The part of the filter menu with a checkbox per value, for a text field.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.search</code></td>\n<td><code>Search:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.searchPlaceholder</code></td>\n<td><code>type to search</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of the search box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.availableOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter Available</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox that lists only the values the other filters let through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.rowsFound</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} rows found</code></td>\n<td><code>{count}</code>: the number of values listed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.truncated</code></td>\n<td><code>Showing only first {max} !</code></td>\n<td><code>{max}</code>: the most values listed (100), when there are more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.selectAll</code></td>\n<td><code>Select All</code></td>\n<td>the checkbox that checks every value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>Blank</code></td>\n<td>the value of the empty cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.values.run</code></td>\n<td><code>Run Search</code></td>\n<td>the button that filters on the checked values</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-column-chooser\">Column chooser</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.title</code></td>\n<td><code>All Fields:</code></td>\n<td>section header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.search</code></td>\n<td><code>search</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of the search box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>columnChooser.close</code></td>\n<td><code>Close</code></td>\n<td>the button that closes it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-filter-editor\">Filter editor</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.title</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter Editor</code></td>\n<td>the title</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.title.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Drag to move the dialog</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the title</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.close.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close without filtering</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the <code>✕</code> in the title bar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorAnd</code></td>\n<td><code>AND</code></td>\n<td>the button of an AND group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorAnd.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>All conditions in this group must match. Click for OR</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorOr</code></td>\n<td><code>OR</code></td>\n<td>the button of an OR group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorOr.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Any condition in this group may match. Click for AND</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.wrapGroup.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Wrap everything in this group inside a new parent group</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of an icon of a group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.addSubGroup.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Add a sub group inside this group</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of an icon of a group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.addCondition.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Add a condition to this group</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of an icon of a group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.deleteGroup.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete this group and everything in it</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the trash icon of a sub group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.clearAll.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear all conditions</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the trash icon of the top group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.field</code></td>\n<td><code>Query Field:</code></td>\n<td>label over the field of a condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.operator</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator:</code></td>\n<td>label over the operator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.label.value</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter value:</code></td>\n<td>label over the value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickAttribute</code></td>\n<td><code>Click me to select field</code></td>\n<td>a condition without a field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickAttribute.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick which column this condition looks at</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickOperator</code></td>\n<td><code>Click me to select Operator</code></td>\n<td>a condition without an operator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickOperator.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick how the value is compared, for example equal or greater than</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickValueAttribute</code></td>\n<td><code>Click me to select attribute</code></td>\n<td>compared with a field that is not picked yet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.pickValueAttribute.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Pick which column to compare against</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.deleteCondition.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Delete this condition</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the trash icon of a condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.useValue.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Compare against a typed in value instead of another column</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the icon that switches to a value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.useAttribute.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Compare against another column instead of a typed in value</code></td>\n<td>tooltip of the icon that switches to another field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterAndClose</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter &amp; Close</code></td>\n<td>button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterAndClose.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply this filter to the grid and close the dialog</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter Only</code></td>\n<td>button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.filterOnly.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Apply this filter to the grid but keep the dialog open, so you can keep adjusting it</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.closeButton</code></td>\n<td><code>Close</code></td>\n<td>button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.closeButton.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Close the dialog and leave the current filter as it is</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.fieldMenu</code></td>\n<td><code>Available Fields:</code></td>\n<td>section header of the menu of fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.fieldMenu.search</code></td>\n<td><code>search</code></td>\n<td>the placeholder of its search box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operatorMenu</code></td>\n<td><code>Operator:</code></td>\n<td>section header of the menu of operators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The names of the operators, in the operator menu and on a condition:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>Equal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>Less Than Or Equal To</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater Than Or Equal To</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>Less Than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>Greater Than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>Contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>Not Equal To</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>Does Not Contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>Begin With</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>End With</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.IN</code></td>\n<td><code>In</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td><code>Not In</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>Is Blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterEditor.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>Is Not Blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-footer\">Footer</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>footer.rowCount</code></td>\n<td><code>{shown}/{total}</code></td>\n<td><code>{shown}</code>: the rows shown, <code>{total}</code>: the rows there are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>✕ Clear</code></td>\n<td>the button that removes the filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.clear.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear filter</code></td>\n<td>its tooltip (<code>title</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.filter.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Edit filter</code></td>\n<td>tooltip (<code>title</code>) of the chips, a click opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.collapsed.one</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} filter — click to edit</code></td>\n<td><code>{count}</code>: the chips do not fit, one condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.collapsed.other</code></td>\n<td><code>{count} filters — click to edit</code></td>\n<td><code>{count}</code>: the chips do not fit, or there are more than 5 conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.and</code></td>\n<td><code>AND</code></td>\n<td>between the chips of an AND group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.or</code></td>\n<td><code>OR</code></td>\n<td>between the chips of an OR group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.changedOnly</code></td>\n<td><code>Changed rows only</code></td>\n<td>the chip in front of the filter&#39;s while only the changed rows are shown (<code>datasource.filterChangedOnly()</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The short names of the operators, on a chip:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>=</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≤</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≥</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>&lt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>≠</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>does not contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>starts with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>ends with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.IN</code></td>\n<td><code>in</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td><code>not in</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>is blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>footer.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>is not blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-aggregates\">Aggregates</h3>\n<p>The aggregates of the columns (<code>ColumnConfig.aggregate</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>. A\nscreen reader reads the label with the value, the column&#39;s header with them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.sum</code></td>\n<td><code>Sum</code></td>\n<td>in front of a sum, in a group row and the totals row: <code>Sum 1234.5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.avg</code></td>\n<td><code>Avg</code></td>\n<td>in front of an average</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Min</code></td>\n<td>in front of the smallest value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Max</code></td>\n<td>in front of the largest value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.count</code></td>\n<td><code>Count</code></td>\n<td>in front of a count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aggregate.total</code></td>\n<td><code>Total</code></td>\n<td>the label of the totals row, at its left under the row numbers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-header-filters\">Header filters</h3>\n<p>What an empty header filter shows, when its column has no <code>placeholderFilter</code> of its own. A date\nfilter shows its formatter&#39;s format (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>), a boolean filter is a checkbox.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filter.placeholder.text</code></td>\n<td><code>Text</code></td>\n<td>an empty header filter of a text field</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filter.placeholder.number</code></td>\n<td><code>Number</code></td>\n<td>an empty header filter of a number field</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-screen-readers\">Screen readers</h3>\n<p>Labels (<code>aria-label</code>) that are read out, not shown. The <a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">keyboard and accessibility\nguide</a> has what a screen reader gets.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>grid.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Grid</code></td>\n<td>the name of the grid. Give each grid its own in its config: <code>texts: { &#39;grid.label&#39;: &#39;Orders&#39; }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.rowSelector</code></td>\n<td><code>row selector</code></td>\n<td>the column header of the row numbers, the top left corner</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter {label}</code></td>\n<td>a header filter. <code>{label}</code>: the column&#39;s label</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.groupToggle</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} ({total})</code></td>\n<td>the expand/collapse button of a group row, which also says whether it is expanded. <code>{name}</code>: the group&#39;s name, <code>Country: Norway</code>; <code>{total}</code>: its number of rows. The row shows <code>{name} ({total})</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.removeGroup</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove the grouping on {title}</code></td>\n<td>the x on a group chip in the panel above the grid. <code>{title}</code>: the title of the grouping</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-row-state-and-edited-cells\">Row state and edited cells</h3>\n<p>The state of a row on its row number (<code>GridConfig.rowStatus</code>) and the marker of an edited cell\n(<code>GridConfig.editedMarker</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Editing</a>. The state is the\ntooltip&#39;s first line and the text a screen reader reads with the row number.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.new</code></td>\n<td><code>New row</code></td>\n<td>a new row: the tooltip of its row number, and for a screen reader</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.edited</code></td>\n<td><code>Edited</code></td>\n<td>an edited row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.deleted</code></td>\n<td><code>Marked for deletion</code></td>\n<td>a row marked for deletion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rowStatus.error</code></td>\n<td><code>Error: {errors}</code></td>\n<td>a row with an error, for a screen reader (the tooltip has the errors, a line each). <code>{errors}</code>: the server&#39;s error and the fields&#39; errors, <code>Age: Must be at least 18</code>, joined with <code>, </code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.editedMarker.tooltip</code></td>\n<td><code>Original value: {value}</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip of the marker of an edited cell. <code>{value}</code>: the value before the edit, shown with the field&#39;s formatter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.editedMarker.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>(blank)</code></td>\n<td>the <code>{value}</code> of an original value that is blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.editedMarker.revert</code></td>\n<td><code>Click to revert</code></td>\n<td>the tooltip&#39;s last line, in an editable grid: a click on the marker puts the original value back</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-multiline-cells\">Multiline cells</h3>\n<p>A cell of several lines (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>, or a value with a line break) and the editor that\nopens over it, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Editing</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.more</code></td>\n<td><code>More lines, Enter shows all</code></td>\n<td>for a screen reader: the description of a cell that shows the first of its lines. Hidden on screen, the cell has a marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.hint</code></td>\n<td><code>Ctrl+Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code></td>\n<td>under the textarea of the editor. Cmd+Enter saves too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cell.multiline.hintReadonly</code></td>\n<td><code>Esc to close</code></td>\n<td>under the textarea of the editor of a readonly cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-date-picker\">Date picker</h3>\n<p>Of a date cell and a date header filter, and a <code>DateInterface</code> of your own.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.hours</code></td>\n<td><code>HH</code></td>\n<td>label over the hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.minutes</code></td>\n<td><code>MM</code></td>\n<td>label over the minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.week</code></td>\n<td><code>W</code></td>\n<td>header of the week numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.clear</code></td>\n<td><code>Clear</code></td>\n<td>button, empties the date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.now</code></td>\n<td><code>Now</code></td>\n<td>button, goes to now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.use</code></td>\n<td><code>Use</code></td>\n<td>button, picks the date</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.january</code></td>\n<td><code>January</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.february</code></td>\n<td><code>February</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.march</code></td>\n<td><code>March</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.april</code></td>\n<td><code>April</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.may</code></td>\n<td><code>May</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.june</code></td>\n<td><code>June</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.july</code></td>\n<td><code>July</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.august</code></td>\n<td><code>August</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.september</code></td>\n<td><code>September</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.october</code></td>\n<td><code>October</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.november</code></td>\n<td><code>November</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.month.december</code></td>\n<td><code>December</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.sunday</code></td>\n<td><code>Su</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.monday</code></td>\n<td><code>Mo</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.tuesday</code></td>\n<td><code>Tu</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.wednesday</code></td>\n<td><code>We</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.thursday</code></td>\n<td><code>Th</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.friday</code></td>\n<td><code>Fr</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datePicker.day.saturday</code></td>\n<td><code>Sa</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-validation-messages\">Validation messages</h3>\n<p>Made by the datasource: <code>setTexts()</code> only. <code>{value}</code> is the rule&#39;s <code>value</code>, and every option of the\nrule can be in a message by its name.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>rule</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>validation.required</code></td>\n<td><code>Required</code></td>\n<td><code>required</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.min</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value}</code></td>\n<td><code>min</code>, <code>{value}</code> is the rule&#39;s value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.max</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value}</code></td>\n<td><code>max</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.minLength</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at least {value} characters</code></td>\n<td><code>minLength</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.maxLength</code></td>\n<td><code>Must be at most {value} characters</code></td>\n<td><code>maxLength</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>validation.pattern</code></td>\n<td><code>Not in the expected format</code></td>\n<td><code>pattern</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-getfilterstring\">getFilterString()</h3>\n<p>Made by the datasource: <code>setTexts()</code> only. The whole string is upper cased:\n<code>&#39;filterString.operator.EQUAL&#39;: &#39;equal_to&#39;</code> is shown as <code>&lt;&lt;EQUAL_TO&gt;&gt;</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterString.and</code></td>\n<td><code>AND</code></td>\n<td>joins the conditions of an AND group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.or</code></td>\n<td><code>OR</code></td>\n<td>joins the conditions of an OR group</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.EQUAL</code></td>\n<td><code>equal_to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.LESS_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>less_or_equal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>greater_than_or_equal</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.LESS_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>less_than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.GREATER_THAN</code></td>\n<td><code>greater_than</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.CONTAINS</code></td>\n<td><code>contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.NOT_EQUAL_TO</code></td>\n<td><code>not_equal_to</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.DOES_NOT_CONTAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>does_not_contain</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.BEGIN_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>start_with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.END_WITH</code></td>\n<td><code>end_with</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.IN</code></td>\n<td><code>IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.NOT_IN</code></td>\n<td><code>NOT_IN</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.IS_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>IS_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>filterString.operator.IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n<td><code>IS_NOT_BLANK</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-grouping\">Grouping</h3>\n<p>Made by the datasource: <code>setTexts()</code> only.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>shown</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>grouping.blank</code></td>\n<td><code>blank</code></td>\n<td>in <code>&#39;Country: blank&#39;</code>, the name of the group of the empty values</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"},{"id":"theming","title":"Theming","html":"<p>Every colour of the grid is a css variable: the grid itself, its menus, the column chooser, the\ntooltips, the filter editor and the date picker. Each variable has a light and a dark colour, and the\ngrid follows the <code>color-scheme</code> of the page: dark mode is one line of css.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"theming\"></div>\n<p>The variables of the grid are set on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> in <code>grid.css</code>, the ones of the date picker\non <code>.simple-html-date</code> in <code>date.css</code>. The tables at the end list them all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-a-theme-of-your-own\">A theme of your own</h2>\n<p>Set the variables you want to change on <code>.simple-html-grid</code>, in a css file loaded after <code>grid.css</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-odd: light-dark(#b9e4dc, #0f5c52);\n    --simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-even: light-dark(#a7dbd1, #11685d);\n    --simple-html-grid-focus-color: light-dark(#b45309, #fbbf24);\n    --simple-html-grid-font-size: 12px;\n}</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>light-dark(&lt;light&gt;, &lt;dark&gt;)</code> gives a colour for each, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/dark-mode\">Dark mode</a>. A plain colour\n(<code>#b9e4dc</code>) is used in both.</li>\n<li>Same selector, so the rule that comes last wins: load your css after <code>grid.css</code>, or make your\nselector stronger (<code>.simple-html-grid.simple-html-grid</code>).</li>\n<li>The menus, the column chooser, the tooltips and the filter editor&#39;s dialog are not inside the grid&#39;s\nelement: the grid puts them on the body (or in the dialog or popover the grid is in). They are\nelements with the <code>simple-html-grid</code> class too, so a rule on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> reaches them.\nA rule on the grid&#39;s container (<code>.my-page .simple-html-grid</code>) only reaches the grid itself, its\nmenus keep the default colours.</li>\n<li>The grid&#39;s date picker is a <code>.simple-html-date</code> in a menu: set its own variables, see\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-date-picker\">The date picker</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What the classes and parts of the grid are, if you want to restyle more than the colours: read\n<code>grid.css</code>, it is commented.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-dark-mode\">Dark mode</h2>\n<p>Each colour is set with the css function <code>light-dark()</code>: the first colour when the element&#39;s\n<code>color-scheme</code> is light, the second when it is dark. <code>color-scheme</code> is inherited, so it is set once\nfor the page:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* always dark */\n:root {\n    color-scheme: dark;\n}\n\n/* dark when the page has a dark class */\nbody.dark {\n    color-scheme: dark;\n}\n\n/* follow the setting of the OS / browser */\n:root {\n    color-scheme: light dark;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>&lt;meta name=&quot;color-scheme&quot; content=&quot;light dark&quot;&gt;</code> in the page&#39;s head does the same as the last one.\nWithout any of these the <code>color-scheme</code> is <code>normal</code>, and the grid is light: the colours it had before\nv6.</p>\n<p>Set it on <code>:root</code> or the body, not on the grid&#39;s container. The menus, the column chooser, the\ntooltips and the filter editor are put on the body (or in the dialog or popover the grid is in): set\non the container only, the grid would be dark and its menus light.</p>\n<p>The browser&#39;s own parts follow <code>color-scheme</code> on their own: the checkboxes, the number inputs of the\ndate picker, scrollbars. The grid&#39;s scrollbars have their colours from the grid&#39;s variables.</p>\n<p><code>light-dark()</code> is in Chrome and Edge 123, Firefox 120 and Safari 17.5 (May 2024). In an older browser\na colour set with it is not valid: the grid has no backgrounds and the text colour of the page. To\nsupport one, set the variables with plain colours for it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>@supports not (color: light-dark(white, black)) {\n    .simple-html-grid {\n        --simple-html-grid-main-bg-color: #f5f5f5;\n        /* ... the light colour of every variable in the table below */\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keyboard-focus\">The keyboard focus</h2>\n<p>The element that has the keyboard focus gets an outline of 2px in <code>--simple-html-grid-focus-color</code>:\na cell, a filter input, a checkbox, a menu item, a button of the filter editor, the date picker. The\noutline is inside the element (<code>outline-offset: -2px</code>): the cells are packed edge to edge, an outline\noutside the cell would be painted over by the next one or cut off at the edge of the grid. A checkbox\nin a cell has it on the cell.</p>\n<p>It is drawn for <code>:focus-visible</code>, so the browser decides when it shows: when you move with the\nkeyboard, and in an input you type in; not when you click a checkbox or a button. It replaces the\ndashed border a focused input had before v6.</p>\n<p>To change more than the colour, override the rule:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid :focus-visible {\n    outline-width: 3px;\n    outline-offset: -3px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The date picker draws the same outline in <code>--simple-html-date-focus-color</code>. In the grid it takes the\ngrid&#39;s colour: <code>grid.css</code> sets <code>--simple-html-date-focus-color</code> to <code>--simple-html-grid-focus-color</code> on\n<code>.simple-html-grid .simple-html-date</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-row-state-and-errors\">The row state and errors</h2>\n<p>The grid shows the state of each row on its row number and marks the edited cells (<code>rowStatus</code> and\n<code>editedMarker</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/showing-the-row-state\">Showing the row state</a>), in these\nvariables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-new-color</code> - the plus of a new row</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-edited-color</code> - the pencil of an edited row, the marker of an edited cell</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-deleted-color</code> - the trash can of a row marked for deletion</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code> and <code>--simple-html-grid-error-bg-color</code> - a cell and a row with an\nerror: the border of the cell, the bar and the exclamation mark of the row number, the tint, see\n<a href=\"#guide/editing/how-the-grid-shows-the-errors\">How the grid shows the errors</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The icons and the marker are graphics: the new, edited and deleted colours have a contrast of at least\n3:1 to the rows, the row numbers and the selected rows, in light and in dark, also under an error&#39;s\ntint (WCAG 1.4.11). Keep that when you change them. Each state has a shape of its own too, the colour is\nnot the only thing that tells them apart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-multiline-text\">Multiline text</h2>\n<p>A cell of several lines (see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>) has a marker at its\nright, lines of text, in <code>--simple-html-grid-multiline-color</code> - at least 3:1 to the rows and the\nselected rows too. The editor that opens over the cell uses the colours of the rest: the textarea is\n<code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code> with <code>--simple-html-grid-main-font-color</code>, its hint\n<code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-color</code>, its frame <code>--simple-html-grid-pinned-border</code> and its shadow\n<code>--simple-html-grid-dialog-shadow-color</code>. The editor is on the body (or in the dialog the grid is in),\nlike the menus: a theme set on <code>.simple-html-grid</code> reaches it, one set on the grid&#39;s container does not.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-edited-color: light-dark(#1d4ed8, #93c5fd);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes are there to restyle more: <code>row-is-new</code>, <code>row-is-modified</code> and <code>row-is-deleted</code> on the\nelement around the row number, <code>simple-html-row-status-icon</code> on the icon (with\n<code>simple-html-row-status-new</code>, <code>-edited</code>, <code>-deleted</code> or <code>-error</code>), <code>simple-html-cell-edited-marker</code> on the\nmarker. With <code>rowStatus: false</code> the classes <code>row-is-*</code> are still there for a look of your own:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.row-is-modified .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#4f46e5, #a5b4fc);\n}\n\n.row-is-new .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#16a34a, #86efac);\n}\n\n.row-is-deleted .simple-html-selector-text {\n    color: light-dark(#dc2626, #fca5a5);\n    text-decoration: line-through;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</h2>\n<p>A range of cells the user selected (see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>) is\na tint over its cells and a 2px line around it, in two variables:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-range-bg-color</code> - laid over the cells, under their text: over the colour of\nthe row, a selected row&#39;s and an error&#39;s tint. A colour with alpha, so those still show through</li>\n<li><code>--simple-html-grid-range-border-color</code> - the line on the outer sides of the range</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The defaults keep the text at 4.5:1 or more over the tint, and the line at 3:1 or more against the\nrows, the selected rows and an error&#39;s tint, in light and in dark (WCAG 1.4.3 and 1.4.11). Keep that\nwhen you change them.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>.simple-html-grid {\n    --simple-html-grid-range-bg-color: light-dark(rgba(22, 163, 74, 0.12), rgba(74, 222, 128, 0.18));\n    --simple-html-grid-range-border-color: light-dark(#166534, #bbf7d0);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The classes: <code>simple-html-cell-in-range</code> on every cell in a range, and <code>simple-html-cell-range-top</code>,\n<code>-bottom</code>, <code>-left</code> and <code>-right</code> on the cells whose side is an edge of the ranges. The line is a\n<code>::after</code> of the cell, so a rule of your own can restyle it:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>/* a dashed line, as a cut in a spreadsheet */\n.simple-html-grid .simple-html-cell-in-range::after {\n    border-style: dashed;\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-variables-of-the-grid\">The variables of the grid</h2>\n<p>On <code>.simple-html-grid</code>, in <code>grid.css</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>variable</th>\n<th>colours</th>\n<th>light</th>\n<th>dark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the labels, the panel, the footer, the row selector, group rows, the menus, the filter editor</td>\n<td><code>#f5f5f5</code></td>\n<td><code>#374151</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the filter row, the inputs and buttons of the filter editor, the filter chips, the tooltips</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-alt-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>scrollbar tracks, the search input of the column chooser, the focus button of a cell</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-even</code></td>\n<td>the even rows</td>\n<td><code>#ffffff</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-odd</code></td>\n<td>the odd rows</td>\n<td><code>#f7f7f7</code></td>\n<td><code>#5a6370</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-odd</code></td>\n<td>selected rows (odd), the number of a selected column, the selected menu item, scrollbar thumbs</td>\n<td><code>#bdd4e7</code></td>\n<td><code>#234882</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-selected-even</code></td>\n<td>selected rows (even), menu items and buttons under the pointer</td>\n<td><code>#afc7db</code></td>\n<td><code>#274e8f</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-bg-border</code></td>\n<td>the lines between cells, rows and menu sections, the frame of the grid and the menus</td>\n<td><code>#e6e5e5</code></td>\n<td><code>#1f2937</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-pinned-border</code></td>\n<td>the lines at the pinned columns and under the header, the frame of the filter editor, the tooltips and the buttons</td>\n<td><code>#d1d0d0</code></td>\n<td><code>#1f2937</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-bg-border</code></td>\n<td>the line right of the grouping columns</td>\n<td><code>#e6e5e5</code></td>\n<td><code>#1f2937</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-main-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>#2e2d2d</code></td>\n<td><code>#f9f7f7</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-sec-font-color</code></td>\n<td>placeholders, the AND/OR between the filter chips, the &quot;too many conditions&quot; box</td>\n<td><code>#979494</code></td>\n<td><code>#c8c8c8</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-boxshadow</code></td>\n<td>the inset shadow of the labels, the panel and the row selector</td>\n<td><code>#fffdfd</code></td>\n<td><code>#4b5563</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-dropzone-color</code></td>\n<td>where a dragged column can go, the resize handle under the pointer</td>\n<td><code>rgba(129, 127, 127, 0.7)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(151, 148, 148, 0.4)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-focus-color</code></td>\n<td>the outline of the element with the keyboard focus</td>\n<td><code>#005fcc</code></td>\n<td><code>#b3d7ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-range-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>laid over the cells of a range of cells, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</a></td>\n<td><code>rgba(37, 99, 235, 0.12)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.2)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-range-border-color</code></td>\n<td>the line around a range of cells</td>\n<td><code>#1e40af</code></td>\n<td><code>#dbeafe</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-error-color</code></td>\n<td>a validation error: the border of the cell, the bar and the exclamation mark of the row selector</td>\n<td><code>#dc2626</code></td>\n<td><code>#fca5a5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-error-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>a validation error: laid over the cell and the row selector</td>\n<td><code>rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.12)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.22)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-edited-color</code></td>\n<td>the marker of an edited cell, the pencil of an edited row (<code>editedMarker</code>, <code>rowStatus</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#9a3412</code></td>\n<td><code>#fdba74</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-new-color</code></td>\n<td>the plus of a new row (<code>rowStatus</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#166534</code></td>\n<td><code>#86efac</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-deleted-color</code></td>\n<td>the trash can of a row marked for deletion (<code>rowStatus</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#57534e</code></td>\n<td><code>#d1d5db</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-multiline-color</code></td>\n<td>the marker of a cell with more lines than it shows (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>)</td>\n<td><code>#475569</code></td>\n<td><code>#e2e8f0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-totals-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the totals row under the rows (<code>totalsRow</code>), see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a></td>\n<td><code>#e8edf3</code></td>\n<td><code>#2a3441</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-totals-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the text of the totals row</td>\n<td><code>#1f2328</code></td>\n<td><code>#f9fafb</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-aggregate-label-color</code></td>\n<td>the label in front of an aggregate (<code>Sum</code>) in a group row and the totals row. At least 4.5:1 to both</td>\n<td><code>#57606a</code></td>\n<td><code>#c9d1d9</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-warning-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the &quot;too many values&quot; warning of the filter menu</td>\n<td><code>red</code></td>\n<td><code>#fca5a5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-danger-font-color</code></td>\n<td>the footer&#39;s Clear filter button under the pointer: its text</td>\n<td><code>#c0392b</code></td>\n<td><code>#fecaca</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-danger-border</code></td>\n<td>the footer&#39;s Clear filter button under the pointer: its border</td>\n<td><code>#d98282</code></td>\n<td><code>#f87171</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-readonly-stripe-color</code></td>\n<td>the red stripes of a readonly cell in an editable grid (<code>.simple-html-readonly</code>)</td>\n<td><code>rgb(255, 0, 0)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgb(255, 0, 0)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-required-stripe-color</code></td>\n<td>the blue stripes of an empty required cell (<code>.simple-html-mandatory</code>)</td>\n<td><code>rgb(0, 8, 255)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgb(120, 140, 255)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-stripe-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>between the stripes</td>\n<td><code>rgb(255, 255, 255)</code></td>\n<td><code>transparent</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-menu-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow of the menus</td>\n<td><code>black</code></td>\n<td><code>black</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-dialog-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow of the filter editor</td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.32)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-tooltip-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow of the tooltips</td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.28)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-scrollbar-shadow-color</code></td>\n<td>the shadow in a scrollbar thumb (Safari, the other browsers use <code>scrollbar-color</code>)</td>\n<td><code>rgb(0 0 0 / 30%)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgb(0 0 0 / 30%)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-backdrop-color</code></td>\n<td>the layer over the page behind the filter editor</td>\n<td><code>rgba(129, 127, 127, 0.2)</code></td>\n<td><code>rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>--simple-html-grid-grouping-border</code> and <code>--simple-html-grid-main-hr-border</code> are still set, but\nnothing in <code>grid.css</code> uses them.</p>\n<p>The stripes are a pattern drawn at 20% opacity over the cell, so their colours show much lighter\nthan they are.</p>\n<p>The fonts:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>variable</th>\n<th>for</th>\n<th>default</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-family</code></td>\n<td>all text</td>\n<td><code>&#39;Roboto&#39;, &#39;Helvetica&#39;, &#39;Arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-size</code></td>\n<td>all text</td>\n<td><code>11px</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight</code></td>\n<td>the cells, the inputs</td>\n<td><code>500</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight-header</code></td>\n<td>the labels, the menu sections, group rows, buttons</td>\n<td><code>700</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight-cell</code></td>\n<td>the menu items, the filter editor&#39;s title and captions</td>\n<td><code>--simple-html-grid-font-weight</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-date-picker\">The date picker</h2>\n<p>On <code>.simple-html-date</code>, in <code>date.css</code>: the grid&#39;s date picker, and the date picker or calendar on\nits own (<code>&lt;simple-html-date&gt;</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>variable</th>\n<th>colours</th>\n<th>light</th>\n<th>dark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-bg-color</code></td>\n<td>the background, the buttons, the hour and minute inputs</td>\n<td><code>white</code></td>\n<td><code>#374151</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-color</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>#2e2d2d</code></td>\n<td><code>#f9f7f7</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-dimmed-color</code></td>\n<td>the days of the months before and after</td>\n<td><code>#dfdfdf</code></td>\n<td><code>#979494</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-week-color</code></td>\n<td>the week numbers, the hour and minute labels</td>\n<td><code>#8b8b8b</code></td>\n<td><code>#b4b4b4</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-bg-border</code></td>\n<td>the lines, the frame of the buttons, the selected day and the day under the pointer</td>\n<td><code>#e6e5e5</code></td>\n<td><code>#979494</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-main-bg-selected</code></td>\n<td>the selected day, a button under the pointer</td>\n<td><code>#bdd4e7</code></td>\n<td><code>#234882</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-focus-color</code></td>\n<td>the outline of the element with the keyboard focus (in the grid: the grid&#39;s)</td>\n<td><code>#005fcc</code></td>\n<td><code>#b3d7ff</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-font-size</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>11px</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--simple-html-date-font-family</code></td>\n<td>the text</td>\n<td><code>&#39;Roboto&#39;, &#39;Helvetica&#39;, &#39;Arial&#39;, &#39;sans-serif&#39;</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>--simple-html-date-header-bg-border</code> is still set, but nothing in <code>date.css</code> uses it.</p>\n"},{"id":"accessibility","title":"Keyboard and accessibility","html":"<p>The grid follows the WAI-ARIA <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/grid/\">grid pattern</a>: a\nscreen reader gets one grid of rows and cells, with column headers, where each row and column is in\nthe whole grid, how the rows are sorted and which are selected. Grouped, it is a\n<a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/treegrid/\">treegrid</a>: the group rows expand and collapse.\nThis page has what the grid exposes, every key, and what it does not do (yet).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-screen-reader-gets\">What a screen reader gets</h2>\n<p>This is Chrome&#39;s accessibility tree of the column layout sample (a pinned column on each side, a\ncolumn of two fields), scrolled down and to the right: columns 3 and 4 are scrolled away, and the\nnumbers in parentheses are the <code>aria-colindex</code>es.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>grid &quot;Grid&quot;  aria-rowcount=502  aria-colcount=10\n  row  aria-rowindex=1\n    columnheader &quot;row selector&quot; (1), &quot;Company&quot; (2), &quot;Phone&quot; (5), &quot;date&quot; (6), &quot;Age&quot; (7) ... &quot;Balance&quot; (10)\n  row  aria-rowindex=2\n    gridcell (2) &gt; textbox &quot;Filter Company&quot;, gridcell (5) &gt; textbox &quot;Filter Phone&quot; ...\n  row  aria-rowindex=71  selected=false\n    gridcell &quot;69&quot; (1), gridcell (2) &gt; textbox &quot;Company&quot;: MASSIVE DYNAMIC,\n    gridcell (5) &gt; textbox &quot;Phone&quot;: +47 45911795 ... gridcell (10) &gt; textbox &quot;Balance&quot;: 2178.34</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The grid</strong> has <code>role=&quot;grid&quot;</code>, <code>role=&quot;treegrid&quot;</code> while the rows are grouped. Its name is the text\n<code>grid.label</code> (see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/labels-and-translating\">Labels</a>). <code>aria-rowcount</code> is the number of header rows\nplus the displayed rows (after the filter, the group rows that are shown included), <code>aria-colcount</code>\nthe row numbers plus every field of the layout. <code>aria-multiselectable=&quot;true&quot;</code> with the selection\nmode <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The header rows</strong>: the labels are <code>columnheader</code>s, the header filters are <code>gridcell</code>s with the\nfilter input in them. The top left corner is the column header of the row numbers. The column\nnumbers under the filters (a click selects a column) are hidden from a screen reader: they are for\nthe mouse. A hidden row of labels or filters (<code>hideLabels</code>, <code>hideFilter</code>) is not counted.</li>\n<li><strong>The column header of the sorted column</strong> has <code>aria-sort=&quot;ascending&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;descending&quot;</code>. Sorted on\nmore columns, only the first one has it: a grid has one sorted column for a screen reader.</li>\n<li><strong>A row</strong> has <code>aria-rowindex</code>: where it is in the whole grid, the header rows included - the first\ndata row is 3 under a header of labels and filters. With a selection mode it has <code>aria-selected</code>,\n<code>true</code> or <code>false</code>; with <code>&#39;none&#39;</code> it has none. A group row can not be selected.</li>\n<li><strong>A cell</strong> is a <code>gridcell</code> with <code>aria-colindex</code>; the input (or checkbox) in it is named with the\ncolumn&#39;s label, the same as the header shows. The row number is the first cell of a row. A column of\ntwo fields (<code>fields: [&#39;a&#39;, &#39;b&#39;]</code>) is two columns here, one per field.</li>\n<li><strong>A cell in a range of cells</strong> (see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>) has\n<code>aria-selected=&quot;true&quot;</code>: <code>gridcell &quot;Company&quot; selected</code>. The other cells have no <code>aria-selected</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/known-limits\">Known limits</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The state of a row</strong> is text in its row number cell, hidden on screen (<code>simple-html-visually-hidden</code>):\n<code>gridcell &quot;3 Marked for deletion&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;2 Edited, Error: Age: Must be at least 18&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;5 New row&quot;</code>. A\nrow as it was is its number only. The icons on screen are hidden from a screen reader, the text says\nthe same - the texts <code>rowStatus.*</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/texts/row-state-and-edited-cells\">Texts</a>. Without the\nrow status (<code>rowStatus: false</code>) the row number is the number only: show the state another way, a\ncolumn of <code>__rowState</code> say.</li>\n<li><strong>An edited cell</strong>: its marker is hidden from a screen reader and is no stop of Tab; the row&#39;s state\nsays the row is edited. Ctrl+Z takes an edit back, the row menu&#39;s <em>Undo cell edit</em> and a click on the\nmarker are for the mouse.</li>\n<li><strong>A cell of several lines</strong> (<code>FieldConfig.multiline</code>, or a value with a line break) is a readonly\ntextbox with its first line as the value. When it has more lines, its description\n(<code>aria-describedby</code>) says so: <code>textbox &quot;Notes&quot; readonly: &quot;Call back on Monday&quot;</code>, described &quot;More\nlines, Enter shows all&quot; (the text <code>cell.multiline.more</code>). The marker on screen is hidden from a\nscreen reader. Enter opens the editor, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-editor-of-a-multiline-cell\">The editor of a multiline cell</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The state is not colour only</strong> (WCAG 1.4.1): each state has a shape of its own - a plus, a pencil,\na trash can, an exclamation mark - and a row marked for deletion is struck through.</li>\n<li><strong>A group row</strong>, grouped, is a row with <code>aria-level</code> (1 for the first grouping) and\n<code>aria-expanded</code>; a data row is one level below the last grouping. Its one cell spans the columns\nafter the row number, with a button that expands and collapses it: named with the text the row\nshows (<code>Country: Norway (12)</code>), <code>aria-expanded</code> says its state.</li>\n<li><strong>The aggregates of a group row</strong> (<code>ColumnConfig.aggregate</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>):\neach is a <code>gridcell</code> with the <code>aria-colindex</code> of its field, <code>Sum 1234.5</code>, so a screen reader reads it\nwith the column&#39;s header. The name cell then spans the columns up to the first aggregate. The\naggregate of the first column is under the name on screen: it is not a cell of its own, its text is\nin the name cell (<code>Country: Norway (12) Count 10</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>The totals row</strong> (<code>totalsRow</code>) is the last row of the grid, after the rows on screen: its first\ncell is a <code>rowheader</code>, &quot;Total&quot; (the text <code>aggregate.total</code>), then a <code>gridcell</code> per aggregate.\n<code>aria-rowcount</code> counts it, its <code>aria-rowindex</code> is the last.</li>\n<li><strong>The row count</strong> in the footer is a status (<code>role=&quot;status&quot;</code>): a screen reader reads it out when it\nchanges - after a filter, an expand or a collapse.</li>\n<li><strong>The panel</strong>: the x of a group chip is a button, &quot;Remove the grouping on Country&quot;.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rows and the columns are virtual: only the rows on screen (and a few more) are in the dom, and\nof the middle section only the columns on screen. That is what <code>aria-rowindex</code> and <code>aria-colindex</code>\nare for, the screen reader knows where it is without having every row.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-how\">How</h3>\n<p>A row of the grid is not one element in the dom. The grid has a section for the row numbers, the\npinned left columns, the middle columns that scroll sideways and the pinned right columns, and a row\nhas an element in each. The row and column elements are reused while scrolling, so their order in the\ndom is not the order of the rows and columns either.</p>\n<p><code>aria-owns</code> gives a screen reader the right shape without moving anything: an element owns other\nelements, which become its children in the accessibility tree, in the order of the list. The grid is\nan element of its own: its children are the header rows, it owns the rows in the order of the rows. A\nrow is the row element of the row numbers: it owns the group row&#39;s cell and the column elements of\nthe left, middle and right sections, in the order of the columns. The other row and column elements\nare <code>role=&quot;none&quot;</code>. The grid updates the lists after every scroll. The elements aria-owns points to\nhave ids of the grid&#39;s own, <code>simple-html-grid-&lt;n&gt;-...</code>, one <code>&lt;n&gt;</code> per grid on the page.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-keyboard\">The keyboard</h2>\n<p>Every key the grid handles, by where the focus is. The cells are inputs, the arrow keys move the\ncaret in them; Alt with an arrow moves between the cells.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-cell\">A cell</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the next / previous cell, across the sections, then the next / previous row. Group rows are skipped. Shift+Tab in the first row goes up to the header filters. Tab on the last cell of the last row, and Shift+Tab on the first cell with the filters hidden, leave the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowLeft / Alt+ArrowRight</td>\n<td>the cell to the left / right, across the sections. Past the end of a row: the next row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the cell above / below: the next field of a column of two fields, then the next row. Up from the first row: the header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>in a text cell that can be edited: the cell below (Alt+ArrowDown). In a multiline cell: opens its editor, also when the cell is readonly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F2</td>\n<td>in a multiline cell: opens its editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a character</td>\n<td>in a multiline cell that can be edited: opens its editor, the character at the end of the text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Space</td>\n<td>in a cell with a <code>focusButton</code>: sends <code>cell-focus-button-click</code>, as a click on the button does. In a checkbox: checks it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the row menu (the browser sends <code>contextmenu</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td>a range of cells: from this cell, the end one cell further each time - a row up or down, a field left or right (the fields of a column of two are two). The focus stays in the cell. See <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-keyboard\">Selection and clipboard</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>clears the ranges of cells (when there are some, and no menu is open)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C, Cmd+C</td>\n<td>with no text selected in the cell: the range of cells as tab separated text, or the cell&#39;s value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+V, Cmd+V</td>\n<td>a block of cells (copied in a spreadsheet, or here) from this cell or the top left of the range; one value into every cell of the ranges. One value with no range is the input&#39;s own paste</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete / Backspace</td>\n<td>with a range of more than one cell and no text selected: empties its cells. Else the input&#39;s own</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The grid scrolls to the cell the focus goes to. Moving to another cell (Tab, Alt+arrows, Enter) clears\nthe ranges of cells, and so does typing in a cell.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-header-filter\">A header filter</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>the next / previous filter. Tab on the last one goes to the first row, Shift+Tab on the first one leaves the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowLeft / Alt+ArrowRight</td>\n<td>the filter to the left / right</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp</td>\n<td>the filter above in a column of two fields, else the column header (label) above it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the filter below in a column of two fields, else the first row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the filter menu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-a-column-header\">A column header</h3>\n<p>A column header can have the focus, but is not a stop of Tab: Alt+ArrowUp from its filter gets there\n(a click too).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>sorts on the column, again for the other direction - as a click. The focus stays on it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+Enter / Shift+Space</td>\n<td>adds the column to the sort - as shift+click</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown</td>\n<td>the label above / below in a column of two fields; down from the last one, the column&#39;s filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+F10, the menu key</td>\n<td>the header menu</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-rest\">The rest</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>where</th>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>the button of a group row</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>expands or collapses the group. The focus stays on the button</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the x of a group chip in the panel</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>removes the grouping. It is in the tab order, before the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filter chips in the footer</td>\n<td>Enter / Space</td>\n<td>opens the filter editor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a menu</td>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes it, the focus goes back where it was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the editor of a multiline cell</td>\n<td>Esc / Ctrl+Enter / Tab</td>\n<td>see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-editor-of-a-multiline-cell\">below</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the filter editor</td>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes it without filtering (a modal dialog)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-editor-of-a-multiline-cell\">The editor of a multiline cell</h3>\n<p>A textarea in a popover over the cell, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>. It gets the\nfocus when it opens: a <code>textbox</code> named with the column&#39;s label (<code>Notes</code>), multi-line, described by the\nhint under it (<code>Ctrl+Enter to save, Esc to cancel</code>, <code>Esc to close</code> when it is readonly - the texts\n<code>cell.multiline.hint</code> and <code>cell.multiline.hintReadonly</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Enter</td>\n<td>a new line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+Enter, Cmd+Enter</td>\n<td>saves and closes it, the focus goes back to the cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>closes it without saving, the focus goes back to the cell. In a modal dialog of your own, the dialog stays open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tab / Shift+Tab</td>\n<td>saves and closes it, and goes on to the next / previous cell as Tab in the cell does</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The keys of an input method (IME) are its own while it composes: Enter and Esc go to it. The other\nkeys are the textarea&#39;s own: the arrows move the caret, Ctrl+Z is the textarea&#39;s undo while it is open.\nA readonly editor (a readonly grid or cell) shows the text, nothing can be typed in it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-focus\">Focus</h2>\n<p>What can have the focus: the cell inputs and checkboxes, the header filters, the column headers, the\nbuttons of the group rows, the x of the group chips and the filter chips in the footer. Each gets a\nvisible focus ring from the theme (<code>:focus-visible</code>, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/the-keyboard-focus\">Theming</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sorting from the keyboard and expanding a group rebuild the header or the rows: the focus is put\nback on the new column header / group button.</li>\n<li>At the ends of the grid Tab is left to the browser: the focus first goes to an empty element at the\nstart or the end of the grid (<code>tabindex=&quot;-1&quot;</code>, hidden from a screen reader), and the browser&#39;s Tab\ngoes on from there to what is before or after the grid.</li>\n<li>The wheel and a swipe take the focus from the cell being edited: its row element is about to show\nanother row. The grid uses a hidden input for it, out of the tab order. They save and close the\neditor of a multiline cell too, and a click outside it does: the focus is where the click put it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-labels-and-translating\">Labels and translating</h2>\n<p>The texts a screen reader reads are texts like the others, with an id - see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/screen-readers\">Texts</a>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>id</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>grid.label</code></td>\n<td><code>Grid</code> - the name of the grid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.rowSelector</code></td>\n<td><code>row selector</code> - the column header of the row numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.filter</code></td>\n<td><code>Filter {label}</code> - a header filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.groupToggle</code></td>\n<td><code>{name} ({total})</code> - the button of a group row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>grid.removeGroup</code></td>\n<td><code>Remove the grouping on {title}</code> - the x of a group chip</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The cells and the column headers use the column&#39;s label (<code>columns[field].label</code>, else the field&#39;s\nlabel, else the field name).</p>\n<p>Give each grid a name of its own, a page with two grids called &quot;Grid&quot; is hard to find your way in:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { createGrid, type GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    layout: { center: [{ fields: ['name'], width: 150 }] },\n    texts: { 'grid.label': 'Customers' }\n};\nconst gridInterface = createGrid(host, { config, datasource });</code></pre>\n<p>Translated for the whole page, with the other texts:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { setTexts } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nsetTexts({\n    'grid.label': 'Tabell',\n    'grid.rowSelector': 'radnummer',\n    'grid.filter': 'Filtrer {label}',\n    'grid.groupToggle': '{name}, {total} rader',\n    'grid.removeGroup': 'Fjern grupperingen på {title}'\n});</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-known-limits\">Known limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Checked in Chrome only</strong>: its accessibility tree (through the DevTools protocol) has the\nstructure above, after scrolling both ways, sorted, grouped, expanded and collapsed. Firefox,\nSafari and real screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver) have not been tried. The structure depends on\n<code>aria-owns</code>, which Safari supports less well than Chrome and Firefox.</li>\n<li><strong>Not the keyboard of the grid pattern</strong>: there every cell is reached with the arrow keys and only\none cell is a stop of Tab. Here every cell input is a stop of Tab for the browser (the grid moves\nthe focus on Tab itself), and the plain arrow keys move the caret in the input, Alt+arrows move\nbetween the cells, Alt+Shift+arrows select a range of cells. There is no Home / End / Page Up / Page\nDown / Ctrl+Home in the grid, and no key to select a whole row or column (Shift+Space, Ctrl+Space).</li>\n<li><strong>Ranges of cells</strong>: only the cells in a range have <code>aria-selected</code> (<code>true</code>). WAI-ARIA asks for\n<code>true</code> or <code>false</code> on every cell that can be selected, in a grid with <code>aria-multiselectable</code>: the\ngrid keeps <code>aria-multiselectable</code> for the row selection (the selection mode <code>&#39;multiple&#39;</code>), and gives\nthe other cells no <code>false</code>: every cell of the grid would get a &quot;not selected&quot; state, the way the\nrows have one with a selection mode. Not tried with a screen reader yet, see above. A range\nmade with Alt+Shift+arrows is not read out while it grows: the focus stays in the active cell. Only\nthe cells on screen are in the dom, so a screen reader reads only those of a large range.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the rows on screen are in the dom</strong>: a screen reader reading the page (browse mode) reads\nthe rows on screen, not row 400. The counts and the indexes say where they are.</li>\n<li><strong>Group rows by keyboard</strong>: Tab and Alt+arrows skip the group rows, so the expand/collapse button\nis no stop of Tab. A screen reader reaches it (and presses it) in its browse mode, a keyboard alone\ndoes not.</li>\n<li><strong>The menus</strong> open from the keyboard (Shift+F10), but their entries can not be reached with it.\nThe date picker of a date cell or filter is for the mouse too - a date can be typed.</li>\n<li><strong>The footer</strong> (the Clear button, the filter chips) is not on the way of Tab from the cells. A\nscreen reader reaches it.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the first sort column</strong> has <code>aria-sort</code>; the number of a column in a sort on several\ncolumns is shown, but not read out.</li>\n<li><strong>An aggregate of a group row under its name</strong> is covered on screen, a screen reader gets it with\nthe name. Put the aggregated columns after the first column.</li>\n<li><strong>The column numbers</strong> under the filters are hidden: selecting a column is for the mouse, and a\nselected column is not read out.</li>\n<li><strong>The two scrollbars</strong> are elements of their own, Chrome makes them stops of Tab (an element that\nscrolls and has nothing focusable in it); they have no name.</li>\n<li><code>setSelectionMode()</code> shows (<code>aria-multiselectable</code>, <code>aria-selected</code>) on the next render of the rows:\na click or <code>refresh()</code> (a scroll only renders the rows that scroll in).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"export","title":"Export","html":"<p>The grid exports its rows as CSV: <code>getCsv()</code> gives the text, <code>downloadCsv()</code> saves it as a file. By\ndefault it is what the user sees - the rows the filter lets through, in the sort order, the columns\nof the layout in their order, each value as the cell shows it - with a header line of the column\nlabels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-getcsv-and-downloadcsv\"><code>getCsv()</code> and <code>downloadCsv()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// the text, for code of your own: send it to a server, put it on the clipboard ...\nconst text = gridInterface.getCsv(); // 'Name,Born\\r\\nAnn,2001-05-17\\r\\n...'\n\n// a file: the browser downloads export.csv\ndocument.getElementById('export')!.onclick = () =&gt; gridInterface.downloadCsv();\n\n// the selected rows, as selected.csv, for Excel in Norway\ngridInterface.downloadCsv('selected.csv', { rows: 'selected', separator: ';' });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>getCsv(options?)</code> returns the text.</li>\n<li><code>downloadCsv(filename?, options?)</code> saves it: a <code>Blob</code> downloaded by the click of an\n<code>&lt;a download&gt;</code> link, like a link the user clicks. The filename defaults to <code>export.csv</code>; give it\nthe <code>.csv</code> ending yourself, it is not added.</li>\n<li>Both take the same options, see <a href=\"#guide/export/the-options\">the options</a>. The only difference is the\n<a href=\"#guide/export/excel-the-separator-and-the-bom\">BOM</a>: <code>downloadCsv()</code> writes one by default, <code>getCsv()</code> does not.</li>\n<li>Both work without the grid on the page: they read the config and the datasource.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"export\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-from-the-row-menu\">From the row menu</h2>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) has an <strong>Export:</strong> section with <em>CSV, all rows</em> and <em>CSV, selected\nrows</em> (greyed out when no row is selected): <code>downloadCsv()</code> with the default options, as <code>export.csv</code>.\nThe ids are <code>row.export</code>, <code>row.export.csv</code> and <code>row.export.csvSelected</code>. To give them other options -\nthe <code>;</code> separator for Excel in Norway, say - replace their actions with the\n<a href=\"#guide/menus\">menus hook</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridHooks } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst hooks: GridHooks = {\n    menus: {\n        row: (defaults, context) =&gt;\n            defaults.map((entry) =&gt;\n                entry.id === 'row.export.csv'\n                    ? { ...entry, action: () =&gt; context.gridInterface.downloadCsv('export.csv', { separator: ';' }) }\n                    : entry\n            )\n    }\n};\ngridInterface.updateHooks(hooks);</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-rows\">The rows</h2>\n<p><code>rows: &#39;all&#39;</code>, the default, is every row the filter lets through, in the sort order - what\n<code>datasource.getDataRows()</code> gives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filtered and sorted</strong>: the header filters, the filter editor and a filter set from code all\ncount, and so does <code>filterChangedOnly()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Grouped</strong>: every data row, in the grouped order. The rows of a collapsed group are exported too,\nthe group rows are not.</li>\n<li><strong>Marked for deletion</strong>: those rows are still rows of the grid (struck through) until they are\nsaved, they are exported.</li>\n<li><strong>No totals</strong>: the totals row (<code>totalsRow</code>) and the aggregates of the group rows are not exported, a\nspreadsheet sums the rows up itself - and a line of totals would be read back as a row. See\n<a href=\"#guide/aggregates\">Aggregates</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>rows: &#39;selected&#39;</code> is the selected rows, in the same order - what <code>datasource.getSelectedRows()</code>\ngives. That is the selected rows that are displayed: a selected row in a collapsed group, or one the\nfilter leaves out, is not exported, as the row menu&#39;s actions on the selected rows leave it out.\nWith nothing selected the text is the header line only.</p>\n<p><code>rows: &#39;range&#39;</code> is the selected range of cells - the last of <code>gridInterface.getRanges()</code>, the one\nthe user made last, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard\">Selection and clipboard</a>: its data rows top to\nbottom (a group row in it is left out) and, unless <code>fields</code> is given, its fields left to right, with\ntheir labels as the header. With no range the text is empty. Ctrl+C copies the same cells as tab\nseparated text, without the header.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// the cells the user selected, as a file\ngridInterface.downloadCsv('range.csv', { rows: 'range' });</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-columns-and-their-labels\">The columns and their labels</h2>\n<p>By default the columns are the layout&#39;s, in the order the user sees them: pinned left, center,\npinned right, as <code>getLayoutFields()</code> gives them. A column with stacked fields gives a CSV column for\neach of its fields, top to bottom. A hidden column is not in the layout, so it is not exported.</p>\n<p><code>fields</code> picks the fields and their order. Any field of the rows can be given, also one the grid has\nno column for:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const text = gridInterface.getCsv({ fields: ['id', 'name', 'email'] });</code></pre>\n<p>The header line has the labels the grid&#39;s header shows: the column&#39;s <code>label</code>, else the datasource\nfield&#39;s <code>label</code>, else the field name prettified (<code>first_name</code> is <code>First Name</code>). <code>header: false</code>\nleaves the line out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-formatted-or-raw-values\">Formatted or raw values</h2>\n<p>By default (<code>formatted: true</code>) each value is written as the cell shows it, the text of the field&#39;s\nformatter (see <a href=\"#guide/formatting\">Formatting</a>):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>field</th>\n<th>written</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>12.5</code></td>\n<td><code>number</code>, <code>numberComma</code></td>\n<td><code>12,5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <code>Date</code></td>\n<td><code>date</code></td>\n<td><code>2026-11-20</code>, or as its formatter writes it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td><code>boolean</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>null</code></td>\n<td>any</td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A column&#39;s <code>numberOverride</code> and <code>dynamicCellTypeColumn</code> are followed, as in the cells.</p>\n<p><code>formatted: false</code> writes the values as they are stored: for a file a program reads.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>value</th>\n<th>written</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>a number</td>\n<td>with a dot, <code>String(value)</code>: <code>12.5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <code>Date</code></td>\n<td><code>toISOString()</code>: <code>2026-11-20T08:15:00.000Z</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a boolean</td>\n<td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>, an invalid <code>Date</code></td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text</td>\n<td>as it is</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A raw date is UTC: a date at midnight in Norway is the day before at 23:00 (22:00 in summer) in\nthe file. Export formatted values to get the dates as the user sees them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-excel-the-separator-and-the-bom\">Excel: the separator and the BOM</h2>\n<p>Excel opens a <code>.csv</code> file with the list separator of the computer&#39;s locale. In a locale with a\ndecimal comma - Norwegian, German, French ... - that is <code>;</code>, and a file with <code>,</code> opens with every row\nin one column. For those users export with <code>separator: &#39;;&#39;</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.downloadCsv('export.csv', { separator: ';' });</code></pre>\n<p>With <code>;</code> a formatted <code>12,5</code> is written as it is; with <code>,</code> it is put in quotes (<code>&quot;12,5&quot;</code>).\n<code>separator: &#39;\\t&#39;</code> gives tab separated text.</p>\n<p>Excel also needs a UTF-8 byte order mark (BOM, the character U+FEFF) at the start of the file to read\nit as UTF-8: without one <code>æøå</code> show up garbled. <code>downloadCsv()</code> writes one by default, <code>bom: false</code>\nleaves it out. <code>getCsv()</code> does not write one by default, <code>bom: true</code> adds it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-formulas-csv-injection\">Formulas (CSV injection)</h2>\n<p>A spreadsheet takes a cell that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code> or <code>@</code> as a formula. A value a user typed\ninto the grid - <code>=HYPERLINK(&quot;http://evil.example&quot;, &quot;Click me&quot;)</code> - would then run on the computer\nof whoever opens the file. That is &quot;CSV injection&quot;, see\n<a href=\"https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/CSV_Injection\">OWASP</a>.</p>\n<p>So by default (<code>escapeFormulas: true</code>) text that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>@</code>, a tab, CR or LF, or\nwith the full width <code>＝ ＋ － ＠</code>, gets a <code>&#39;</code> in front: <code>&#39;=HYPERLINK(...)</code>, which a spreadsheet shows\nas text.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only text is escaped: a value that is text in the row (or an object), and the labels. A number, a\nboolean, a <code>Date</code> and a blank never are, whatever their formatter writes: <code>-5</code> stays <code>-5</code>.</li>\n<li>The value decides, not the field&#39;s type: a number field holding the text <code>&#39;-5&#39;</code> is written as\n<code>&#39;-5</code>. Keep the values of a number field numbers.</li>\n<li>Text that only looks like a formula gets the <code>&#39;</code> too: a phone number <code>+47 912 34 567</code> is written\n<code>&#39;+47 912 34 567</code>, a note <code>- see above</code> is <code>&#39;- see above</code>.</li>\n<li>The <code>&#39;</code> is part of the text in the file: a program that reads it gets it too. For a file a\nprogram reads, not a spreadsheet, turn it off with <code>escapeFormulas: false</code>.</li>\n<li>OWASP notes the escaping is not reliable in Excel once the file is saved and opened again: treat a\nfile of data users typed with care anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-file-format\">The file format</h2>\n<p>The text follows <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180\">RFC 4180</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a line per row, after the header line; every line ends with CRLF (<code>\\r\\n</code>), the last one too</li>\n<li>a value with the separator, a double quote, CR or LF is put in double quotes, and a double quote\nin it is doubled: <code>say &quot;hi&quot;</code> is written <code>&quot;say &quot;&quot;hi&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></li>\n<li>spaces are part of a value: they are kept, without quotes</li>\n<li>a row of a single empty value is written <code>&quot;&quot;</code>: many programs read an empty line as no value at all</li>\n<li>with no columns the text is empty</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-options\">The options</h2>\n<p><code>CsvOptions</code>, every option optional. It is plain JSON: it can be stored, or come from a menu of your\nown. An option the grid does not know, or a value an option does not take (<code>rows: &#39;some&#39;</code>,\n<code>separator: &#39;|&#39;</code>), throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rows</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;all&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;all&#39;</code>: the rows the filter lets through, sorted. <code>&#39;selected&#39;</code>: the selected rows. <code>&#39;range&#39;</code>: the rows of the selected range of cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fields</code></td>\n<td>the layout (<code>&#39;range&#39;</code>: the range&#39;s)</td>\n<td>the fields, in order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>header</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a first line with the column labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>formatted</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the values as the cells show them; <code>false</code> as they are stored</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>separator</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;,&#39;</code></td>\n<td><code>&#39;;&#39;</code> for Excel in a locale with a decimal comma, <code>&#39;\\t&#39;</code> tab separated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bom</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> for <code>downloadCsv()</code>, <code>false</code> for <code>getCsv()</code></td>\n<td>a UTF-8 BOM first, for Excel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>escapeFormulas</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>a <code>&#39;</code> in front of text that starts with <code>=</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>@</code> ...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { CsvOptions } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst forExcel: CsvOptions = { separator: ';' };\nconst forAProgram: CsvOptions = { formatted: false, escapeFormulas: false };\n\ngridInterface.downloadCsv('for-excel.csv', forExcel);\nconst text = gridInterface.getCsv(forAProgram);</code></pre>\n"},{"id":"live-data","title":"Live data","html":"<p>Rows that change while the grid shows them: prices from a server every second, the rows another user\nsaved. <code>datasource.upsert()</code> merges what the server sends by key, the cells show the new values on\ntheir own, and an edit the user has not saved is never overwritten - when the server changed it too,\nit is a conflict, for the user to resolve.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"live-data\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-values-show-on-their-own\">Values show on their own</h2>\n<p>A value set on a row shows in the grid without <code>gridInterface.refresh()</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>const row = datasource.getDataRows()[0];\nrow.price = 10; // shows in the next animation frame</code></pre>\n<p>The datasource sends <code>rows-changed</code> once the code that changed the rows is done (in a microtask):\none event, however many rows and fields it set, with the rows and the fields of each. The grid\nrenders the rows of it that are on screen in the next animation frame - once per frame, also for a\nloop over 100 000 rows. A row off screen is rendered when it scrolls in.</p>\n<p>A row is in <code>rows-changed</code> when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a field was set on it: from code, by the user, by <code>undo()</code> / <code>redo()</code>, <code>resetField()</code>,\n<code>resetRows()</code>, <code>resetData()</code>, <code>resolveConflict()</code>, or by <code>upsert()</code>. The validation of the edit is\ndone by then: its errors show with it</li>\n<li>its deletion mark changed, or its server error (<code>getDataContainer().setErrors()</code>)</li>\n<li>one of its conflicts came or went</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Still call <code>refresh()</code> when what the cells show changed without a row changing: what the\n<code>isReadonly</code> or <code>cellClass</code> hooks read, a value written to your own object instead of the row the\ndatasource hands out (that is no edit either, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/edits-from-code\">Edits from code</a>).\nA formatter that shows values differently needs <code>rebuild()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>The cell being typed in is left as it is.</strong> Rendering it would put the formatted value into what\nthe user is typing - <code>12,</code> would become <code>12</code> - so no render touches the input the user typed in\nsince it got the focus, not even <code>refresh()</code>. Once the focus leaves it, the grid renders the rows on\nscreen again. A cell with the focus the user has not typed in yet is rendered: its text is its value,\nnothing is lost.</p>\n<p><strong>Scrolling renders only the rows that scroll in.</strong> A row that stays on screen is up to date already:\nits changes render themselves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-merging-rows-by-key-upsert\">Merging rows by key: <code>upsert()</code></h2>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { Datasource } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ninterface Stock {\n    id: number;\n    name: string;\n    price: number;\n}\ndeclare function fetchStocks(): Promise&lt;Stock[]&gt;;\n\nconst stocks = new Datasource&lt;Stock&gt;({ key: 'id', fields: { price: 'number' } });\nstocks.setData(await fetchStocks());\n\n// a full poll: every row the server has. A row it no longer has goes, unless it has changes here\nsetInterval(async () =&gt; {\n    stocks.upsert(await fetchStocks(), { removeMissing: true });\n}, 1000);\n\n// a push of what changed: only the fields sent change\nstocks.upsert([{ id: 7, price: 12.5 }]);</code></pre>\n<p><code>upsert()</code> needs a key (<code>new Datasource({ key: &#39;id&#39; })</code>), it throws a <code>TypeError</code> without one, and\nfor a row without its key. For each row it gets:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>the row</th>\n<th>what happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>its key is in the data</td>\n<td>the fields it has get its values, as the server&#39;s: the value and its original, so the row is not edited by them. The fields it does not have stay as they are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a field the user has edited</td>\n<td>the user&#39;s value stays, the server&#39;s becomes its original - see <a href=\"#guide/live-data/conflicts\">Conflicts</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a row marked for deletion</td>\n<td>its fields get the values, it stays marked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>its key is not in the data</td>\n<td>added at the end: a loaded row, not new, not edited - the object itself, as <code>setData()</code> adds it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The keys are compared as text</strong>: <code>7</code> and <code>&#39;7&#39;</code> are the same key. The key field is never written.</li>\n<li><strong>A new row</strong> (<code>addNewEmpty()</code>) is not on the server yet: its key is a generated one, it never\nmatches a row of the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Not in the undo history</strong>: the server&#39;s values are no edits. The fields that change are\nvalidated, as for an edit. Undoing the user&#39;s own edits of a field the server changed goes back to\nthe server&#39;s value, not to the value the field had before - that would be an edit of the server&#39;s\nvalue, and a save would send the old value back.</li>\n<li><strong><code>removeMissing: true</code></strong> (default <code>false</code>): the rows are every row there is, and a row whose key\nis not among them is removed for good. Not a new row, and not a row with changes of the user&#39;s - an\nedit, a deletion mark, a cell being typed in: that one stays, in conflict. Use it for a full poll,\nleave it out for a push of changes.</li>\n<li><strong><code>refilter: false</code></strong> (default <code>true</code>): the filter does not run, so a row whose new values the\nfilter leaves out stays shown until it runs again - as for <code>setData(rows, { refilter: false })</code>.\nThe sorting and grouping still run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Then the displayed rows are rebuilt once - filtered (<code>filterChangedOnly()</code> too), sorted, grouped. The\nselection (it follows the key), the expanded groups and the scroll position stay. A row removed\nleaves the selection, and <code>currentEntity</code> is set to <code>null</code> when it was the current row. The grid keeps\nits row elements: the input the user types in keeps its focus and its text.</p>\n<p>It returns what it did: the rows <code>added</code>, <code>updated</code> and <code>removed</code>, and the <code>conflicts</code> it made.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>declare const rowsFromServer: object[];\n\nconst { added, updated, removed, conflicts } = datasource.upsert(rowsFromServer, { removeMissing: true });\nconsole.log(`${added.length} new, ${updated.length} changed, ${removed.length} removed, ${conflicts.length} conflicts`);</code></pre>\n<p>It is cheap on many rows: one look up by key per row. 100 000 rows of which 1% changed take about 55\nms in node, 1000 rows into 100 000 about 16 ms - plus the sorting and grouping, which run as for\n<code>filter()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-conflicts\">Conflicts</h2>\n<p>A conflict is a change of the server&#39;s that <code>upsert()</code> did not write because the user changed the\nsame thing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A field</strong>: the server changed a field the user has edited and not saved (or is typing in). The\nuser&#39;s value stays; the server&#39;s value becomes the field&#39;s original, so <code>getChanges()</code> sends the\nuser&#39;s value as an edit of what the server has now, and reverting the field goes to the server&#39;s\nvalue. When the server has the user&#39;s value, the edit is done (not edited any more), and when it\nis back at the value the edit started from, nothing has to be chosen: neither is a conflict.</li>\n<li><strong>A row</strong>: <code>removeMissing</code> did not get a row that has changes here. It stays.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>datasource.on('conflicts-changed', () =&gt; {\n    for (const conflict of datasource.getConflicts()) {\n        if (conflict.field === null) {\n            console.log(`row ${conflict.row.__KEY} was removed on the server, it has changes here`);\n        } else {\n            console.log(`${conflict.field}: yours ${conflict.local}, the server's ${conflict.remote}, it was ${conflict.original}`);\n        }\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>method, event</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>getConflicts(rows?)</code></td>\n<td>the conflicts, every row&#39;s or the rows given: <code>{ row, field, local, remote, original }</code> for a field, <code>{ row, field: null, remote: &#39;removed&#39; }</code> for a row. <code>local</code> is the value the row has when it is called</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>hasConflicts()</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code> when there is one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>resolveConflict(row, field, &#39;local&#39;)</code></td>\n<td>the user&#39;s value stays, still edited: a save sends it. A row the server removed stays, and is not in conflict again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>resolveConflict(row, field, &#39;remote&#39;)</code></td>\n<td>the server&#39;s value, as <code>resetField()</code> sets it: an edit, a step of the undo history. A row the server removed is removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conflicts-changed</code></td>\n<td>sent when they changed, once per run of code</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A conflict also goes when what it is about goes: the field is not edited any more (reverted, undone,\ntyped back to the server&#39;s value, saved with <code>acceptChanges()</code>), the row has no changes any more, or\nit leaves the data (<code>removeData()</code>, <code>setData()</code> without <code>add</code> ...). <code>resetData()</code> clears every one.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// the server wins every conflict\nfor (const conflict of datasource.getConflicts()) {\n    datasource.resolveConflict(conflict.row, conflict.field, 'remote');\n}</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-showing-what-a-merge-changed\">Showing what a merge changed</h2>\n<p><code>datasource.getMergedAt(row, field)</code> is the time (<code>Date.now()</code>) the last <code>upsert()</code> changed the value\nof a field - <code>undefined</code> when no upsert changed it. A field the user has edited keeps the user&#39;s value,\nan upsert does not change it (a conflict is no change of the value). The grid renders the rows an\nupsert changed, so a <code>cellClass</code> hook can give those cells a class for a short highlight:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>gridInterface.updateHooks({\n    cellClass: (row, field) =&gt; {\n        const at = datasource.getMergedAt(row, field);\n        return at !== undefined &amp;&amp; Date.now() - at &lt; 1000 ? { input: 'just-changed' } : null;\n    }\n});</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"css\"><code>@keyframes just-changed {\n    from { background: #ffe58a; }\n    to { background: transparent; }\n}\n.just-changed {\n    animation: just-changed 1s ease-out;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The animation runs when the class is added, once: a cell rendered again keeps it until it is\nrendered after the second is over.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-events\">Events</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>event</th>\n<th>sent when</th>\n<th>payload</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>rows-changed</code></td>\n<td>values of rows changed (see above), once per run of code</td>\n<td><code>{ rows, fields }</code>: the rows, and <code>fields.get(row)</code> the fields set on each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>conflicts-changed</code></td>\n<td>conflicts came or went, once per run of code, after <code>rows-changed</code></td>\n<td><code>{}</code>, read <code>getConflicts()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collection-changed</code></td>\n<td><code>upsert()</code> added or removed rows, or the displayed rows changed (filter, sort order, groups)</td>\n<td><code>{ info: &#39;upsert&#39;, added, removed? }</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>An upsert that only changed values sends no <code>collection-changed</code>: the grid renders the cells from\n<code>rows-changed</code>. Both live data events are the data container&#39;s: every datasource on the same\n<code>DataContainer</code> sends them. See <a href=\"#guide/events/datasource-events\">Events</a>.</p>\n"},{"id":"selection-and-clipboard","title":"Selection and clipboard","html":"<p>The grid has two kinds of selection. <strong>Rows</strong> are selected in the datasource: a click on the row\nnumber, the rows a delete or an export works on. <strong>Ranges of cells</strong> are selected in the grid, as in\na spreadsheet: drag over the cells, then copy them (they paste into Excel or Google Sheets), paste\ncells copied there into the grid, or empty them with Delete.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"ranges\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-selecting-rows\">Selecting rows</h2>\n<p>A click on the row number selects the row, Ctrl+click adds one, Shift+click selects the rows in\nbetween - the datasource&#39;s selection, <code>datasource.getSelectedRows()</code>. See\n<a href=\"#guide/datasource/selection\">Selection</a>. The row menu&#39;s Copy, Paste cell, Clear cell, Delete and the\nexport of the selected rows work on these rows.</p>\n<p>A range of cells selects no rows, and selecting rows changes no range: they are two things.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</h2>\n<p>A range is the rectangle between two cells, both included:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>its rows</strong> are the displayed rows between them (<code>datasource.getRows()</code>, 0 based - the <code>row</code> of\nthe grid events). A group row in between is part of the rectangle, but has no cells: it is left out\nof a copy, a paste and a clear</li>\n<li><strong>its fields</strong> are the fields between them in the layout: pinned left, center, pinned right, in the\norder of <code>gridInterface.getLayoutFields()</code>. A column of two stacked fields is two columns here, the\ntop one first - as in the CSV export and for a screen reader</li>\n<li><strong>it starts</strong> at the cell the mouse was pressed in, or the cell with the focus - and ends at the\nopposite corner, which can be above it or left of it</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There can be several ranges: Ctrl+click adds one. The last one is the one made last; where it starts\nis the <strong>active cell</strong>, the cell with the focus.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-mouse\">The mouse</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>press in a cell and drag</td>\n<td>a range from that cell to the cell under the pointer, across the pinned sections. Inside the cell pressed it is the input&#39;s own text selection; once the pointer is on another cell it is a range, and the text the press selected is not selected any more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shift+click, Shift+drag</td>\n<td>the last range from where it starts to the cell clicked - with no range, from the cell with the focus. The focus stays in the active cell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+click, Cmd+click</td>\n<td>another range, of the cell clicked; Ctrl+drag makes it larger. With no range yet the cell with the focus stays selected, as a range of its own (as in Excel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a plain click</td>\n<td>clears the ranges, and does what it did before: the cell gets the focus, its row is the current row, a date cell opens its picker</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Dragged past the top or the bottom of the rows the grid scrolls, a row at a time while the pointer\nstays out there; past the left or right edge of the grid the middle columns scroll.</p>\n<p>A click on a cell does not select its row - it never did: the row number does that. Shift+click does\nnot make the row clicked the current row (the active cell stays where it was), Ctrl+click and\nShift+click do not open the date picker of a date cell and do not check a checkbox.</p>\n<p><strong>A pen</strong> works as the mouse, where the browser does not take its drag for scrolling. <strong>A finger</strong>\nmakes no range: a swipe scrolls the grid, and a long press is the browser&#39;s own (the menu of the row,\nor text selection). Use <code>setRanges()</code> from a button of your own, or a keyboard.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-keyboard\">The keyboard</h3>\n<p>In a cell (see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/the-keyboard\">Keyboard and accessibility</a> for every key):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>key</th>\n<th>does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Alt+Shift+arrows</td>\n<td>starts a range at the cell, or moves the end of the last range one cell: a row up or down (group rows are skipped), a field left or right in the order of the layout - in a column of two stacked fields, right of the top field is the field under it. The focus stays in the active cell, the grid scrolls to the end</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Esc</td>\n<td>clears the ranges. With a menu open, Esc closes the menu first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V, Delete</td>\n<td>see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/copy\">Copy</a>, <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/paste\">Paste</a>, <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/delete\">Delete</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The arrows move the caret in the cell&#39;s input and Shift+arrows select text in it, as always; Alt+arrows\nmove to the next cell. On a Mac, Alt is the Option key: Option+Shift+arrows select a range, not words.</p>\n<p>Moving to another cell - Tab, Alt+arrows, Enter, a click - clears the ranges, and so does typing in a\ncell: as moving the active cell does in a spreadsheet. A right click does not, the row menu works on\nthem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-how-a-range-looks\">How a range looks</h3>\n<p>The cells of a range get a tint, and the range a line around it; the empty cells under a column with\nfewer stacked fields than the others go with the field above them, so a range is a rectangle on screen\ntoo. The colours are <code>--simple-html-grid-range-bg-color</code> and <code>--simple-html-grid-range-border-color</code>,\nlight and dark, see <a href=\"#guide/theming/ranges-of-cells\">Ranges of cells</a>. The cells have the class\n<code>simple-html-cell-in-range</code>, the cells at its edges <code>simple-html-cell-range-top</code>, <code>-bottom</code>, <code>-left</code>\nand <code>-right</code>.</p>\n<p>The cells are reused while the grid scrolls: the classes follow the rows and fields they show.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-when-the-ranges-are-cleared\">When the ranges are cleared</h3>\n<p>The rows of a range are indexes of the displayed rows, and its fields are good for the layout it was\nmade in. So the grid clears the ranges, and sends <code>range-change</code>, when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>the displayed rows change</strong>: a filter, a sort, grouping, a group expanded or collapsed, rows set,\nadded or removed (<code>setData()</code>, <code>addNewEmpty()</code>, <code>removeData()</code>, an <code>upsert()</code> that adds, removes or\nmoves rows), rows marked for deletion, <code>setState()</code> / <code>resetState()</code> with any of these</li>\n<li><strong>the layout changes</strong>: a column moved, pinned, unpinned, hidden, added, its fields stacked another\nway, <code>setState({ layout })</code>, <code>updateConfig({ layout })</code></li>\n<li><strong>the grid is put back on the page</strong>: its rows may have changed while it was away</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are kept when a value changes (an edit, a paste, <code>upsert()</code> of values only), when rows are\nselected or the current row changes, when a column is resized, and on <code>refresh()</code> and <code>rebuild()</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"section-the-api\">The API</h3>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { CellRange } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\n// rows 0 to 4, every field from name to age in the layout\nconst range: CellRange = { fromRow: 0, fromField: 'name', toRow: 4, toField: 'age' };\ngridInterface.setRanges([range]);\n\ngridInterface.getRanges(); // [{ fromRow: 0, fromField: 'name', toRow: 4, toField: 'age' }]\ngridInterface.clearRanges();\n\ngridInterface.on('range-change', ({ ranges }) =&gt; {\n    console.log(ranges.length ? `${ranges.length} ranges` : 'no cells selected');\n});\n\n// the cells of the last range as CSV, with their labels\nconst csv = gridInterface.getCsv({ rows: 'range' });</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>getRanges()</code></strong> gives copies: changing them changes nothing. <code>[]</code> when no cells are selected.</li>\n<li><strong><code>setRanges(ranges)</code></strong> replaces the ranges, as if the user had selected them. It does not move the\nfocus or scroll. A row that is not displayed (0 to <code>datasource.length() - 1</code>) or a field that is\nnot in the layout throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>range-change</code></strong> is sent each time they change, with what <code>getRanges()</code> gives - while the mouse\ndrags, each time the range reaches another cell. Not when nothing changed.</li>\n<li>A range is plain JSON. Its row indexes are only good for the rows as they are displayed now, so a\nrange is not part of <code>getState()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The export</strong>: <code>getCsv({ rows: &#39;range&#39; })</code> and <code>downloadCsv(filename, { rows: &#39;range&#39; })</code> export\nthe last range, see <a href=\"#guide/export/the-rows\">Export</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-copy\">Copy</h2>\n<p>Ctrl+C (Cmd+C) in a cell, with no text selected in it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>with a range of more than one cell, or several ranges</strong>: the last range, as tab separated text -\na line per data row ended with CRLF, a tab between the fields, as Excel puts cells on the clipboard.\nA value with a tab, a line break or a double quote is put in double quotes, a quote in it doubled.\nEvery value is the text the cell shows, through the field&#39;s formatter: <code>12,5</code> with a decimal comma,\na date as its formatter writes it.</li>\n<li><strong>else</strong>: the value of the cell with the focus, as the cell shows it, without a line end - as the\nrow menu&#39;s <em>Copy: Cell Value</em>. Before v6 this copied nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An html table of the same cells goes on the clipboard too, for the programs that paste html rather\nthan plain text. A line break in a cell is a <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> with the style Excel reads as a line break in the\ncell (<code>mso-data-placement:same-cell</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Text selected in the cell&#39;s input</strong> is copied as before: the input&#39;s own copy. Making a range ends\nthe text selection of the active cell, so after a drag or Alt+Shift+arrows Ctrl+C copies the range.</p>\n<p><strong>Several ranges</strong>: the last one is copied. Excel refuses to copy ranges that do not make a rectangle\ntogether; the grid copies the one made last, so Ctrl+C always copies something you can see.</p>\n<p>Text that looks like a formula (<code>=SUM(A1:A2)</code>) is copied as it is: what is copied is what is in the\ncells, and a paste back into the grid gives the same values. A spreadsheet may take it as a formula\nwhen it is pasted there - the CSV export escapes it (<code>escapeFormulas</code>), the clipboard does not.</p>\n<p>The row menu (right click a cell) has <em>Copy: Range</em> while there are ranges: the last range, as Ctrl+C.\nThat also works when the focus is not in a cell, after the wheel scrolled the grid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-paste\">Paste</h2>\n<p>Ctrl+V (Cmd+V) in a cell of an editable grid reads the plain text of the clipboard as tab separated\ncells: what Excel and Google Sheets copy, and what the grid copies. A line per row (CRLF, LF or CR);\na value in double quotes can hold tabs and line breaks, <code>&quot;&quot;</code> is a quote; the line end Excel puts after\nthe last row is not a row.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One value, ranges of more than one cell</strong>: every cell of every range gets it - copy one cell,\nselect a range, paste.</li>\n<li><strong>A block of cells</strong>: from the top left cell of the last range - or the cell with the focus when\nthere is no range - down and right, a row of the block per data row. With no range the cells pasted\ninto become a range, so you see where they went. A block smaller than the range is pasted once, it\nis not repeated to fill the range; a larger one is pasted whole.</li>\n<li><strong>One value, no range</strong>: the input&#39;s own paste, at the caret. Unless the input can not take it: a\nvalue with a line break (an input has none, the browser would make it a space), a checkbox, a\nmultiline cell (its input is readonly, see <a href=\"#guide/editing/multiline-text\">Multiline text</a>) or a\ncolumn with <code>allowPasteClearOnly</code> - the grid writes the value into the cell then.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every cell is written as typing it does: the text through the field&#39;s formatter (<code>parse</code>) - <code>12,5</code>\nis 12.5 in a number field, <code>2026-11-20</code> a <code>Date</code> in a date field, <code>true</code> in a boolean field. An\n<strong>empty text empties the cell</strong> (<code>null</code>), as Delete does: a blank cell copied in Excel is blank in the\ngrid, also in a number field.</p>\n<p>What is skipped:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>readonly cells</strong>: the grid&#39;s <code>readonly</code>, the column&#39;s <code>readonly</code>, the <code>isReadonly</code> hook. A\nreadonly grid pastes nothing</li>\n<li><strong>group rows</strong>: the next row of the block goes to the next data row</li>\n<li><strong>rows past the last displayed row</strong>: a paste adds no rows. Paste fewer, or add rows first\n(<code>addNewEmpty()</code>)</li>\n<li><strong>fields past the last field of the layout</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The validation runs on every cell written, as for an edit: the errors show on the cells and the row\nnumbers. The edited markers and the row states follow too. A paste sends no <code>paste</code> event (that is the\nrow menu&#39;s <em>Paste cell</em>): the rows changed are in <code>rows-changed</code>.</p>\n<p>The row menu has <em>Paste: Paste range</em> while there are ranges: it pastes as Ctrl+V does with ranges.\nIt reads the clipboard with <code>navigator.clipboard.readText()</code>, which the browser may ask the user to\nallow first (Ctrl+V needs no permission).</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-delete\">Delete</h2>\n<p>Delete or Backspace in a cell, with a range of more than one cell (or several ranges) and no text\nselected in the input: every cell of every range is emptied (<code>null</code>), the readonly cells skipped, in\nan editable grid. In one cell they are the input&#39;s own keys, as before. The row menu&#39;s <em>Clear: Clear\nrange</em> does the same.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-undo\">Undo</h2>\n<p>A paste, a Delete and a clear of ranges are written in one go: <strong>one Ctrl+Z</strong> takes all of it back,\nsee <a href=\"#guide/editing/undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</a>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>// what a paste or a Delete did, from code: one step\ndatasource.undo();</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"section-what-a-screen-reader-gets\">What a screen reader gets</h2>\n<p>The cells of a range have <code>aria-selected=&quot;true&quot;</code>; the other cells have none. The keys are in the\ntable of <a href=\"#guide/accessibility/a-cell\">Keyboard and accessibility</a>, the limits in its\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility/known-limits\">Known limits</a>: a range made with Alt+Shift+arrows is not read out\nwhile it grows, the focus stays in the active cell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-known-limits\">Known limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No ranges by touch</strong>, see <a href=\"#guide/selection-and-clipboard/the-mouse\">The mouse</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Copy and paste listen while the focus is in a cell of the grid.</strong> The wheel takes the focus from\nthe cell (its row element is reused for other rows), then Ctrl+C goes to the page: use the row\nmenu&#39;s <em>Copy: Range</em>, or Shift+click the cell at the end of the range again - the range stays as it\nis and the cell gets the focus.</li>\n<li><strong>Cut</strong> (Ctrl+X) is the input&#39;s own: it cuts the text selected in the cell, not a range. Copy, then\nDelete.</li>\n<li><strong>Several ranges</strong> copy the last one; a block is pasted into the last one.</li>\n<li><strong>No fill</strong>: a block is not repeated to fill a larger range, only one value fills it.</li>\n<li><strong>Checked in Chrome</strong>: the drag over the cells, the text selection it ends, the clipboard. Firefox\nand Safari have not been tried.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"id":"aggregates","title":"Aggregates","html":"<p>A column can be summed up: its sum, average, smallest or largest number, or the number of values it\nhas. The grid shows it in the column&#39;s place in every group row - over the rows of the group - and in\na totals row under the rows, over every row the filter lets through. The datasource computes the\nvalues, so they are there without a grid too: <code>datasource.aggregate()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"doc-sample\" data-sample=\"aggregates\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-the-config\">In the config</h2>\n<p><code>aggregate</code> is an option of a column, plain JSON like the rest of the config:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import type { GridConfig } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\nconst config: GridConfig = {\n    columns: {\n        balance: { aggregate: 'sum' },\n        age: { aggregate: 'avg' },\n        email: { aggregate: 'count' }\n    },\n    layout: {\n        center: [\n            { fields: ['company'], width: 160 },\n            { fields: ['email'], width: 200 },\n            { fields: ['age'], width: 80 },\n            { fields: ['balance'], width: 120 }\n        ]\n    },\n    totalsRow: true, // off by default\n    initialState: { grouping: [{ attribute: 'country', title: 'Country' }] }\n};</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>function</th>\n<th>the value</th>\n<th>of no values</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>&#39;sum&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the sum of the numbers</td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;avg&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the average of the numbers</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;min&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the smallest number</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;max&#39;</code></td>\n<td>the largest number</td>\n<td>blank</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&#39;count&#39;</code></td>\n<td>how many values are not blank, of any type</td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> are for <code>number</code> fields</strong>, the type the datasource&#39;s <code>fields</code> give\nthem. On another field the grid throws a <code>TypeError</code> when it is made, or when <code>updateConfig()</code> is\ngiven the column - a config from JSON could otherwise show nothing without a word. They take the\nnumbers: a blank (<code>null</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>&#39;&#39;</code>) and what is no number (text, even <code>&#39;12&#39;</code>, <code>NaN</code>) are\nleft out, of the average too. <code>0</code> is a number.</li>\n<li><strong><code>count</code></strong> counts the values that are not blank, in any field: <code>0</code> and <code>false</code> are values.</li>\n<li><strong>Rows marked for deletion count</strong> until they are saved (<code>clearMarkedForDeletion()</code>, or\n<code>acceptChanges()</code> after the server took them): they are rows of the grid until then, as in the\nfooter&#39;s count and the export.</li>\n<li><strong>One function per column.</strong> A field shown in two columns of the layout has the same function in both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two options of the grid:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>option</th>\n<th>default</th>\n<th>what it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>groupAggregates</code></td>\n<td><code>true</code></td>\n<td>the aggregates in the group rows. <code>false</code> shows the name of the group only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>totalsRow</code></td>\n<td><code>false</code></td>\n<td>the totals row under the rows</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"section-how-they-are-shown\">How they are shown</h2>\n<p>Each value is shown with a short label in front, <code>Sum 1234.5</code>: the texts <code>aggregate.sum</code>,\n<code>aggregate.avg</code>, <code>aggregate.min</code>, <code>aggregate.max</code> and <code>aggregate.count</code>, see\n<a href=\"#guide/texts/aggregates\">Texts</a>. Translate them like the other texts, or make them shorter:\n<code>setTexts({ &#39;aggregate.sum&#39;: &#39;Σ&#39; })</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The field&#39;s formatter formats the value</strong>: a <code>balance</code> field with a formatter of two decimals\nshows its sum with two decimals. Nothing is rounded before that - an average of whole numbers is\n<code>34.666666666666664</code> until the formatter makes it <code>34.7</code>. A formatter that shows whole numbers as\nthey are and the rest with one decimal suits a field of whole numbers, see the sample. <code>count</code> is\nshown as a whole number.</li>\n<li><strong>Right aligned in the column</strong>, like the numbers in the cells. A value too long for its column is\ncut off with <code>...</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>The sum does not drift</strong> over many rows: it is added up with a compensated sum, 100 000 times\n<code>0.1</code> is <code>10000</code>, not <code>10000.000000018848</code>. What is left is what a number in javascript is:\n<code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is <code>0.30000000000000004</code> - the formatter shows it.</li>\n<li><strong>A column of stacked fields</strong> (<code>fields: [&#39;debit&#39;, &#39;credit&#39;]</code>): each field&#39;s value is on the line of\nits field. When an aggregated field is not the first of its column, the group rows get the lines\nthey need to show it, and so does the totals row.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-in-the-group-rows\">In the group rows</h2>\n<p>A group row shows the group&#39;s name at the left, and each aggregate in its column, over every row of\nthe group: the rows of its sub groups, and of collapsed groups too. A collapsed group shows the same\nvalues as an expanded one.</p>\n<p>The name is over the first columns: an aggregate in a column under the name is covered by it. Put the\naggregated columns after the first column, or make the first column wide enough for the name. A\nscreen reader gets the covered value with the name, see <a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">Accessibility</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-the-totals-row\">The totals row</h2>\n<p><code>totalsRow: true</code> shows a row under the rows: &quot;Total&quot; under the row numbers (the text\n<code>aggregate.total</code>) and each aggregate in its column, over every row the filter lets through - when\ngrouped, the rows of collapsed groups too. It follows the columns when the middle scrolls sideways,\nand the pinned columns stay where they are, as in the rows.</p>\n<p>The horizontal scrollbar is between the rows and the totals row. The totals row takes its own room:\nthe rows get less, the footer stays as it is.</p>\n<p>The totals row is not part of an export: <code>getCsv()</code> and <code>downloadCsv()</code> give the rows, a spreadsheet\nsums them up itself, and a line of totals would be read back as a row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-when-they-change\">When they change</h2>\n<p>The values follow the rows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An edit</strong>, in the grid or from code (<code>row.balance = 10</code>), <code>undo()</code>, <code>redo()</code>, <code>upsert()</code>,\n<code>resetData()</code> ...: the group rows on screen and the totals row show the new values in the next\nanimation frame, with the cells - the datasource&#39;s <code>rows-changed</code> event.</li>\n<li><strong>The filter</strong>: the totals and the groups are over the rows it lets through.</li>\n<li><strong>Sorting</strong> changes no value, <strong>grouping</strong> makes new group rows.</li>\n<li>A value set on your own object, not on the row the datasource hands out (<code>getDataRows()</code> ...), is\nnot seen: neither the grid nor the datasource knows it changed. Set it on the row.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"section-from-code\">From code</h2>\n<p><code>datasource.aggregate(field, fn, rows?)</code> gives the value the grid shows, without a grid too:</p>\n<pre class=\"code-block\" data-lang=\"ts\"><code>import { isGroupRow } from '@simple-html/grid';\n\ndatasource.aggregate('balance', 'sum'); // the rows the filter lets through\ndatasource.aggregate('age', 'avg'); // null when no row has an age\n\n// a group's rows: the rows of its sub groups and of collapsed groups included\nfor (const row of datasource.getRows()) {\n    if (isGroupRow(row)) {\n        console.log(row.__groupName, datasource.aggregate('balance', 'sum', row));\n    }\n}\n\n// rows of your own, the selected ones say - formatted as the field shows its values\nconst sum = datasource.aggregate('balance', 'sum', datasource.getSelectedRows());\ndatasource.getFormatter('balance').format(sum);</code></pre>\n<p>The value of the filtered rows and of a group row is kept until a value of its field changes or\nother rows are filtered, so the grid can ask on every rebuild and every render of a group row: an\nedit of another field changes nothing. Computing it again reads the field of every row: measured in\nChrome, 4ms for 100 000 rows, about 18ms once they are grouped. A list of rows you give is computed\non each call.</p>\n<h2 id=\"section-theming-and-screen-readers\">Theming and screen readers</h2>\n<p>The totals row has colours of its own, <code>--simple-html-grid-totals-bg-color</code> and\n<code>--simple-html-grid-totals-font-color</code>; the label in front of a value is\n<code>--simple-html-grid-aggregate-label-color</code>. The group rows keep their colours. See\n<a href=\"#guide/theming/the-variables-of-the-grid\">Theming</a>.</p>\n<p>For a screen reader each aggregate is a cell of its column, read with the column&#39;s header, and the\ntotals row is the last row of the grid with &quot;Total&quot; as its row header. See\n<a href=\"#guide/accessibility\">Accessibility</a>.</p>\n"}]